(function(m,c){var L=window.ACADEMY.lessons;for(var id in c){Object.assign(L[id],c[id]);}(window.ACADEMY_LOADED=window.ACADEMY_LOADED||{})[m]=1;})("kubernetes",{"kubernetes/architecture":{"html":"\u003cp>You run one container on one server and it works. Now you need ten copies across five servers, automatic replacement when one dies, and updates without downtime. Doing that by hand does not scale. Kubernetes is the system that does it for you: you describe what you want, and it keeps working until reality matches.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>The components of the control plane and of a worker node\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>The declarative model and the reconcile loop\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>The core objects you will meet in every cluster\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>The first kubectl commands to run on an unfamiliar cluster\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"the-components\">The components\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A cluster is a set of machines called \u003cstrong>nodes\u003c/strong>. The \u003cstrong>control plane\u003c/strong> makes decisions; \u003cstrong>worker nodes\u003c/strong> run your containers.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"table-wrap\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\n\u003cth>Component\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Where\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Job\u003c/th>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>kube-apiserver\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Control plane\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>The front door. Every client and component talks to it. It validates and stores requests\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>etcd\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Control plane\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>The database holding all cluster state. Back it up\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>kube-scheduler\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Control plane\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Chooses a node for each new Pod\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>kube-controller-manager\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Control plane\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Runs controllers that drive actual state toward desired state\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>kubelet\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Every node\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Starts and monitors the containers assigned to its node\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>kube-proxy\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Every node\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Maintains network rules so Services reach Pods\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Container runtime\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Every node\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Actually runs containers (containerd or CRI-O)\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"declarative-and-reconciling\">Declarative and reconciling\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>You do not tell Kubernetes \u003cem>how\u003c/em> to do something. You submit a \u003cstrong>desired state\u003c/strong>, for example &quot;run 3 replicas of this image&quot;, and controllers repeatedly compare it with the \u003cstrong>actual state\u003c/strong> and act on the difference. If a node dies and a replica disappears, the controller notices 2 is not 3 and creates a replacement. This loop is why Kubernetes heals itself, and it is the most important idea in this module.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"kubectl create deployment web --image=nginx:1.27 --replicas=3\nkubectl get pods -l app=web\nkubectl delete pod web-7c5ddbdf54-4xk2p\nkubectl get pods -l app=web\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl create deployment web --image=nginx:1.27 --replicas=3\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">deployment.apps/web created\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl get pods -l app=web\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">NAME                   READY   STATUS    RESTARTS   AGE\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">web-7c5ddbdf54-4xk2p   1/1     Running   0          20s\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">web-7c5ddbdf54-9tq8m   1/1     Running   0          20s\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">web-7c5ddbdf54-zl6vh   1/1     Running   0          20s\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl delete pod web-7c5ddbdf54-4xk2p\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">pod &quot;web-7c5ddbdf54-4xk2p&quot; deleted\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl get pods -l app=web\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>After the delete, run the last command again and you will see three Pods once more, one of them brand new. You asked for 3, so Kubernetes made it so.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"core-objects\">Core objects\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"table-wrap\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\n\u003cth>Object\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Purpose\u003c/th>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003cstrong>Pod\u003c/strong>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>One or more containers sharing a network and storage. The smallest unit you deploy\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003cstrong>Deployment\u003c/strong>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Manages identical Pods, rolling updates and rollbacks\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003cstrong>Service\u003c/strong>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>A stable name and IP in front of a changing set of Pods\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003cstrong>ConfigMap / Secret\u003c/strong>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Configuration and sensitive data, kept out of images\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003cstrong>Namespace\u003c/strong>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>A named partition of the cluster for teams or environments\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003cstrong>Node\u003c/strong>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>A machine in the cluster\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Objects are written as YAML with four top-level parts: \u003ccode>apiVersion\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>kind\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>metadata\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>spec\u003c/code>. You will write many in the coming lessons.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"first-commands-on-any-cluster\">First commands on any cluster\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"kubectl config current-context\nkubectl get nodes -o wide\nkubectl get pods -A\nkubectl describe node worker-2 | tail -15\nkubectl api-resources | head\nkubectl explain pod.spec.containers\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl config current-context\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">prod-eu-west\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl get nodes -o wide\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl get pods -A\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl describe node worker-2 | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">tail\u003c/span> -15\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl api-resources | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">head\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl explain pod.spec.containers\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Always check \u003ccode>current-context\u003c/code> first. Running a command against the wrong cluster is a classic and expensive mistake. \u003ccode>kubectl explain\u003c/code> documents any field from inside the terminal, which is also what you will rely on in an exam.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-kubectl-is-pointed-at-the-wrong-cluster\">Incident: kubectl is pointed at the wrong cluster\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>You run \u003ccode>kubectl delete deployment web\u003c/code> to clean up a test, and an alert fires: the production website is down.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cp>Your shell was still using the production context from earlier. Confirm and prevent:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"kubectl config get-contexts\nkubectl config use-context dev-sandbox\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl config get-contexts\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">CURRENT   NAME          CLUSTER       NAMESPACE\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">*         prod-eu-west  prod-eu-west  shop\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">          dev-sandbox   dev-sandbox   default\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl config use-context dev-sandbox\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Restore service first by re-applying the Deployment manifest from your records (\u003ccode>kubectl apply -f web.yaml\u003c/code> on the production context), then fix the habit: show the context in your shell prompt, use a separate kubeconfig file per environment, and add \u003ccode>--context\u003c/code> explicitly in scripts. Role-based access (a later lesson) should also stop everyday accounts from deleting production workloads at all.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Editing Pods directly instead of the Deployment that owns them\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Forgetting the namespace (\u003ccode>-n\u003c/code>) and concluding that a resource is missing\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Treating Pod IP addresses as stable\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Not backing up etcd on self-managed clusters\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"the-components","text":"The components"},{"id":"declarative-and-reconciling","text":"Declarative and reconciling"},{"id":"core-objects","text":"Core objects"},{"id":"first-commands-on-any-cluster","text":"First commands on any cluster"},{"id":"incident-kubectl-is-pointed-at-the-wrong-cluster","text":"Incident: kubectl is pointed at the wrong cluster"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Kubernetes cluster architecture","u":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/architecture/"},{"t":"Kubernetes concepts","u":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/"},{"t":"kubectl quick reference","u":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/reference/kubectl/quick-reference/"}],"text":"You run one container on one server and it works. Now you need ten copies across five servers, automatic replacement when one dies, and updates without downtime. Doing that by hand does not scale. Kubernetes is the system that does it for you: you describe what you want, and it keeps working until reality matches. What you will learn The components of the control plane and of a worker node The declarative model and the reconcile loop The core objects you will meet in every cluster The first kubectl commands to run on an unfamiliar cluster The components A cluster is a set of machines called nodes . The control plane makes decisions; worker nodes run your containers. Component Where Job kube-apiserver Control plane The front door. Every client and component talks to it. It validates and stores requests etcd Control plane The database holding all cluster state. Back it up kube-scheduler Control plane Chooses a node for each new Pod kube-controller-manager Control plane Runs controllers that drive actual state toward desired state kubelet Every node Starts and monitors the containers assigned to its node kube-proxy Every node Maintains network rules so Services reach Pods Container runtime Every node Actually runs containers (containerd or CRI-O) Declarative and reconciling You do not tell Kubernetes how to do something. You submit a desired state , for example \"run 3 replicas of this image\", and controllers repeatedly compare it with the actual state and act on the difference. If a node dies and a replica disappears, the controller notices 2 is not 3 and creates a replacement. This loop is why Kubernetes heals itself, and it is the most important idea in this module. $ kubectl create deployment web --image=nginx:1.27 --replicas=3 deployment.apps/web created $ kubectl get pods -l app=web NAME READY STATUS RESTARTS AGE web-7c5ddbdf54-4xk2p 1/1 Running 0 20s web-7c5ddbdf54-9tq8m 1/1 Running 0 20s web-7c5ddbdf54-zl6vh 1/1 Running 0 20s $ kubectl delete pod web-7c5ddbdf54-4xk2p pod \"web-7c5ddbdf54-4xk2p\" deleted $ kubectl get pods -l app=web After the delete, run the last command again and you will see three Pods once more, one of them brand new. You asked for 3, so Kubernetes made it so. Core objects Object Purpose Pod One or more containers sharing a network and storage. The smallest unit you deploy Deployment Manages identical Pods, rolling updates and rollbacks Service A stable name and IP in front of a changing set of Pods ConfigMap / Secret Configuration and sensitive data, kept out of images Namespace A named partition of the cluster for teams or environments Node A machine in the cluster Objects are written as YAML with four top-level parts: apiVersion , kind , metadata and spec . You will write many in the coming lessons. First commands on any cluster $ kubectl config current-context prod-eu-west $ kubectl get nodes -o wide $ kubectl get pods -A $ kubectl describe node worker-2 | tail -15 $ kubectl api-resources | head $ kubectl explain pod.spec.containers Always check current-context first. Running a command against the wrong cluster is a classic and expensive mistake. kubectl explain documents any field from inside the terminal, which is also what you will rely on in an exam. Incident: kubectl is pointed at the wrong cluster Incident You run kubectl delete deployment web to clean up a test, and an alert fires: the production website is down. Your shell was still using the production context from earlier. Confirm and prevent: $ kubectl config get-contexts CURRENT NAME CLUSTER NAMESPACE * prod-eu-west prod-eu-west shop dev-sandbox dev-sandbox default $ kubectl config use-context dev-sandbox Restore service first by re-applying the Deployment manifest from your records ( kubectl apply -f web.yaml on the production context), then fix the habit: show the context in your shell prompt, use a separate kubeconfig file per environment, and add --context explicitly in scripts. Role-based access (a later lesson) should also stop every"},"kubernetes/pods-and-deployments":{"html":"\u003cp>A Deployment that merely &quot;runs the container&quot; is a Deployment that will hurt you. It will receive traffic before it is ready, hog or starve its neighbours, and roll out a broken version to every replica. Resource settings, probes and an update strategy turn a demo into something you can operate.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>The anatomy of a Pod and a Deployment\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Requests and limits, and what each does\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Readiness, liveness and startup probes\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Rolling updates, rollbacks and rollout status\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"a-production-shaped-deployment\">A production-shaped Deployment\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"yaml\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"apiVersion: apps/v1\nkind: Deployment\nmetadata:\n  name: shop-api\n  namespace: shop\nspec:\n  replicas: 3\n  selector:\n    matchLabels:\n      app: shop-api\n  strategy:\n    type: RollingUpdate\n    rollingUpdate:\n      maxUnavailable: 0\n      maxSurge: 1\n  template:\n    metadata:\n      labels:\n        app: shop-api\n    spec:\n      containers:\n        - name: api\n          image: registry.example.com/shop-api:1.8.2\n          ports:\n            - containerPort: 8080\n          resources:\n            requests:\n              cpu: 100m\n              memory: 128Mi\n            limits:\n              memory: 256Mi\n          readinessProbe:\n            httpGet:\n              path: /healthz/ready\n              port: 8080\n            periodSeconds: 5\n            failureThreshold: 3\n          livenessProbe:\n            httpGet:\n              path: /healthz/live\n              port: 8080\n            initialDelaySeconds: 10\n            periodSeconds: 10\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">apiVersion:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">apps/v1\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">kind:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">Deployment\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">metadata:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">shop-api\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">namespace:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">shop\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">spec:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">replicas:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">3\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">selector:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">matchLabels:\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">app:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">shop-api\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">strategy:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">type:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">RollingUpdate\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">rollingUpdate:\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">maxUnavailable:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">0\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">maxSurge:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">1\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">template:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">metadata:\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">labels:\u003c/span>\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">app:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">shop-api\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">spec:\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">containers:\u003c/span>\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">api\u003c/span>\n          \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">image:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">registry.example.com/shop-api:1.8.2\u003c/span>\n          \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">ports:\u003c/span>\n            \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">containerPort:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">8080\u003c/span>\n          \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">resources:\u003c/span>\n            \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">requests:\u003c/span>\n              \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">cpu:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">100m\u003c/span>\n              \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">memory:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">128Mi\u003c/span>\n            \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">limits:\u003c/span>\n              \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">memory:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">256Mi\u003c/span>\n          \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">readinessProbe:\u003c/span>\n            \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">httpGet:\u003c/span>\n              \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">path:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">/healthz/ready\u003c/span>\n              \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">port:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">8080\u003c/span>\n            \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">periodSeconds:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">5\u003c/span>\n            \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">failureThreshold:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">3\u003c/span>\n          \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">livenessProbe:\u003c/span>\n            \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">httpGet:\u003c/span>\n              \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">path:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">/healthz/live\u003c/span>\n              \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">port:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">8080\u003c/span>\n            \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">initialDelaySeconds:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">10\u003c/span>\n            \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">periodSeconds:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">10\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The \u003ccode>selector\u003c/code> must match the Pod template&#39;s labels; that link is how the Deployment knows which Pods it owns. Apply it with \u003ccode>kubectl apply -f shop-api.yaml\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"requests-and-limits\">Requests and limits\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"table-wrap\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\n\u003cth>Setting\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Meaning\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Effect\u003c/th>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>requests\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>What the container is guaranteed\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>The scheduler uses it to place the Pod\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>limits.memory\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>A hard ceiling\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Exceeding it gets the container killed (OOMKilled)\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>limits.cpu\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>A throttle\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Exceeding it slows the container; it is not killed\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Set a memory request near typical use and a memory limit with headroom. Many teams omit CPU limits to avoid throttling while still setting CPU requests. Without requests the scheduler is guessing, and nodes become overcommitted.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"probes\">Probes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Probes let Kubernetes ask your application how it is doing. They answer three different questions:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"table-wrap\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\n\u003cth>Probe\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Question\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Failure action\u003c/th>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003cstrong>Readiness\u003c/strong>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Can you take traffic right now?\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Pod removed from Service endpoints, not restarted\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003cstrong>Liveness\u003c/strong>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Are you stuck beyond recovery?\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Container restarted\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003cstrong>Startup\u003c/strong>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Have you finished starting?\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Holds off the other probes until it passes\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-warning\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Warning\u003c/div>\u003cp>Do not make a liveness probe depend on a database or another service. If the database is slow, every Pod fails its liveness check, all restart together, and a partial slowdown becomes a full outage. Liveness should only check the process itself.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003ch2 id=\"rolling-updates-and-rollbacks\">Rolling updates and rollbacks\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>With \u003ccode>maxUnavailable: 0\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>maxSurge: 1\u003c/code>, Kubernetes starts one new Pod, waits until it is \u003cstrong>ready\u003c/strong>, then removes one old Pod, and repeats. Readiness probes are therefore what stop a broken version from replacing a working one.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"kubectl set image deployment/shop-api api=registry.example.com/shop-api:1.9.0 -n shop\nkubectl rollout status deployment/shop-api -n shop\nkubectl rollout history deployment/shop-api -n shop\nkubectl rollout undo deployment/shop-api -n shop\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">set\u003c/span> image deployment/shop-api api=registry.example.com/shop-api:1.9.0 -n shop\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl rollout status deployment/shop-api -n shop\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">Waiting for deployment &quot;shop-api&quot; rollout to finish: 1 out of 3 new replicas have been updated...\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl rollout \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">history\u003c/span> deployment/shop-api -n shop\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl rollout undo deployment/shop-api -n shop\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">deployment.apps/shop-api rolled back\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-a-bad-release-stalls-halfway\">Incident: a bad release stalls halfway\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>After deploying 1.9.0, \u003ccode>rollout status\u003c/code> hangs. Users are fine, but the release never finishes.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"kubectl get pods -n shop -l app=shop-api\nkubectl describe pod shop-api-84c6b7d4f-ddddd -n shop | tail -5\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl get pods -n shop -l app=shop-api\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">NAME                        READY   STATUS    RESTARTS   AGE\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">shop-api-6d8f7c9b5-aaaaa    1/1     Running   0          2d\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">shop-api-6d8f7c9b5-bbbbb    1/1     Running   0          2d\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">shop-api-6d8f7c9b5-ccccc    1/1     Running   0          2d\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">shop-api-84c6b7d4f-ddddd    0/1     Running   0          4m\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl describe pod shop-api-84c6b7d4f-ddddd -n shop | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">tail\u003c/span> -5\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">  Warning  Unhealthy  3m  kubelet  Readiness probe failed: HTTP probe failed with statuscode: 503\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The new Pod runs but never becomes ready, so the rollout cannot continue. This is the strategy working as designed: the three healthy old Pods keep serving and users noticed nothing. Look at its logs (\u003ccode>kubectl logs shop-api-84c6b7d4f-ddddd -n shop\u003c/code>), find the cause, then either ship a fix or abandon the release with \u003ccode>kubectl rollout undo\u003c/code>. After a failed attempt, \u003ccode>kubectl rollout status\u003c/code> returns an error once the progress deadline passes, which your pipeline can use to fail the deploy automatically.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"predict-the-rollout\">Predict the rollout\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"predict\">\u003cp class=\"predict-q\">\u003cstrong>Predict first.\u003c/strong> A Deployment has 3 replicas, maxUnavailable 1 and maxSurge 1. You update the image. How many pods can be unavailable at one time?\u003c/p>\u003clabel class=\"predict-l\">Your guess\u003ctextarea rows=\"2\" class=\"predict-guess\" placeholder=\"Type what you expect, then reveal the answer.\">\u003c/textarea>\u003c/label>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-primary\" data-act=\"predict-reveal\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Reveal the answer\u003c/button>\u003cdiv class=\"predict-out\" hidden>\u003cp class=\"predict-ans\">\u003cstrong>Answer:\u003c/strong> At most one.\u003c/p>\u003cp>maxUnavailable caps pods that are not ready during the rollout. maxSurge allows one extra pod, so up to four can exist.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n\u003cdiv class=\"predict\">\u003cp class=\"predict-q\">\u003cstrong>Predict first.\u003c/strong> A pod's readiness probe fails. Is the pod restarted?\u003c/p>\u003cpre class=\"predict-code\" tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode>readinessProbe:\n  httpGet: {path: /ready, port: 8080}\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003clabel class=\"predict-l\">Your guess\u003ctextarea rows=\"2\" class=\"predict-guess\" placeholder=\"Type what you expect, then reveal the answer.\">\u003c/textarea>\u003c/label>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-primary\" data-act=\"predict-reveal\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Reveal the answer\u003c/button>\u003cdiv class=\"predict-out\" hidden>\u003cp class=\"predict-ans\">\u003cstrong>Answer:\u003c/strong> No. It is removed from Service endpoints until it passes.\u003c/p>\u003cp>A failing readiness probe stops traffic. A failing liveness probe is what causes a restart.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>No readiness probe, so traffic arrives before the app can handle it\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Liveness probes that check dependencies\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>No memory limit, or a limit equal to average use\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Using \u003ccode>latest\u003c/code> tags so a rollback cannot go back to anything\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Selector and template labels that do not match\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"a-production-shaped-deployment","text":"A production-shaped Deployment"},{"id":"requests-and-limits","text":"Requests and limits"},{"id":"probes","text":"Probes"},{"id":"rolling-updates-and-rollbacks","text":"Rolling updates and rollbacks"},{"id":"incident-a-bad-release-stalls-halfway","text":"Incident: a bad release stalls halfway"},{"id":"predict-the-rollout","text":"Predict the rollout"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Kubernetes: Pods","u":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/workloads/pods/"},{"t":"Kubernetes: Deployments","u":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/workloads/controllers/deployment/"},{"t":"Kubernetes: liveness, readiness and startup probes","u":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/tasks/configure-pod-container/configure-liveness-readiness-startup-probes/"}],"text":"A Deployment that merely \"runs the container\" is a Deployment that will hurt you. It will receive traffic before it is ready, hog or starve its neighbours, and roll out a broken version to every replica. Resource settings, probes and an update strategy turn a demo into something you can operate. What you will learn The anatomy of a Pod and a Deployment Requests and limits, and what each does Readiness, liveness and startup probes Rolling updates, rollbacks and rollout status A production-shaped Deployment apiVersion: apps/v1 kind: Deployment metadata: name: shop-api namespace: shop spec: replicas: 3 selector: matchLabels: app: shop-api strategy: type: RollingUpdate rollingUpdate: maxUnavailable: 0 maxSurge: 1 template: metadata: labels: app: shop-api spec: containers: - name: api image: registry.example.com/shop-api:1.8.2 ports: - containerPort: 8080 resources: requests: cpu: 100m memory: 128Mi limits: memory: 256Mi readinessProbe: httpGet: path: /healthz/ready port: 8080 periodSeconds: 5 failureThreshold: 3 livenessProbe: httpGet: path: /healthz/live port: 8080 initialDelaySeconds: 10 periodSeconds: 10 The selector must match the Pod template&#39;s labels; that link is how the Deployment knows which Pods it owns. Apply it with kubectl apply -f shop-api.yaml . Requests and limits Setting Meaning Effect requests What the container is guaranteed The scheduler uses it to place the Pod limits.memory A hard ceiling Exceeding it gets the container killed (OOMKilled) limits.cpu A throttle Exceeding it slows the container; it is not killed Set a memory request near typical use and a memory limit with headroom. Many teams omit CPU limits to avoid throttling while still setting CPU requests. Without requests the scheduler is guessing, and nodes become overcommitted. Probes Probes let Kubernetes ask your application how it is doing. They answer three different questions: Probe Question Failure action Readiness Can you take traffic right now? Pod removed from Service endpoints, not restarted Liveness Are you stuck beyond recovery? Container restarted Startup Have you finished starting? Holds off the other probes until it passes Warning Do not make a liveness probe depend on a database or another service. If the database is slow, every Pod fails its liveness check, all restart together, and a partial slowdown becomes a full outage. Liveness should only check the process itself. Rolling updates and rollbacks With maxUnavailable: 0 and maxSurge: 1 , Kubernetes starts one new Pod, waits until it is ready , then removes one old Pod, and repeats. Readiness probes are therefore what stop a broken version from replacing a working one. $ kubectl set image deployment/shop-api api=registry.example.com/shop-api:1.9.0 -n shop $ kubectl rollout status deployment/shop-api -n shop Waiting for deployment \"shop-api\" rollout to finish: 1 out of 3 new replicas have been updated... $ kubectl rollout history deployment/shop-api -n shop $ kubectl rollout undo deployment/shop-api -n shop deployment.apps/shop-api rolled back Incident: a bad release stalls halfway Incident After deploying 1.9.0, rollout status hangs. Users are fine, but the release never finishes. $ kubectl get pods -n shop -l app=shop-api NAME READY STATUS RESTARTS AGE shop-api-6d8f7c9b5-aaaaa 1/1 Running 0 2d shop-api-6d8f7c9b5-bbbbb 1/1 Running 0 2d shop-api-6d8f7c9b5-ccccc 1/1 Running 0 2d shop-api-84c6b7d4f-ddddd 0/1 Running 0 4m $ kubectl describe pod shop-api-84c6b7d4f-ddddd -n shop | tail -5 Warning Unhealthy 3m kubelet Readiness probe failed: HTTP probe failed with statuscode: 503 The new Pod runs but never becomes ready, so the rollout cannot continue. This is the strategy working as designed: the three healthy old Pods keep serving and users noticed nothing. Look at its logs ( kubectl logs shop-api-84c6b7d4f-ddddd -n shop ), find the cause, then either ship a fix or abandon the release with kubectl rollout undo . After a failed attempt, kubectl rollout status returns an error once the p"},"kubernetes/services-and-ingress":{"html":"\u003cp>Pods are disposable. Each replacement gets a new IP address, so nothing can rely on one. A \u003cstrong>Service\u003c/strong> solves that by providing a stable name and virtual IP in front of whichever Pods currently match. Most &quot;it cannot connect&quot; problems in Kubernetes come down to Services that select nothing.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>The Service types and when to use each\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>How labels connect a Service to Pods\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Cluster DNS names\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Ingress for HTTP routing, and NetworkPolicy for restriction\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"services\">Services\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"yaml\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"apiVersion: v1\nkind: Service\nmetadata:\n  name: shop-api\n  namespace: shop\nspec:\n  selector:\n    app: shop-api\n  ports:\n    - port: 80\n      targetPort: 8080\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">apiVersion:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">v1\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">kind:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">Service\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">metadata:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">shop-api\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">namespace:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">shop\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">spec:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">selector:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">app:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">shop-api\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">ports:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">port:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">80\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">targetPort:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">8080\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The Service sends traffic arriving on port 80 to port 8080 of every \u003cstrong>ready\u003c/strong> Pod that has the label \u003ccode>app: shop-api\u003c/code>. The list of those Pod addresses is called the Service&#39;s \u003cstrong>endpoints\u003c/strong>.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"table-wrap\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\n\u003cth>Type\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Reachable from\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Typical use\u003c/th>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>ClusterIP\u003c/code> (default)\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Inside the cluster\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Internal service-to-service calls\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>NodePort\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Any node&#39;s IP on a high port\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Simple tests, on-premises setups\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>LoadBalancer\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>A cloud load balancer&#39;s address\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Exposing one service directly\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>ExternalName\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Inside the cluster, as a DNS alias\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Pointing at an external hostname\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"cluster-dns\">Cluster DNS\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Every Service gets a DNS name: \u003ccode>&lt;service&gt;.&lt;namespace&gt;.svc.cluster.local\u003c/code>. Within the same namespace the short name \u003ccode>shop-api\u003c/code> works; from another namespace use \u003ccode>shop-api.shop\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"kubectl run tmp --rm -it --image=busybox:1.36 --restart=Never -n shop -- nslookup shop-api\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl run tmp --\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">rm\u003c/span> -it --image=busybox:1.36 --restart=Never -n shop -- nslookup shop-api\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">Name:      shop-api.shop.svc.cluster.local\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">Address 1: 10.96.142.7\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>This throwaway Pod is the standard way to test connectivity from inside the cluster.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"ingress\">Ingress\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Creating a \u003ccode>LoadBalancer\u003c/code> per service gets expensive. An \u003cstrong>Ingress\u003c/strong> routes HTTP and HTTPS traffic from one entry point to many Services by host or path. It needs an \u003cstrong>Ingress controller\u003c/strong> (for example ingress-nginx) running in the cluster; the Ingress object alone does nothing.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"yaml\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1\nkind: Ingress\nmetadata:\n  name: shop\n  namespace: shop\nspec:\n  ingressClassName: nginx\n  tls:\n    - hosts: [shop.example.com]\n      secretName: shop-tls\n  rules:\n    - host: shop.example.com\n      http:\n        paths:\n          - path: /api\n            pathType: Prefix\n            backend:\n              service:\n                name: shop-api\n                port:\n                  number: 80\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">apiVersion:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">networking.k8s.io/v1\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">kind:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">Ingress\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">metadata:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">shop\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">namespace:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">shop\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">spec:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">ingressClassName:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">nginx\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">tls:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">hosts:\u003c/span> [\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">shop.example.com\u003c/span>]\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">secretName:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">shop-tls\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">rules:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">host:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">shop.example.com\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">http:\u003c/span>\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">paths:\u003c/span>\n          \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">path:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">/api\u003c/span>\n            \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">pathType:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">Prefix\u003c/span>\n            \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">backend:\u003c/span>\n              \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">service:\u003c/span>\n                \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">shop-api\u003c/span>\n                \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">port:\u003c/span>\n                  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">number:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">80\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"networkpolicy\">NetworkPolicy\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>By default every Pod can talk to every other Pod. A NetworkPolicy restricts that, but only if your network plugin enforces policies. A common pattern is to deny everything and then allow what is needed:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"yaml\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1\nkind: NetworkPolicy\nmetadata:\n  name: db-allow-api-only\n  namespace: shop\nspec:\n  podSelector:\n    matchLabels:\n      app: postgres\n  policyTypes: [Ingress]\n  ingress:\n    - from:\n        - podSelector:\n            matchLabels:\n              app: shop-api\n      ports:\n        - port: 5432\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">apiVersion:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">networking.k8s.io/v1\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">kind:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">NetworkPolicy\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">metadata:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">db-allow-api-only\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">namespace:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">shop\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">spec:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">podSelector:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">matchLabels:\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">app:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">postgres\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">policyTypes:\u003c/span> [\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">Ingress\u003c/span>]\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">ingress:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">from:\u003c/span>\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">podSelector:\u003c/span>\n            \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">matchLabels:\u003c/span>\n              \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">app:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">shop-api\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">ports:\u003c/span>\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">port:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">5432\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-service-has-no-endpoints\">Incident: the Service has no endpoints\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>\u003ccode>curl http://shop-api\u003c/code> from another Pod times out or is refused, yet \u003ccode>kubectl get pods\u003c/code> shows all three Pods running.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"kubectl get endpoints shop-api -n shop\nkubectl get svc shop-api -n shop -o jsonpath='{.spec.selector}'\nkubectl get pods -n shop --show-labels\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl get endpoints shop-api -n shop\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">NAME       ENDPOINTS   AGE\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">shop-api   &lt;none&gt;      9m\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl get svc shop-api -n shop -o jsonpath=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;{.spec.selector}&#x27;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">{&quot;app&quot;:&quot;shop-api&quot;}\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl get pods -n shop --show-labels\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">NAME                       READY   STATUS    LABELS\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">shop-api-84c6b7d4f-ddddd   1/1     Running   app=shop-backend,pod-template-hash=84c6b7d4f\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>&lt;none&gt;\u003c/code> is the clue. The Service selects \u003ccode>app=shop-api\u003c/code>, but the Pods carry \u003ccode>app=shop-backend\u003c/code>, so nothing matches. Fix the label in the Deployment template or the selector in the Service so they agree, apply, and check that the endpoints list appears. A second cause of empty endpoints is Pods that are running but not \u003cstrong>ready\u003c/strong>, so always look at both labels and the READY column. A third is the wrong \u003ccode>targetPort\u003c/code>, which gives you endpoints but connection refused.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"3\" data-edges=\"cl&gt;lb;lb&gt;ing;ing&gt;svc;svc&gt;pod\" style=\"--rows:3\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">How a request reaches a pod\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"50.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"50.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"50.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"16.7\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"16.7\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"83.3\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"83.3\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"50.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-client\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"cl\" data-kind=\"client\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:50.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Client\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Client\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-edge\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"lb\" data-kind=\"edge\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:50.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Load balancer\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Network edge\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-edge\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"ing\" data-kind=\"edge\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:16.7%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Ingress\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Network edge\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-service\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"svc\" data-kind=\"service\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:83.3%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Service\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-service\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"pod\" data-kind=\"service\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:50.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Pod\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Client to Load balancer; 2, Load balancer to Ingress; 3, Ingress to Service; 4, Service to Pod.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-client\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Client\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-edge\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Network edge\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-service\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Service\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"cl\">\u003cdt>Client \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Client\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Sends HTTPS to the public name.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> A wrong DNS record sends it elsewhere.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"lb\">\u003cdt>Load balancer \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Network edge\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Forwards to the ingress controller.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> If its health check fails, no traffic passes.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"ing\">\u003cdt>Ingress \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Network edge\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Matches host and path and picks a Service.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> A wrong path rule returns 404 from the controller.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"svc\">\u003cdt>Service \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Gives a stable address and selects pods by label.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> A selector that matches no pods gives empty endpoints.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"pod\">\u003cdt>Pod \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Receives the request on the container port.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> A wrong targetPort gives connection refused.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Mismatched labels between Service selector and Pods\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Using the Pod port as \u003ccode>port\u003c/code> and the Service port as \u003ccode>targetPort\u003c/code> the wrong way round\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Creating an Ingress without an Ingress controller\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Writing NetworkPolicies on a network plugin that does not enforce them\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"services","text":"Services"},{"id":"cluster-dns","text":"Cluster DNS"},{"id":"ingress","text":"Ingress"},{"id":"networkpolicy","text":"NetworkPolicy"},{"id":"incident-the-service-has-no-endpoints","text":"Incident: the Service has no endpoints"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Kubernetes: Service","u":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/services-networking/service/"},{"t":"Kubernetes: Ingress","u":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/services-networking/ingress/"},{"t":"Kubernetes: DNS for Services and Pods","u":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/services-networking/dns-pod-service/"}],"text":"Pods are disposable. Each replacement gets a new IP address, so nothing can rely on one. A Service solves that by providing a stable name and virtual IP in front of whichever Pods currently match. Most \"it cannot connect\" problems in Kubernetes come down to Services that select nothing. What you will learn The Service types and when to use each How labels connect a Service to Pods Cluster DNS names Ingress for HTTP routing, and NetworkPolicy for restriction Services apiVersion: v1 kind: Service metadata: name: shop-api namespace: shop spec: selector: app: shop-api ports: - port: 80 targetPort: 8080 The Service sends traffic arriving on port 80 to port 8080 of every ready Pod that has the label app: shop-api . The list of those Pod addresses is called the Service&#39;s endpoints . Type Reachable from Typical use ClusterIP (default) Inside the cluster Internal service-to-service calls NodePort Any node&#39;s IP on a high port Simple tests, on-premises setups LoadBalancer A cloud load balancer&#39;s address Exposing one service directly ExternalName Inside the cluster, as a DNS alias Pointing at an external hostname Cluster DNS Every Service gets a DNS name: \u003cservice>.\u003cnamespace>.svc.cluster.local . Within the same namespace the short name shop-api works; from another namespace use shop-api.shop . $ kubectl run tmp -- rm -it --image=busybox:1.36 --restart=Never -n shop -- nslookup shop-api Name: shop-api.shop.svc.cluster.local Address 1: 10.96.142.7 This throwaway Pod is the standard way to test connectivity from inside the cluster. Ingress Creating a LoadBalancer per service gets expensive. An Ingress routes HTTP and HTTPS traffic from one entry point to many Services by host or path. It needs an Ingress controller (for example ingress-nginx) running in the cluster; the Ingress object alone does nothing. apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1 kind: Ingress metadata: name: shop namespace: shop spec: ingressClassName: nginx tls: - hosts: [ shop.example.com ] secretName: shop-tls rules: - host: shop.example.com http: paths: - path: /api pathType: Prefix backend: service: name: shop-api port: number: 80 NetworkPolicy By default every Pod can talk to every other Pod. A NetworkPolicy restricts that, but only if your network plugin enforces policies. A common pattern is to deny everything and then allow what is needed: apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1 kind: NetworkPolicy metadata: name: db-allow-api-only namespace: shop spec: podSelector: matchLabels: app: postgres policyTypes: [ Ingress ] ingress: - from: - podSelector: matchLabels: app: shop-api ports: - port: 5432 Incident: the Service has no endpoints Incident curl http://shop-api from another Pod times out or is refused, yet kubectl get pods shows all three Pods running. $ kubectl get endpoints shop-api -n shop NAME ENDPOINTS AGE shop-api \u003cnone> 9m $ kubectl get svc shop-api -n shop -o jsonpath= &#x27;{.spec.selector}&#x27; {\"app\":\"shop-api\"} $ kubectl get pods -n shop --show-labels NAME READY STATUS LABELS shop-api-84c6b7d4f-ddddd 1/1 Running app=shop-backend,pod-template-hash=84c6b7d4f \u003cnone> is the clue. The Service selects app=shop-api , but the Pods carry app=shop-backend , so nothing matches. Fix the label in the Deployment template or the selector in the Service so they agree, apply, and check that the endpoints list appears. A second cause of empty endpoints is Pods that are running but not ready , so always look at both labels and the READY column. A third is the wrong targetPort , which gives you endpoints but connection refused. How a request reaches a pod Flow in order: 1, Client to Load balancer; 2, Load balancer to Ingress; 3, Ingress to Service; 4, Service to Pod. Client Network edge Service Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Client Client What it does: Sends HTTPS to the public name. If it fails or is weak: A wrong DNS record sends it elsewhere. Load balancer Network edge What it does: Forward"},"kubernetes/config-and-secrets":{"html":"\u003cp>One image should run unchanged in development, staging and production. What changes between them is configuration: database hostnames, feature flags, credentials. Kubernetes keeps that configuration in separate objects and injects it when the Pod starts.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>ConfigMaps for non-sensitive settings\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Secrets for sensitive values, and their real limits\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Injecting as environment variables versus mounted files\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>What happens when configuration changes\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"configmap\">ConfigMap\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"yaml\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"apiVersion: v1\nkind: ConfigMap\nmetadata:\n  name: shop-config\n  namespace: shop\ndata:\n  LOG_LEVEL: info\n  FEATURE_NEW_CHECKOUT: &quot;true&quot;\n  app.properties: |\n    cache.ttl=300\n    region=eu-west\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">apiVersion:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">v1\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">kind:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">ConfigMap\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">metadata:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">shop-config\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">namespace:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">shop\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">data:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">LOG_LEVEL:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">info\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">FEATURE_NEW_CHECKOUT:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;true&quot;\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">app.properties:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">|\n    cache.ttl=300\n    region=eu-west\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Values are strings, so booleans and numbers are quoted. Keys can hold single values or whole files.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"secret\">Secret\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"yaml\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"apiVersion: v1\nkind: Secret\nmetadata:\n  name: shop-db\n  namespace: shop\ntype: Opaque\nstringData:\n  username: app\n  password: change-me\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">apiVersion:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">v1\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">kind:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">Secret\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">metadata:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">shop-db\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">namespace:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">shop\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">type:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">Opaque\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">stringData:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">username:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">app\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">password:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">change-me\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>stringData\u003c/code> accepts plain text and Kubernetes stores it Base64-encoded in \u003ccode>data\u003c/code>. \u003c/p>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-warning\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Warning\u003c/div>\u003cp>Base64 is an encoding, not encryption. Anyone who can read the Secret object can decode it with \u003ccode>base64 -d\u003c/code>. Real protection comes from three things: RBAC that limits who can read Secrets, encryption at rest for etcd, and ideally an external secret manager that syncs values in. Never commit Secret manifests containing real values to a shared repository.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003ch2 id=\"using-them-in-a-pod\">Using them in a Pod\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"yaml\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"spec:\n  containers:\n    - name: api\n      image: registry.example.com/shop-api:1.8.2\n      env:\n        - name: LOG_LEVEL\n          valueFrom:\n            configMapKeyRef:\n              name: shop-config\n              key: LOG_LEVEL\n        - name: DB_PASSWORD\n          valueFrom:\n            secretKeyRef:\n              name: shop-db\n              key: password\n      volumeMounts:\n        - name: config\n          mountPath: /etc/shop\n          readOnly: true\n  volumes:\n    - name: config\n      configMap:\n        name: shop-config\n        items:\n          - key: app.properties\n            path: app.properties\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">spec:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">containers:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">api\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">image:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">registry.example.com/shop-api:1.8.2\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">env:\u003c/span>\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">LOG_LEVEL\u003c/span>\n          \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">valueFrom:\u003c/span>\n            \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">configMapKeyRef:\u003c/span>\n              \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">shop-config\u003c/span>\n              \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">key:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">LOG_LEVEL\u003c/span>\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">DB_PASSWORD\u003c/span>\n          \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">valueFrom:\u003c/span>\n            \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">secretKeyRef:\u003c/span>\n              \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">shop-db\u003c/span>\n              \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">key:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">password\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">volumeMounts:\u003c/span>\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">config\u003c/span>\n          \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">mountPath:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">/etc/shop\u003c/span>\n          \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">readOnly:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-literal\">true\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">volumes:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">config\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">configMap:\u003c/span>\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">shop-config\u003c/span>\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">items:\u003c/span>\n          \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">key:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">app.properties\u003c/span>\n            \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">path:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">app.properties\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cdiv class=\"table-wrap\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\n\u003cth>Method\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Updates when the object changes?\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Notes\u003c/th>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Environment variable\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>No, only at Pod start\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Simple; values appear in process environment\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Mounted volume\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Yes, files refresh after a short delay\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>The application must re-read the file\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Mounted files are usually the better choice for secrets, because environment variables leak easily into crash dumps and debug output.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"verifying-what-a-pod-sees\">Verifying what a Pod sees\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"kubectl exec deploy/shop-api -n shop -- printenv LOG_LEVEL\nkubectl exec deploy/shop-api -n shop -- cat /etc/shop/app.properties\nkubectl get secret shop-db -n shop -o jsonpath='{.data.password}' | base64 -d\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">exec\u003c/span> deploy/shop-api -n shop -- \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">printenv\u003c/span> LOG_LEVEL\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">info\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">exec\u003c/span> deploy/shop-api -n shop -- \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">cat\u003c/span> /etc/shop/app.properties\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">cache.ttl=300\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">region=eu-west\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl get secret shop-db -n shop -o jsonpath=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;{.data.password}&#x27;\u003c/span> | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">base64\u003c/span> -d\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-changing-the-configmap-did-nothing\">Incident: changing the ConfigMap did nothing\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>You change \u003ccode>LOG_LEVEL\u003c/code> from \u003ccode>info\u003c/code> to \u003ccode>debug\u003c/code> in the ConfigMap and apply it. The application keeps logging at \u003ccode>info\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"kubectl get configmap shop-config -n shop -o jsonpath='{.data.LOG_LEVEL}'\nkubectl exec deploy/shop-api -n shop -- printenv LOG_LEVEL\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl get configmap shop-config -n shop -o jsonpath=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;{.data.LOG_LEVEL}&#x27;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">debug\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">exec\u003c/span> deploy/shop-api -n shop -- \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">printenv\u003c/span> LOG_LEVEL\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">info\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The object is updated, but the running Pods still hold the old value. Environment variables are read once when the container starts. Restart the Pods to pick up the change:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"kubectl rollout restart deployment/shop-api -n shop\nkubectl rollout status deployment/shop-api -n shop\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl rollout restart deployment/shop-api -n shop\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl rollout status deployment/shop-api -n shop\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>For a durable approach, many teams include a hash of the config in the Pod template (Helm and Kustomize can do this), so any config change automatically triggers a rolling update and the change is recorded as a new revision you can roll back.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Treating Base64 as secure\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Expecting environment variables to refresh without a restart\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Putting secrets in images, ConfigMaps or Deployment manifests\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Naming a key wrongly, which leaves the Pod stuck in \u003ccode>CreateContainerConfigError\u003c/code>\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"configmap","text":"ConfigMap"},{"id":"secret","text":"Secret"},{"id":"using-them-in-a-pod","text":"Using them in a Pod"},{"id":"verifying-what-a-pod-sees","text":"Verifying what a Pod sees"},{"id":"incident-changing-the-configmap-did-nothing","text":"Incident: changing the ConfigMap did nothing"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Kubernetes: ConfigMaps","u":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/configuration/configmap/"},{"t":"Kubernetes: Secrets","u":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/configuration/secret/"},{"t":"The Twelve-Factor App","u":"https://12factor.net/"}],"text":"One image should run unchanged in development, staging and production. What changes between them is configuration: database hostnames, feature flags, credentials. Kubernetes keeps that configuration in separate objects and injects it when the Pod starts. What you will learn ConfigMaps for non-sensitive settings Secrets for sensitive values, and their real limits Injecting as environment variables versus mounted files What happens when configuration changes ConfigMap apiVersion: v1 kind: ConfigMap metadata: name: shop-config namespace: shop data: LOG_LEVEL: info FEATURE_NEW_CHECKOUT: \"true\" app.properties: | cache.ttl=300 region=eu-west Values are strings, so booleans and numbers are quoted. Keys can hold single values or whole files. Secret apiVersion: v1 kind: Secret metadata: name: shop-db namespace: shop type: Opaque stringData: username: app password: change-me stringData accepts plain text and Kubernetes stores it Base64-encoded in data . Warning Base64 is an encoding, not encryption. Anyone who can read the Secret object can decode it with base64 -d . Real protection comes from three things: RBAC that limits who can read Secrets, encryption at rest for etcd, and ideally an external secret manager that syncs values in. Never commit Secret manifests containing real values to a shared repository. Using them in a Pod spec: containers: - name: api image: registry.example.com/shop-api:1.8.2 env: - name: LOG_LEVEL valueFrom: configMapKeyRef: name: shop-config key: LOG_LEVEL - name: DB_PASSWORD valueFrom: secretKeyRef: name: shop-db key: password volumeMounts: - name: config mountPath: /etc/shop readOnly: true volumes: - name: config configMap: name: shop-config items: - key: app.properties path: app.properties Method Updates when the object changes? Notes Environment variable No, only at Pod start Simple; values appear in process environment Mounted volume Yes, files refresh after a short delay The application must re-read the file Mounted files are usually the better choice for secrets, because environment variables leak easily into crash dumps and debug output. Verifying what a Pod sees $ kubectl exec deploy/shop-api -n shop -- printenv LOG_LEVEL info $ kubectl exec deploy/shop-api -n shop -- cat /etc/shop/app.properties cache.ttl=300 region=eu-west $ kubectl get secret shop-db -n shop -o jsonpath= &#x27;{.data.password}&#x27; | base64 -d Incident: changing the ConfigMap did nothing Incident You change LOG_LEVEL from info to debug in the ConfigMap and apply it. The application keeps logging at info . $ kubectl get configmap shop-config -n shop -o jsonpath= &#x27;{.data.LOG_LEVEL}&#x27; debug $ kubectl exec deploy/shop-api -n shop -- printenv LOG_LEVEL info The object is updated, but the running Pods still hold the old value. Environment variables are read once when the container starts. Restart the Pods to pick up the change: $ kubectl rollout restart deployment/shop-api -n shop $ kubectl rollout status deployment/shop-api -n shop For a durable approach, many teams include a hash of the config in the Pod template (Helm and Kustomize can do this), so any config change automatically triggers a rolling update and the change is recorded as a new revision you can roll back. Common mistakes Treating Base64 as secure Expecting environment variables to refresh without a restart Putting secrets in images, ConfigMaps or Deployment manifests Naming a key wrongly, which leaves the Pod stuck in CreateContainerConfigError"},"kubernetes/storage":{"html":"\u003cp>Containers are disposable, but a database is not. When a Pod running PostgreSQL is rescheduled to another node, its data must follow it. Kubernetes separates the \u003cem>request\u003c/em> for storage from the \u003cem>supply\u003c/em> of storage, so applications do not need to know where disks come from.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>PersistentVolume, PersistentVolumeClaim and StorageClass\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Access modes and reclaim policies\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>When to use a StatefulSet instead of a Deployment\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>How to diagnose a Pod stuck waiting for a volume\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"the-three-objects\">The three objects\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"table-wrap\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\n\u003cth>Object\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Role\u003c/th>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003cstrong>StorageClass\u003c/strong>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Describes a kind of storage (fast SSD, cheap HDD) and how to provision it\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003cstrong>PersistentVolumeClaim (PVC)\u003c/strong>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>An application&#39;s request: &quot;I need 10Gi of this class&quot;\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003cstrong>PersistentVolume (PV)\u003c/strong>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>The actual disk. Usually created automatically when a claim appears\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>With dynamic provisioning you only write the claim; the StorageClass creates the disk and the PV for you.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"yaml\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"apiVersion: v1\nkind: PersistentVolumeClaim\nmetadata:\n  name: shop-uploads\n  namespace: shop\nspec:\n  accessModes: [ReadWriteOnce]\n  storageClassName: standard\n  resources:\n    requests:\n      storage: 10Gi\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">apiVersion:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">v1\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">kind:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">PersistentVolumeClaim\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">metadata:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">shop-uploads\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">namespace:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">shop\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">spec:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">accessModes:\u003c/span> [\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">ReadWriteOnce\u003c/span>]\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">storageClassName:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">standard\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">resources:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">requests:\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">storage:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">10Gi\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"yaml\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"# In the Pod template\n      containers:\n        - name: api\n          volumeMounts:\n            - name: uploads\n              mountPath: /var/lib/shop/uploads\n      volumes:\n        - name: uploads\n          persistentVolumeClaim:\n            claimName: shop-uploads\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># In the Pod template\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">containers:\u003c/span>\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">api\u003c/span>\n          \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">volumeMounts:\u003c/span>\n            \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">uploads\u003c/span>\n              \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">mountPath:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">/var/lib/shop/uploads\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">volumes:\u003c/span>\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">uploads\u003c/span>\n          \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">persistentVolumeClaim:\u003c/span>\n            \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">claimName:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">shop-uploads\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"access-modes-and-reclaim-policy\">Access modes and reclaim policy\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"table-wrap\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\n\u003cth>Access mode\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Meaning\u003c/th>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>ReadWriteOnce\u003c/code> (RWO)\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Mounted read-write by one node at a time\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>ReadOnlyMany\u003c/code> (ROX)\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Mounted read-only by many nodes\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>ReadWriteMany\u003c/code> (RWX)\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Mounted read-write by many nodes; needs a storage system that supports it, such as NFS or a managed file service\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>ReadWriteOncePod\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Mounted read-write by a single Pod\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The \u003cstrong>reclaim policy\u003c/strong> decides what happens to the disk when the claim is deleted. \u003ccode>Delete\u003c/code> removes the disk and its data; \u003ccode>Retain\u003c/code> keeps it for manual recovery. Production databases should use a class with \u003ccode>Retain\u003c/code>, or you should take snapshots, because deleting a claim is one command.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"statefulsets\">StatefulSets\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A Deployment treats Pods as interchangeable. A \u003cstrong>StatefulSet\u003c/strong> gives each Pod a stable identity (\u003ccode>db-0\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>db-1\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>db-2\u003c/code>), starts and stops them in order, and creates a separate PVC for each through \u003ccode>volumeClaimTemplates\u003c/code>. Use it for databases and clustered systems where each member has its own data and name. A \u003cstrong>headless Service\u003c/strong> (\u003ccode>clusterIP: None\u003c/code>) gives each Pod its own DNS entry such as \u003ccode>db-0.db.shop.svc.cluster.local\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"kubectl get pvc -n shop\nkubectl get sc\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl get pvc -n shop\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">NAME            STATUS   VOLUME                                     CAPACITY   ACCESS MODES   STORAGECLASS\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">data-db-0       Bound    pvc-2f1c8d0e-...                           20Gi       RWO            standard\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">data-db-1       Bound    pvc-7a93b1c4-...                           20Gi       RWO            standard\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl get sc\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>If you only need a single database for a small service, a managed database service from your cloud provider is usually safer than operating one yourself on Kubernetes.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-pod-is-stuck-pending-with-an-unbound-claim\">Incident: the Pod is stuck Pending with an unbound claim\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A new Pod stays \u003ccode>Pending\u003c/code> for ten minutes after you added a volume.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"kubectl get pvc shop-uploads -n shop\nkubectl describe pvc shop-uploads -n shop | tail -4\nkubectl get sc\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl get pvc shop-uploads -n shop\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">NAME           STATUS    VOLUME   CAPACITY   ACCESS MODES   STORAGECLASS\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">shop-uploads   Pending                                      fast-ssd\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl describe pvc shop-uploads -n shop | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">tail\u003c/span> -4\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">Events:\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">  Warning  ProvisioningFailed  persistentvolume-controller  storageclass.storage.k8s.io &quot;fast-ssd&quot; not found\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl get sc\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">NAME                 PROVISIONER             RECLAIMPOLICY\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">standard (default)   ebs.csi.aws.com         Delete\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The claim names a StorageClass that does not exist, so nothing provisions a disk and the Pod cannot be scheduled. Correct \u003ccode>storageClassName\u003c/code> to a real class (here \u003ccode>standard\u003c/code>), delete the pending claim and apply it again, since most claim fields cannot be changed after creation. Other frequent causes: the requested access mode is not supported by the class, the zone has no capacity, or the volume exists in a different availability zone than the node the Pod landed on. Always read the events at the bottom of \u003ccode>kubectl describe\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Assuming a PVC is deleted with its Pod (it is not) or that data survives claim deletion (it may not)\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Using ReadWriteOnce volumes for a multi-replica Deployment on different nodes\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Running databases on \u003ccode>emptyDir\u003c/code>, which dies with the Pod\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Skipping backups because &quot;the disk is persistent&quot;\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"the-three-objects","text":"The three objects"},{"id":"access-modes-and-reclaim-policy","text":"Access modes and reclaim policy"},{"id":"statefulsets","text":"StatefulSets"},{"id":"incident-the-pod-is-stuck-pending-with-an-unbound-claim","text":"Incident: the Pod is stuck Pending with an unbound claim"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Kubernetes: Storage","u":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/storage/"},{"t":"Kubernetes: StatefulSets","u":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/workloads/controllers/statefulset/"},{"t":"kubectl quick reference","u":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/reference/kubectl/quick-reference/"}],"text":"Containers are disposable, but a database is not. When a Pod running PostgreSQL is rescheduled to another node, its data must follow it. Kubernetes separates the request for storage from the supply of storage, so applications do not need to know where disks come from. What you will learn PersistentVolume, PersistentVolumeClaim and StorageClass Access modes and reclaim policies When to use a StatefulSet instead of a Deployment How to diagnose a Pod stuck waiting for a volume The three objects Object Role StorageClass Describes a kind of storage (fast SSD, cheap HDD) and how to provision it PersistentVolumeClaim (PVC) An application&#39;s request: \"I need 10Gi of this class\" PersistentVolume (PV) The actual disk. Usually created automatically when a claim appears With dynamic provisioning you only write the claim; the StorageClass creates the disk and the PV for you. apiVersion: v1 kind: PersistentVolumeClaim metadata: name: shop-uploads namespace: shop spec: accessModes: [ ReadWriteOnce ] storageClassName: standard resources: requests: storage: 10Gi # In the Pod template containers: - name: api volumeMounts: - name: uploads mountPath: /var/lib/shop/uploads volumes: - name: uploads persistentVolumeClaim: claimName: shop-uploads Access modes and reclaim policy Access mode Meaning ReadWriteOnce (RWO) Mounted read-write by one node at a time ReadOnlyMany (ROX) Mounted read-only by many nodes ReadWriteMany (RWX) Mounted read-write by many nodes; needs a storage system that supports it, such as NFS or a managed file service ReadWriteOncePod Mounted read-write by a single Pod The reclaim policy decides what happens to the disk when the claim is deleted. Delete removes the disk and its data; Retain keeps it for manual recovery. Production databases should use a class with Retain , or you should take snapshots, because deleting a claim is one command. StatefulSets A Deployment treats Pods as interchangeable. A StatefulSet gives each Pod a stable identity ( db-0 , db-1 , db-2 ), starts and stops them in order, and creates a separate PVC for each through volumeClaimTemplates . Use it for databases and clustered systems where each member has its own data and name. A headless Service ( clusterIP: None ) gives each Pod its own DNS entry such as db-0.db.shop.svc.cluster.local . $ kubectl get pvc -n shop NAME STATUS VOLUME CAPACITY ACCESS MODES STORAGECLASS data-db-0 Bound pvc-2f1c8d0e-... 20Gi RWO standard data-db-1 Bound pvc-7a93b1c4-... 20Gi RWO standard $ kubectl get sc If you only need a single database for a small service, a managed database service from your cloud provider is usually safer than operating one yourself on Kubernetes. Incident: the Pod is stuck Pending with an unbound claim Incident A new Pod stays Pending for ten minutes after you added a volume. $ kubectl get pvc shop-uploads -n shop NAME STATUS VOLUME CAPACITY ACCESS MODES STORAGECLASS shop-uploads Pending fast-ssd $ kubectl describe pvc shop-uploads -n shop | tail -4 Events: Warning ProvisioningFailed persistentvolume-controller storageclass.storage.k8s.io \"fast-ssd\" not found $ kubectl get sc NAME PROVISIONER RECLAIMPOLICY standard (default) ebs.csi.aws.com Delete The claim names a StorageClass that does not exist, so nothing provisions a disk and the Pod cannot be scheduled. Correct storageClassName to a real class (here standard ), delete the pending claim and apply it again, since most claim fields cannot be changed after creation. Other frequent causes: the requested access mode is not supported by the class, the zone has no capacity, or the volume exists in a different availability zone than the node the Pod landed on. Always read the events at the bottom of kubectl describe . Common mistakes Assuming a PVC is deleted with its Pod (it is not) or that data survives claim deletion (it may not) Using ReadWriteOnce volumes for a multi-replica Deployment on different nodes Running databases on emptyDir , which dies with the Pod Skipping backups because \"the disk is pe"},"kubernetes/jobs-cronjobs-daemonsets":{"html":"\u003cp>Not every workload is a web server that runs forever. Database migrations run once, reports run nightly, and log collectors must run on every node. Kubernetes has a controller for each pattern, and picking the wrong one produces duplicated work, missed runs or Pods that never finish.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Jobs for run-to-completion work, with retries and parallelism\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>CronJobs for scheduled work, and their concurrency and history settings\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>DaemonSets for one Pod per node\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>How to pick between Deployment, StatefulSet, Job and DaemonSet\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"jobs\">Jobs\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A \u003cstrong>Job\u003c/strong> creates Pods and keeps retrying until a set number complete successfully. The Pod&#39;s restart policy must be \u003ccode>Never\u003c/code> or \u003ccode>OnFailure\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"yaml\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"apiVersion: batch/v1\nkind: Job\nmetadata:\n  name: db-migrate\n  namespace: shop\nspec:\n  backoffLimit: 3\n  activeDeadlineSeconds: 600\n  ttlSecondsAfterFinished: 3600\n  template:\n    spec:\n      restartPolicy: Never\n      containers:\n        - name: migrate\n          image: registry.example.com/shop/api:1.8.2\n          command: [&quot;python&quot;, &quot;manage.py&quot;, &quot;migrate&quot;]\n          envFrom:\n            - secretRef:\n                name: shop-db\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">apiVersion:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">batch/v1\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">kind:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">Job\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">metadata:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">db-migrate\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">namespace:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">shop\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">spec:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">backoffLimit:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">3\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">activeDeadlineSeconds:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">600\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">ttlSecondsAfterFinished:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">3600\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">template:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">spec:\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">restartPolicy:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">Never\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">containers:\u003c/span>\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">migrate\u003c/span>\n          \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">image:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">registry.example.com/shop/api:1.8.2\u003c/span>\n          \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">command:\u003c/span> [\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;python&quot;\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;manage.py&quot;\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;migrate&quot;\u003c/span>]\n          \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">envFrom:\u003c/span>\n            \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">secretRef:\u003c/span>\n                \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">shop-db\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>backoffLimit\u003c/code> caps retries before the Job is marked failed. \u003ccode>activeDeadlineSeconds\u003c/code> kills a Job that runs too long. \u003ccode>ttlSecondsAfterFinished\u003c/code> deletes the finished Job and its Pods automatically, so completed objects do not accumulate. For parallel work, set \u003ccode>completions\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>parallelism\u003c/code>, for example 10 completions with 3 running at a time.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"kubectl apply -f job.yaml\nkubectl get jobs -n shop\nkubectl logs job/db-migrate -n shop\nkubectl describe job db-migrate -n shop | tail -8\nkubectl wait --for=condition=complete job/db-migrate -n shop --timeout=300s\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl apply -f job.yaml\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl get \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">jobs\u003c/span> -n shop\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl logs job/db-migrate -n shop\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl describe job db-migrate -n shop | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">tail\u003c/span> -8\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">wait\u003c/span> --\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">for\u003c/span>=condition=complete job/db-migrate -n shop --\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">timeout\u003c/span>=300s\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"cronjobs\">CronJobs\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A \u003cstrong>CronJob\u003c/strong> creates a Job on a schedule, using the same five-field syntax as cron.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"yaml\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"apiVersion: batch/v1\nkind: CronJob\nmetadata:\n  name: nightly-report\n  namespace: shop\nspec:\n  schedule: &quot;30 2 * * *&quot;\n  timeZone: &quot;Etc/UTC&quot;\n  concurrencyPolicy: Forbid\n  startingDeadlineSeconds: 600\n  successfulJobsHistoryLimit: 3\n  failedJobsHistoryLimit: 5\n  jobTemplate:\n    spec:\n      backoffLimit: 2\n      template:\n        spec:\n          restartPolicy: OnFailure\n          containers:\n            - name: report\n              image: registry.example.com/shop/report:2.4.0\n              args: [&quot;--date&quot;, &quot;yesterday&quot;]\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">apiVersion:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">batch/v1\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">kind:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">CronJob\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">metadata:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">nightly-report\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">namespace:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">shop\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">spec:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">schedule:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;30 2 * * *&quot;\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">timeZone:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Etc/UTC&quot;\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">concurrencyPolicy:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">Forbid\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">startingDeadlineSeconds:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">600\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">successfulJobsHistoryLimit:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">3\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">failedJobsHistoryLimit:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">5\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">jobTemplate:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">spec:\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">backoffLimit:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">2\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">template:\u003c/span>\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">spec:\u003c/span>\n          \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">restartPolicy:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">OnFailure\u003c/span>\n          \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">containers:\u003c/span>\n            \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">report\u003c/span>\n              \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">image:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">registry.example.com/shop/report:2.4.0\u003c/span>\n              \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">args:\u003c/span> [\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;--date&quot;\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;yesterday&quot;\u003c/span>]\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>concurrencyPolicy\u003c/code> decides what happens if the previous run is still going: \u003ccode>Allow\u003c/code> runs them together, \u003ccode>Forbid\u003c/code> skips the new one, and \u003ccode>Replace\u003c/code> kills the old one. Use \u003ccode>Forbid\u003c/code> for most jobs. \u003ccode>startingDeadlineSeconds\u003c/code> lets a missed run start late within the window. History limits keep recent Jobs around so you can read their logs.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"kubectl get cronjob -n shop\nkubectl create job manual-report --from=cronjob/nightly-report -n shop\nkubectl patch cronjob nightly-report -n shop -p '{&quot;spec&quot;:{&quot;suspend&quot;:true}}'\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl get cronjob -n shop\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl create job manual-report --from=cronjob/nightly-report -n shop\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl patch cronjob nightly-report -n shop -p \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;{&quot;spec&quot;:{&quot;suspend&quot;:true}}&#x27;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"daemonsets\">DaemonSets\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A \u003cstrong>DaemonSet\u003c/strong> runs one Pod on every node, or on every node that matches a selector. New nodes get the Pod automatically, and removed nodes lose it. Typical uses are log shippers, metric exporters, storage drivers and network plugins.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"yaml\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"apiVersion: apps/v1\nkind: DaemonSet\nmetadata:\n  name: node-exporter\n  namespace: monitoring\nspec:\n  selector:\n    matchLabels:\n      app: node-exporter\n  updateStrategy:\n    type: RollingUpdate\n    rollingUpdate:\n      maxUnavailable: 1\n  template:\n    metadata:\n      labels:\n        app: node-exporter\n    spec:\n      tolerations:\n        - operator: Exists\n      containers:\n        - name: exporter\n          image: prom/node-exporter:v1.8.2\n          ports:\n            - containerPort: 9100\n              hostPort: 9100\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">apiVersion:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">apps/v1\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">kind:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">DaemonSet\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">metadata:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">node-exporter\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">namespace:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">monitoring\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">spec:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">selector:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">matchLabels:\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">app:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">node-exporter\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">updateStrategy:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">type:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">RollingUpdate\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">rollingUpdate:\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">maxUnavailable:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">1\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">template:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">metadata:\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">labels:\u003c/span>\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">app:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">node-exporter\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">spec:\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">tolerations:\u003c/span>\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">operator:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">Exists\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">containers:\u003c/span>\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">exporter\u003c/span>\n          \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">image:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">prom/node-exporter:v1.8.2\u003c/span>\n          \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">ports:\u003c/span>\n            \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">containerPort:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">9100\u003c/span>\n              \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">hostPort:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">9100\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Because control-plane nodes and special nodes carry taints, a DaemonSet needs \u003ccode>tolerations\u003c/code> to run there. Use resource requests so an agent cannot starve the workloads it monitors.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"picking-the-right-controller\">Picking the right controller\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"table-wrap\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\n\u003cth>Need\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Use\u003c/th>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Stateless service, interchangeable replicas\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Deployment\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Stable identity and storage per replica\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>StatefulSet\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Run to completion once\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Job\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Run on a schedule\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>CronJob\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>One copy per node\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>DaemonSet\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-report-that-ran-twice-and-doubled-the-invoices\">Incident: the report that ran twice and doubled the invoices\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A nightly invoicing CronJob takes longer than usual because of a slow database. At the next scheduled time the controller starts a second Job while the first is still running. Both read the same unprocessed invoices and customers are billed twice.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cp>The default \u003ccode>concurrencyPolicy: Allow\u003c/code> permits overlap, and the job was not idempotent. Inspect and fix:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"kubectl get jobs -n billing --sort-by=.metadata.creationTimestamp | tail -3\nkubectl get cronjob invoicing -n billing -o jsonpath='{.spec.concurrencyPolicy}'\nkubectl patch cronjob invoicing -n billing -p '{&quot;spec&quot;:{&quot;concurrencyPolicy&quot;:&quot;Forbid&quot;}}'\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl get \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">jobs\u003c/span> -n billing --sort-by=.metadata.creationTimestamp | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">tail\u003c/span> -3\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl get cronjob invoicing -n billing -o jsonpath=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;{.spec.concurrencyPolicy}&#x27;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">Allow\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl patch cronjob invoicing -n billing -p \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;{&quot;spec&quot;:{&quot;concurrencyPolicy&quot;:&quot;Forbid&quot;}}&#x27;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Set \u003ccode>Forbid\u003c/code>, make the work idempotent by marking each invoice as claimed in a transaction, add an \u003ccode>activeDeadlineSeconds\u003c/code>, and alert if a run takes longer than expected.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Using \u003ccode>restartPolicy: Always\u003c/code> in a Job template, which is invalid\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Leaving finished Jobs forever instead of setting a TTL\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Allowing overlap on jobs that are not idempotent\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Forgetting tolerations so a DaemonSet misses tainted nodes\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Assuming CronJob times are in your local timezone\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"jobs","text":"Jobs"},{"id":"cronjobs","text":"CronJobs"},{"id":"daemonsets","text":"DaemonSets"},{"id":"picking-the-right-controller","text":"Picking the right controller"},{"id":"incident-the-report-that-ran-twice-and-doubled-the-invoices","text":"Incident: the report that ran twice and doubled the invoices"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Kubernetes: Jobs","u":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/workloads/controllers/job/"},{"t":"Kubernetes: CronJob","u":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/workloads/controllers/cron-jobs/"},{"t":"Kubernetes: DaemonSet","u":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/workloads/controllers/daemonset/"}],"text":"Not every workload is a web server that runs forever. Database migrations run once, reports run nightly, and log collectors must run on every node. Kubernetes has a controller for each pattern, and picking the wrong one produces duplicated work, missed runs or Pods that never finish. What you will learn Jobs for run-to-completion work, with retries and parallelism CronJobs for scheduled work, and their concurrency and history settings DaemonSets for one Pod per node How to pick between Deployment, StatefulSet, Job and DaemonSet Jobs A Job creates Pods and keeps retrying until a set number complete successfully. The Pod&#39;s restart policy must be Never or OnFailure . apiVersion: batch/v1 kind: Job metadata: name: db-migrate namespace: shop spec: backoffLimit: 3 activeDeadlineSeconds: 600 ttlSecondsAfterFinished: 3600 template: spec: restartPolicy: Never containers: - name: migrate image: registry.example.com/shop/api:1.8.2 command: [ \"python\" , \"manage.py\" , \"migrate\" ] envFrom: - secretRef: name: shop-db backoffLimit caps retries before the Job is marked failed. activeDeadlineSeconds kills a Job that runs too long. ttlSecondsAfterFinished deletes the finished Job and its Pods automatically, so completed objects do not accumulate. For parallel work, set completions and parallelism , for example 10 completions with 3 running at a time. $ kubectl apply -f job.yaml $ kubectl get jobs -n shop $ kubectl logs job/db-migrate -n shop $ kubectl describe job db-migrate -n shop | tail -8 $ kubectl wait -- for =condition=complete job/db-migrate -n shop -- timeout =300s CronJobs A CronJob creates a Job on a schedule, using the same five-field syntax as cron. apiVersion: batch/v1 kind: CronJob metadata: name: nightly-report namespace: shop spec: schedule: \"30 2 * * *\" timeZone: \"Etc/UTC\" concurrencyPolicy: Forbid startingDeadlineSeconds: 600 successfulJobsHistoryLimit: 3 failedJobsHistoryLimit: 5 jobTemplate: spec: backoffLimit: 2 template: spec: restartPolicy: OnFailure containers: - name: report image: registry.example.com/shop/report:2.4.0 args: [ \"--date\" , \"yesterday\" ] concurrencyPolicy decides what happens if the previous run is still going: Allow runs them together, Forbid skips the new one, and Replace kills the old one. Use Forbid for most jobs. startingDeadlineSeconds lets a missed run start late within the window. History limits keep recent Jobs around so you can read their logs. $ kubectl get cronjob -n shop $ kubectl create job manual-report --from=cronjob/nightly-report -n shop $ kubectl patch cronjob nightly-report -n shop -p &#x27;{\"spec\":{\"suspend\":true}}&#x27; DaemonSets A DaemonSet runs one Pod on every node, or on every node that matches a selector. New nodes get the Pod automatically, and removed nodes lose it. Typical uses are log shippers, metric exporters, storage drivers and network plugins. apiVersion: apps/v1 kind: DaemonSet metadata: name: node-exporter namespace: monitoring spec: selector: matchLabels: app: node-exporter updateStrategy: type: RollingUpdate rollingUpdate: maxUnavailable: 1 template: metadata: labels: app: node-exporter spec: tolerations: - operator: Exists containers: - name: exporter image: prom/node-exporter:v1.8.2 ports: - containerPort: 9100 hostPort: 9100 Because control-plane nodes and special nodes carry taints, a DaemonSet needs tolerations to run there. Use resource requests so an agent cannot starve the workloads it monitors. Picking the right controller Need Use Stateless service, interchangeable replicas Deployment Stable identity and storage per replica StatefulSet Run to completion once Job Run on a schedule CronJob One copy per node DaemonSet Incident: the report that ran twice and doubled the invoices Incident A nightly invoicing CronJob takes longer than usual because of a slow database. At the next scheduled time the controller starts a second Job while the first is still running. Both read the same unprocessed invoices and customers are billed twice. The default concurrencyPo"},"kubernetes/resources-and-quotas":{"html":"\u003cp>The scheduler places Pods using what they \u003cstrong>request\u003c/strong>, and the kernel enforces what they are \u003cstrong>limited\u003c/strong> to. Misunderstanding the difference causes some of the most common Kubernetes incidents: Pods stuck Pending, CPU throttling, and containers killed with &quot;OOMKilled&quot;.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>What requests and limits mean for scheduling and for enforcement\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>CPU and memory behave differently when a limit is hit\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>QoS classes and eviction order\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Namespace controls: LimitRange and ResourceQuota\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"requests-versus-limits\">Requests versus limits\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A \u003cstrong>request\u003c/strong> is the amount the scheduler reserves for the container. A Pod fits on a node only if the sum of requests fits in the node&#39;s allocatable resources. A \u003cstrong>limit\u003c/strong> is the maximum the container may use at runtime.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"yaml\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"apiVersion: apps/v1\nkind: Deployment\nmetadata:\n  name: shop-api\n  namespace: shop\nspec:\n  replicas: 3\n  selector:\n    matchLabels:\n      app: shop-api\n  template:\n    metadata:\n      labels:\n        app: shop-api\n    spec:\n      containers:\n        - name: api\n          image: registry.example.com/shop/api:1.8.2\n          resources:\n            requests:\n              cpu: 250m\n              memory: 256Mi\n            limits:\n              memory: 512Mi\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">apiVersion:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">apps/v1\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">kind:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">Deployment\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">metadata:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">shop-api\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">namespace:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">shop\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">spec:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">replicas:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">3\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">selector:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">matchLabels:\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">app:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">shop-api\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">template:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">metadata:\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">labels:\u003c/span>\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">app:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">shop-api\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">spec:\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">containers:\u003c/span>\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">api\u003c/span>\n          \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">image:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">registry.example.com/shop/api:1.8.2\u003c/span>\n          \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">resources:\u003c/span>\n            \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">requests:\u003c/span>\n              \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">cpu:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">250m\u003c/span>\n              \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">memory:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">256Mi\u003c/span>\n            \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">limits:\u003c/span>\n              \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">memory:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">512Mi\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>CPU is measured in cores: \u003ccode>250m\u003c/code> is a quarter of a core. Memory is in bytes: \u003ccode>256Mi\u003c/code> is mebibytes. CPU is \u003cstrong>compressible\u003c/strong>: if a container tries to use more than its CPU limit, it is throttled and slows down. Memory is \u003cstrong>incompressible\u003c/strong>: if a container exceeds its memory limit the kernel kills it, and the status shows \u003ccode>OOMKilled\u003c/code> with exit code 137.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Many teams set memory requests equal to memory limits, and set CPU requests but omit CPU limits so containers can use idle CPU without throttling. Whatever you choose, measure first and set requests near typical usage.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"seeing-real-usage\">Seeing real usage\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"kubectl top nodes\nkubectl top pods -n shop --containers\nkubectl describe node worker-2 | sed -n '/Allocated resources/,/Events/p'\nkubectl get pod shop-api-7d9c-abcde -n shop -o jsonpath='{.status.containerStatuses[0].lastState.terminated.reason}'\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl top nodes\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl top pods -n shop --containers\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl describe node worker-2 | sed -n \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;/Allocated resources/,/Events/p&#x27;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl get pod shop-api-7d9c-abcde -n shop -o jsonpath=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;{.status.containerStatuses[0].lastState.terminated.reason}&#x27;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">OOMKilled\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>kubectl top\u003c/code> needs the metrics server. Compare usage over days in Prometheus, not a single moment, before changing requests.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"qos-classes\">QoS classes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Kubernetes assigns each Pod a Quality of Service class from its requests and limits, which determines who is evicted first under node memory pressure.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"table-wrap\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\n\u003cth>Class\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Condition\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Evicted\u003c/th>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Guaranteed\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Every container has requests equal to limits for CPU and memory\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Last\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Burstable\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>At least one request or limit set, but not Guaranteed\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Middle\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>BestEffort\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>No requests or limits at all\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>First\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Production workloads should never be BestEffort. Check a Pod&#39;s class with \u003ccode>kubectl get pod NAME -o jsonpath=&#39;{.status.qosClass}&#39;\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"limitrange-and-resourcequota\">LimitRange and ResourceQuota\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Without guard rails, one team can consume a whole shared cluster. Two namespace-level objects set boundaries.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"yaml\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"apiVersion: v1\nkind: LimitRange\nmetadata:\n  name: defaults\n  namespace: shop\nspec:\n  limits:\n    - type: Container\n      default:\n        memory: 512Mi\n      defaultRequest:\n        cpu: 100m\n        memory: 128Mi\n      max:\n        memory: 2Gi\n---\napiVersion: v1\nkind: ResourceQuota\nmetadata:\n  name: shop-quota\n  namespace: shop\nspec:\n  hard:\n    requests.cpu: &quot;8&quot;\n    requests.memory: 16Gi\n    limits.memory: 32Gi\n    pods: &quot;40&quot;\n    persistentvolumeclaims: &quot;10&quot;\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">apiVersion:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">v1\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">kind:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">LimitRange\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">metadata:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">defaults\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">namespace:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">shop\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">spec:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">limits:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">type:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">Container\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">default:\u003c/span>\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">memory:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">512Mi\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">defaultRequest:\u003c/span>\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">cpu:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">100m\u003c/span>\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">memory:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">128Mi\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">max:\u003c/span>\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">memory:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">2Gi\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-meta\">---\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">apiVersion:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">v1\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">kind:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">ResourceQuota\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">metadata:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">shop-quota\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">namespace:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">shop\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">spec:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">hard:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">requests.cpu:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;8&quot;\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">requests.memory:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">16Gi\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">limits.memory:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">32Gi\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">pods:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;40&quot;\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">persistentvolumeclaims:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;10&quot;\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>A \u003cstrong>LimitRange\u003c/strong> supplies defaults for containers that omit them and rejects unreasonable sizes. A \u003cstrong>ResourceQuota\u003c/strong> caps the total a namespace may request. When a quota covers CPU or memory, every Pod must declare them or it is rejected.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"kubectl describe quota shop-quota -n shop\nkubectl describe limitrange defaults -n shop\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl describe quota shop-quota -n shop\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl describe limitrange defaults -n shop\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-pending-pods-and-a-cluster-that-looks-empty\">Incident: Pending Pods and a cluster that looks empty\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>New Pods stay \u003ccode>Pending\u003c/code> with \u003ccode>0/6 nodes are available: 6 Insufficient cpu\u003c/code>. Yet \u003ccode>kubectl top nodes\u003c/code> shows every node at 15 percent CPU use. A finance meeting is waiting on the new reporting service.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cp>The scheduler counts \u003cstrong>requests\u003c/strong>, not usage. Teams had set generous CPU requests years ago, and the nodes are fully reserved while mostly idle.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"kubectl describe node worker-1 | sed -n '/Allocated resources/,/Events/p'\nkubectl top pods -A --sort-by=cpu | head -5\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl describe node worker-1 | sed -n \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;/Allocated resources/,/Events/p&#x27;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">  cpu                7800m (97%)    0 (0%)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl top pods -A --sort-by=cpu | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">head\u003c/span> -5\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Right-size by lowering requests to match measured usage, using the Vertical Pod Autoscaler in recommendation mode as a guide. Meanwhile add nodes if the service is urgent. Prevent drift with quotas and a periodic review of requested versus used.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Setting memory limits far below real peak usage, causing OOM kills\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Setting huge CPU requests &quot;to be safe&quot;, wasting capacity\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Leaving workloads BestEffort\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Believing \u003ccode>kubectl top\u003c/code> usage determines scheduling\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Forgetting that a quota forces every Pod to declare resources\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"requests-versus-limits","text":"Requests versus limits"},{"id":"seeing-real-usage","text":"Seeing real usage"},{"id":"qos-classes","text":"QoS classes"},{"id":"limitrange-and-resourcequota","text":"LimitRange and ResourceQuota"},{"id":"incident-pending-pods-and-a-cluster-that-looks-empty","text":"Incident: Pending Pods and a cluster that looks empty"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Kubernetes: resource management for containers","u":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/configuration/manage-resources-containers/"},{"t":"Kubernetes: resource quotas","u":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/policy/resource-quotas/"},{"t":"Kubernetes: Pod QoS classes","u":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/workloads/pods/pod-qos/"}],"text":"The scheduler places Pods using what they request , and the kernel enforces what they are limited to. Misunderstanding the difference causes some of the most common Kubernetes incidents: Pods stuck Pending, CPU throttling, and containers killed with \"OOMKilled\". What you will learn What requests and limits mean for scheduling and for enforcement CPU and memory behave differently when a limit is hit QoS classes and eviction order Namespace controls: LimitRange and ResourceQuota Requests versus limits A request is the amount the scheduler reserves for the container. A Pod fits on a node only if the sum of requests fits in the node&#39;s allocatable resources. A limit is the maximum the container may use at runtime. apiVersion: apps/v1 kind: Deployment metadata: name: shop-api namespace: shop spec: replicas: 3 selector: matchLabels: app: shop-api template: metadata: labels: app: shop-api spec: containers: - name: api image: registry.example.com/shop/api:1.8.2 resources: requests: cpu: 250m memory: 256Mi limits: memory: 512Mi CPU is measured in cores: 250m is a quarter of a core. Memory is in bytes: 256Mi is mebibytes. CPU is compressible : if a container tries to use more than its CPU limit, it is throttled and slows down. Memory is incompressible : if a container exceeds its memory limit the kernel kills it, and the status shows OOMKilled with exit code 137. Many teams set memory requests equal to memory limits, and set CPU requests but omit CPU limits so containers can use idle CPU without throttling. Whatever you choose, measure first and set requests near typical usage. Seeing real usage $ kubectl top nodes $ kubectl top pods -n shop --containers $ kubectl describe node worker-2 | sed -n &#x27;/Allocated resources/,/Events/p&#x27; $ kubectl get pod shop-api-7d9c-abcde -n shop -o jsonpath= &#x27;{.status.containerStatuses[0].lastState.terminated.reason}&#x27; OOMKilled kubectl top needs the metrics server. Compare usage over days in Prometheus, not a single moment, before changing requests. QoS classes Kubernetes assigns each Pod a Quality of Service class from its requests and limits, which determines who is evicted first under node memory pressure. Class Condition Evicted Guaranteed Every container has requests equal to limits for CPU and memory Last Burstable At least one request or limit set, but not Guaranteed Middle BestEffort No requests or limits at all First Production workloads should never be BestEffort. Check a Pod&#39;s class with kubectl get pod NAME -o jsonpath=&#39;{.status.qosClass}&#39; . LimitRange and ResourceQuota Without guard rails, one team can consume a whole shared cluster. Two namespace-level objects set boundaries. apiVersion: v1 kind: LimitRange metadata: name: defaults namespace: shop spec: limits: - type: Container default: memory: 512Mi defaultRequest: cpu: 100m memory: 128Mi max: memory: 2Gi --- apiVersion: v1 kind: ResourceQuota metadata: name: shop-quota namespace: shop spec: hard: requests.cpu: \"8\" requests.memory: 16Gi limits.memory: 32Gi pods: \"40\" persistentvolumeclaims: \"10\" A LimitRange supplies defaults for containers that omit them and rejects unreasonable sizes. A ResourceQuota caps the total a namespace may request. When a quota covers CPU or memory, every Pod must declare them or it is rejected. $ kubectl describe quota shop-quota -n shop $ kubectl describe limitrange defaults -n shop Incident: Pending Pods and a cluster that looks empty Incident New Pods stay Pending with 0/6 nodes are available: 6 Insufficient cpu . Yet kubectl top nodes shows every node at 15 percent CPU use. A finance meeting is waiting on the new reporting service. The scheduler counts requests , not usage. Teams had set generous CPU requests years ago, and the nodes are fully reserved while mostly idle. $ kubectl describe node worker-1 | sed -n &#x27;/Allocated resources/,/Events/p&#x27; cpu 7800m (97%) 0 (0%) $ kubectl top pods -A --sort-by=cpu | head -5 Right-size by lowering requests to match measured usag"},"kubernetes/autoscaling":{"html":"\u003cp>Traffic is never flat. Autoscaling adds capacity when demand rises and removes it when demand falls, so you neither fail under load nor pay for idle machines. Kubernetes has several autoscalers that work at different levels, and they must be configured to cooperate.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>The Horizontal Pod Autoscaler and its formula\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>The Vertical Pod Autoscaler and when to use it\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Event-driven scaling and scale-to-zero with KEDA\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>The cluster autoscaler or Karpenter for nodes\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"horizontal-pod-autoscaler\">Horizontal Pod Autoscaler\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>The \u003cstrong>HPA\u003c/strong> changes the replica count of a Deployment based on observed metrics. The core rule is:\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>desiredReplicas = ceil(currentReplicas x currentMetric / targetMetric)\u003c/code>\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>If three Pods run at 90 percent CPU against a 60 percent target, the HPA wants ceil(3 x 90 / 60) = 5 replicas.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"yaml\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"apiVersion: autoscaling/v2\nkind: HorizontalPodAutoscaler\nmetadata:\n  name: shop-api\n  namespace: shop\nspec:\n  scaleTargetRef:\n    apiVersion: apps/v1\n    kind: Deployment\n    name: shop-api\n  minReplicas: 3\n  maxReplicas: 20\n  metrics:\n    - type: Resource\n      resource:\n        name: cpu\n        target:\n          type: Utilization\n          averageUtilization: 60\n  behavior:\n    scaleDown:\n      stabilizationWindowSeconds: 300\n      policies:\n        - type: Percent\n          value: 25\n          periodSeconds: 60\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">apiVersion:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">autoscaling/v2\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">kind:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">HorizontalPodAutoscaler\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">metadata:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">shop-api\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">namespace:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">shop\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">spec:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">scaleTargetRef:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">apiVersion:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">apps/v1\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">kind:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">Deployment\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">shop-api\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">minReplicas:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">3\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">maxReplicas:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">20\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">metrics:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">type:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">Resource\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">resource:\u003c/span>\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">cpu\u003c/span>\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">target:\u003c/span>\n          \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">type:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">Utilization\u003c/span>\n          \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">averageUtilization:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">60\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">behavior:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">scaleDown:\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">stabilizationWindowSeconds:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">300\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">policies:\u003c/span>\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">type:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">Percent\u003c/span>\n          \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">value:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">25\u003c/span>\n          \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">periodSeconds:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">60\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>CPU utilisation is measured \u003cstrong>relative to the Pod&#39;s CPU request\u003c/strong>, so the Deployment must set requests or the HPA reports \u003ccode>&lt;unknown&gt;\u003c/code>. The \u003ccode>behavior\u003c/code> block slows scale-down to avoid flapping: here the HPA waits five minutes and removes at most a quarter of the Pods per minute.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"kubectl get hpa -n shop\nkubectl describe hpa shop-api -n shop | sed -n '/Metrics/,/Events/p'\nkubectl top pods -n shop\nkubectl get events -n shop --field-selector involvedObject.kind=HorizontalPodAutoscaler\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl get hpa -n shop\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl describe hpa shop-api -n shop | sed -n \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;/Metrics/,/Events/p&#x27;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl top pods -n shop\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl get events -n shop --field-selector involvedObject.kind=HorizontalPodAutoscaler\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Requires the metrics server for CPU and memory. For request rate or queue depth, use a custom metrics adapter such as the Prometheus adapter. CPU is a poor signal for many applications; requests per second or latency often predicts load better.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"vertical-pod-autoscaler\">Vertical Pod Autoscaler\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>The \u003cstrong>VPA\u003c/strong> adjusts container requests and limits. In \u003ccode>Off\u003c/code> mode it only recommends values, which is a safe way to right-size a workload.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"kubectl get vpa shop-api -n shop -o jsonpath='{.status.recommendation.containerRecommendations[0]}'\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl get vpa shop-api -n shop -o jsonpath=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;{.status.recommendation.containerRecommendations[0]}&#x27;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Do not let HPA and VPA both act on CPU for the same workload, since they fight. A common pattern is VPA in recommendation mode for sizing and HPA for scaling out.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"event-driven-scaling-with-keda\">Event-driven scaling with KEDA\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>\u003cstrong>KEDA\u003c/strong> scales workloads from external event sources such as queue length, Kafka lag or a cron schedule, and can scale to \u003cstrong>zero\u003c/strong>.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"yaml\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"apiVersion: keda.sh/v1alpha1\nkind: ScaledObject\nmetadata:\n  name: invoice-worker\n  namespace: billing\nspec:\n  scaleTargetRef:\n    name: invoice-worker\n  minReplicaCount: 0\n  maxReplicaCount: 30\n  triggers:\n    - type: aws-sqs-queue\n      metadata:\n        queueURL: https://sqs.eu-west-1.amazonaws.com/123456789012/invoices\n        queueLength: &quot;20&quot;\n        awsRegion: eu-west-1\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">apiVersion:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">keda.sh/v1alpha1\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">kind:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">ScaledObject\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">metadata:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">invoice-worker\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">namespace:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">billing\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">spec:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">scaleTargetRef:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">invoice-worker\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">minReplicaCount:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">0\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">maxReplicaCount:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">30\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">triggers:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">type:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">aws-sqs-queue\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">metadata:\u003c/span>\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">queueURL:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">https://sqs.eu-west-1.amazonaws.com/123456789012/invoices\u003c/span>\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">queueLength:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;20&quot;\u003c/span>\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">awsRegion:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">eu-west-1\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>One worker per twenty queued messages, and none when the queue is empty. Scale-to-zero suits batch consumers; avoid it for services where cold-start latency would hurt users.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"scaling-nodes\">Scaling nodes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Pod autoscaling only helps if nodes exist to hold the new Pods. The \u003cstrong>cluster autoscaler\u003c/strong> (or \u003cstrong>Karpenter\u003c/strong> on AWS) watches for Pods that are \u003ccode>Pending\u003c/code> because nothing fits and adds nodes, then removes underused nodes.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"kubectl get pods -A --field-selector=status.phase=Pending\nkubectl get nodes -L node.kubernetes.io/instance-type\nkubectl -n kube-system logs deploy/cluster-autoscaler | tail -5\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl get pods -A --field-selector=status.phase=Pending\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl get nodes -L node.kubernetes.io/instance-type\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl -n kube-system logs deploy/cluster-autoscaler | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">tail\u003c/span> -5\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>New nodes take minutes to start, so keep some headroom and use PodDisruptionBudgets so scale-down does not remove too many replicas of one service at once.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-hpa-that-could-not-scale\">Incident: the HPA that could not scale\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A marketing email triples traffic. Latency climbs, but the HPA stays at 3 replicas. \u003ccode>kubectl get hpa\u003c/code> shows \u003ccode>cpu: &lt;unknown&gt;/60%\u003c/code> and the team sees no scaling events.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cp>The HPA had no metric to act on. Find out why:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"kubectl describe hpa shop-api -n shop | tail -8\nkubectl get deploy shop-api -n shop -o jsonpath='{.spec.template.spec.containers[0].resources}'\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl describe hpa shop-api -n shop | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">tail\u003c/span> -8\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">Warning  FailedGetResourceMetric  missing request for cpu\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl get deploy shop-api -n shop -o jsonpath=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;{.spec.template.spec.containers[0].resources}&#x27;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">{}\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The Deployment had no CPU request, so utilisation could not be computed. Set a request, confirm \u003ccode>kubectl get hpa\u003c/code> shows a number, load-test before the next campaign, and alert when an HPA reports unknown metrics or sits at its maximum.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Using the HPA with no CPU request set\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>HPA and VPA both controlling CPU on the same workload\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>A maximum replica count that exceeds node capacity or quota\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Scaling on CPU for an I/O-bound application\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>No scale-down stabilisation, causing flapping\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"horizontal-pod-autoscaler","text":"Horizontal Pod Autoscaler"},{"id":"vertical-pod-autoscaler","text":"Vertical Pod Autoscaler"},{"id":"event-driven-scaling-with-keda","text":"Event-driven scaling with KEDA"},{"id":"scaling-nodes","text":"Scaling nodes"},{"id":"incident-the-hpa-that-could-not-scale","text":"Incident: the HPA that could not scale"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Kubernetes: Horizontal Pod Autoscaling","u":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/tasks/run-application/horizontal-pod-autoscale/"},{"t":"KEDA documentation","u":"https://keda.sh/docs/"},{"t":"Karpenter documentation","u":"https://karpenter.sh/docs/"}],"text":"Traffic is never flat. Autoscaling adds capacity when demand rises and removes it when demand falls, so you neither fail under load nor pay for idle machines. Kubernetes has several autoscalers that work at different levels, and they must be configured to cooperate. What you will learn The Horizontal Pod Autoscaler and its formula The Vertical Pod Autoscaler and when to use it Event-driven scaling and scale-to-zero with KEDA The cluster autoscaler or Karpenter for nodes Horizontal Pod Autoscaler The HPA changes the replica count of a Deployment based on observed metrics. The core rule is: desiredReplicas = ceil(currentReplicas x currentMetric / targetMetric) If three Pods run at 90 percent CPU against a 60 percent target, the HPA wants ceil(3 x 90 / 60) = 5 replicas. apiVersion: autoscaling/v2 kind: HorizontalPodAutoscaler metadata: name: shop-api namespace: shop spec: scaleTargetRef: apiVersion: apps/v1 kind: Deployment name: shop-api minReplicas: 3 maxReplicas: 20 metrics: - type: Resource resource: name: cpu target: type: Utilization averageUtilization: 60 behavior: scaleDown: stabilizationWindowSeconds: 300 policies: - type: Percent value: 25 periodSeconds: 60 CPU utilisation is measured relative to the Pod&#39;s CPU request , so the Deployment must set requests or the HPA reports \u003cunknown> . The behavior block slows scale-down to avoid flapping: here the HPA waits five minutes and removes at most a quarter of the Pods per minute. $ kubectl get hpa -n shop $ kubectl describe hpa shop-api -n shop | sed -n &#x27;/Metrics/,/Events/p&#x27; $ kubectl top pods -n shop $ kubectl get events -n shop --field-selector involvedObject.kind=HorizontalPodAutoscaler Requires the metrics server for CPU and memory. For request rate or queue depth, use a custom metrics adapter such as the Prometheus adapter. CPU is a poor signal for many applications; requests per second or latency often predicts load better. Vertical Pod Autoscaler The VPA adjusts container requests and limits. In Off mode it only recommends values, which is a safe way to right-size a workload. $ kubectl get vpa shop-api -n shop -o jsonpath= &#x27;{.status.recommendation.containerRecommendations[0]}&#x27; Do not let HPA and VPA both act on CPU for the same workload, since they fight. A common pattern is VPA in recommendation mode for sizing and HPA for scaling out. Event-driven scaling with KEDA KEDA scales workloads from external event sources such as queue length, Kafka lag or a cron schedule, and can scale to zero . apiVersion: keda.sh/v1alpha1 kind: ScaledObject metadata: name: invoice-worker namespace: billing spec: scaleTargetRef: name: invoice-worker minReplicaCount: 0 maxReplicaCount: 30 triggers: - type: aws-sqs-queue metadata: queueURL: https://sqs.eu-west-1.amazonaws.com/123456789012/invoices queueLength: \"20\" awsRegion: eu-west-1 One worker per twenty queued messages, and none when the queue is empty. Scale-to-zero suits batch consumers; avoid it for services where cold-start latency would hurt users. Scaling nodes Pod autoscaling only helps if nodes exist to hold the new Pods. The cluster autoscaler (or Karpenter on AWS) watches for Pods that are Pending because nothing fits and adds nodes, then removes underused nodes. $ kubectl get pods -A --field-selector=status.phase=Pending $ kubectl get nodes -L node.kubernetes.io/instance-type $ kubectl -n kube-system logs deploy/cluster-autoscaler | tail -5 New nodes take minutes to start, so keep some headroom and use PodDisruptionBudgets so scale-down does not remove too many replicas of one service at once. Incident: the HPA that could not scale Incident A marketing email triples traffic. Latency climbs, but the HPA stays at 3 replicas. kubectl get hpa shows cpu: \u003cunknown>/60% and the team sees no scaling events. The HPA had no metric to act on. Find out why: $ kubectl describe hpa shop-api -n shop | tail -8 Warning FailedGetResourceMetric missing request for cpu $ kubectl get deploy shop-api -n shop -o jsonpath= &#x"},"kubernetes/rbac-and-security":{"html":"\u003cp>By default, an administrator can do anything in a cluster and so can any careless script that borrows their credentials. Role-based access control (RBAC) limits what each person and workload may do, and Pod security settings limit what a compromised container can do. These are the two layers that most security reviews and the CKS exam focus on.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Roles, ClusterRoles and bindings\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>ServiceAccounts for workloads\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>How to test permissions with \u003ccode>kubectl auth can-i\u003c/code>\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>A securityContext that follows least privilege\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"rbac-building-blocks\">RBAC building blocks\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"table-wrap\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\n\u003cth>Object\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Scope\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Purpose\u003c/th>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003cstrong>Role\u003c/strong>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>One namespace\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>A set of allowed verbs on resources\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003cstrong>ClusterRole\u003c/strong>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Whole cluster\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Same, for cluster-wide or non-namespaced resources\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003cstrong>RoleBinding\u003c/strong>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>One namespace\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Grants a Role (or ClusterRole) to users, groups or ServiceAccounts there\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003cstrong>ClusterRoleBinding\u003c/strong>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Whole cluster\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Grants a ClusterRole everywhere\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>RBAC is \u003cstrong>additive\u003c/strong>: there are no deny rules. A subject can do something only if some binding allows it.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"yaml\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"apiVersion: v1\nkind: ServiceAccount\nmetadata:\n  name: deployer\n  namespace: shop\n---\napiVersion: rbac.authorization.k8s.io/v1\nkind: Role\nmetadata:\n  name: deployment-manager\n  namespace: shop\nrules:\n  - apiGroups: [&quot;apps&quot;]\n    resources: [&quot;deployments&quot;]\n    verbs: [&quot;get&quot;, &quot;list&quot;, &quot;watch&quot;, &quot;update&quot;, &quot;patch&quot;]\n---\napiVersion: rbac.authorization.k8s.io/v1\nkind: RoleBinding\nmetadata:\n  name: deployer-can-manage-deployments\n  namespace: shop\nsubjects:\n  - kind: ServiceAccount\n    name: deployer\n    namespace: shop\nroleRef:\n  kind: Role\n  name: deployment-manager\n  apiGroup: rbac.authorization.k8s.io\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">apiVersion:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">v1\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">kind:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">ServiceAccount\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">metadata:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">deployer\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">namespace:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">shop\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-meta\">---\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">apiVersion:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">rbac.authorization.k8s.io/v1\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">kind:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">Role\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">metadata:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">deployment-manager\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">namespace:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">shop\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">rules:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">apiGroups:\u003c/span> [\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;apps&quot;\u003c/span>]\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">resources:\u003c/span> [\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;deployments&quot;\u003c/span>]\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">verbs:\u003c/span> [\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;get&quot;\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;list&quot;\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;watch&quot;\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;update&quot;\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;patch&quot;\u003c/span>]\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-meta\">---\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">apiVersion:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">rbac.authorization.k8s.io/v1\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">kind:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">RoleBinding\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">metadata:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">deployer-can-manage-deployments\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">namespace:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">shop\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">subjects:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">kind:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">ServiceAccount\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">deployer\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">namespace:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">shop\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">roleRef:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">kind:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">Role\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">deployment-manager\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">apiGroup:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">rbac.authorization.k8s.io\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Test rather than assume:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"kubectl auth can-i update deployments -n shop --as=system:serviceaccount:shop:deployer\nkubectl auth can-i delete secrets -n shop --as=system:serviceaccount:shop:deployer\nkubectl auth can-i --list -n shop --as=system:serviceaccount:shop:deployer\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl auth can-i update deployments -n shop --as=system:serviceaccount:shop:deployer\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">yes\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl auth can-i delete secrets -n shop --as=system:serviceaccount:shop:deployer\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">no\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl auth can-i --list -n shop --as=system:serviceaccount:shop:deployer\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-warning\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Warning\u003c/div>\u003cp>Avoid granting \u003ccode>cluster-admin\u003c/code>, wildcard verbs (\u003ccode>*\u003c/code>), or the ability to read Secrets or create Pods broadly. The right to create Pods can usually be turned into the right to read any Secret in the namespace.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003ch2 id=\"serviceaccounts-for-pods\">ServiceAccounts for Pods\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Every Pod runs as a ServiceAccount, and its token lets it call the API. Most applications never need to call the API, so turn the token off:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"yaml\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"spec:\n  serviceAccountName: shop-api\n  automountServiceAccountToken: false\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">spec:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">serviceAccountName:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">shop-api\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">automountServiceAccountToken:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-literal\">false\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"securitycontext\">securityContext\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"yaml\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"      securityContext:\n        runAsNonRoot: true\n        runAsUser: 10001\n        seccompProfile:\n          type: RuntimeDefault\n      containers:\n        - name: api\n          securityContext:\n            allowPrivilegeEscalation: false\n            readOnlyRootFilesystem: true\n            capabilities:\n              drop: [&quot;ALL&quot;]\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">securityContext:\u003c/span>\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">runAsNonRoot:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-literal\">true\u003c/span>\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">runAsUser:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">10001\u003c/span>\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">seccompProfile:\u003c/span>\n          \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">type:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">RuntimeDefault\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">containers:\u003c/span>\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">api\u003c/span>\n          \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">securityContext:\u003c/span>\n            \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">allowPrivilegeEscalation:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-literal\">false\u003c/span>\n            \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">readOnlyRootFilesystem:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-literal\">true\u003c/span>\n            \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">capabilities:\u003c/span>\n              \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">drop:\u003c/span> [\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;ALL&quot;\u003c/span>]\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>These mirror the container hardening you met in the Docker module. \u003cstrong>Pod Security Admission\u003c/strong> can enforce them per namespace by labelling it, for example \u003ccode>pod-security.kubernetes.io/enforce=restricted\u003c/code>, so non-compliant Pods are rejected at creation.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-a-ci-job-fails-with-forbidden\">Incident: a CI job fails with &quot;forbidden&quot;\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>The deploy job reports \u003ccode>deployments.apps &quot;shop-api&quot; is forbidden: User &quot;system:serviceaccount:shop:deployer&quot; cannot patch resource &quot;deployments&quot; in API group &quot;apps&quot; in the namespace &quot;shop&quot;\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cp>The message names the user, the verb, the resource and the namespace. Check the effective permissions:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"kubectl auth can-i patch deployments -n shop --as=system:serviceaccount:shop:deployer\nkubectl get rolebinding -n shop -o wide\nkubectl describe role deployment-manager -n shop\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl auth can-i patch deployments -n shop --as=system:serviceaccount:shop:deployer\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">no\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl get rolebinding -n shop -o wide\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl describe role deployment-manager -n shop\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Typical causes are a missing verb in the Role, a binding in a different namespace, a typo in the ServiceAccount name, or a Role bound to the wrong subject. Add only the missing verb (\u003ccode>patch\u003c/code> here), apply, and re-run \u003ccode>auth can-i\u003c/code>. Resist the urge to bind \u003ccode>cluster-admin\u003c/code> &quot;to make it work&quot;. The error message gave you exactly what the account was missing.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Granting cluster-admin to pipelines and humans for convenience\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Leaving the default ServiceAccount token mounted in every Pod\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Running containers as root with a writable filesystem\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Believing a Role denies things; RBAC only allows\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"rbac-building-blocks","text":"RBAC building blocks"},{"id":"serviceaccounts-for-pods","text":"ServiceAccounts for Pods"},{"id":"securitycontext","text":"securityContext"},{"id":"incident-a-ci-job-fails-with-forbidden","text":"Incident: a CI job fails with forbidden"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Kubernetes: RBAC authorization","u":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/reference/access-authn-authz/rbac/"},{"t":"Kubernetes: Pod Security Standards","u":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/security/pod-security-standards/"},{"t":"Certified Kubernetes Security Specialist (CKS)","u":"https://training.linuxfoundation.org/certification/certified-kubernetes-security-specialist/"}],"text":"By default, an administrator can do anything in a cluster and so can any careless script that borrows their credentials. Role-based access control (RBAC) limits what each person and workload may do, and Pod security settings limit what a compromised container can do. These are the two layers that most security reviews and the CKS exam focus on. What you will learn Roles, ClusterRoles and bindings ServiceAccounts for workloads How to test permissions with kubectl auth can-i A securityContext that follows least privilege RBAC building blocks Object Scope Purpose Role One namespace A set of allowed verbs on resources ClusterRole Whole cluster Same, for cluster-wide or non-namespaced resources RoleBinding One namespace Grants a Role (or ClusterRole) to users, groups or ServiceAccounts there ClusterRoleBinding Whole cluster Grants a ClusterRole everywhere RBAC is additive : there are no deny rules. A subject can do something only if some binding allows it. apiVersion: v1 kind: ServiceAccount metadata: name: deployer namespace: shop --- apiVersion: rbac.authorization.k8s.io/v1 kind: Role metadata: name: deployment-manager namespace: shop rules: - apiGroups: [ \"apps\" ] resources: [ \"deployments\" ] verbs: [ \"get\" , \"list\" , \"watch\" , \"update\" , \"patch\" ] --- apiVersion: rbac.authorization.k8s.io/v1 kind: RoleBinding metadata: name: deployer-can-manage-deployments namespace: shop subjects: - kind: ServiceAccount name: deployer namespace: shop roleRef: kind: Role name: deployment-manager apiGroup: rbac.authorization.k8s.io Test rather than assume: $ kubectl auth can-i update deployments -n shop --as=system:serviceaccount:shop:deployer yes $ kubectl auth can-i delete secrets -n shop --as=system:serviceaccount:shop:deployer no $ kubectl auth can-i --list -n shop --as=system:serviceaccount:shop:deployer Warning Avoid granting cluster-admin , wildcard verbs ( * ), or the ability to read Secrets or create Pods broadly. The right to create Pods can usually be turned into the right to read any Secret in the namespace. ServiceAccounts for Pods Every Pod runs as a ServiceAccount, and its token lets it call the API. Most applications never need to call the API, so turn the token off: spec: serviceAccountName: shop-api automountServiceAccountToken: false securityContext securityContext: runAsNonRoot: true runAsUser: 10001 seccompProfile: type: RuntimeDefault containers: - name: api securityContext: allowPrivilegeEscalation: false readOnlyRootFilesystem: true capabilities: drop: [ \"ALL\" ] These mirror the container hardening you met in the Docker module. Pod Security Admission can enforce them per namespace by labelling it, for example pod-security.kubernetes.io/enforce=restricted , so non-compliant Pods are rejected at creation. Incident: a CI job fails with \"forbidden\" Incident The deploy job reports deployments.apps \"shop-api\" is forbidden: User \"system:serviceaccount:shop:deployer\" cannot patch resource \"deployments\" in API group \"apps\" in the namespace \"shop\" . The message names the user, the verb, the resource and the namespace. Check the effective permissions: $ kubectl auth can-i patch deployments -n shop --as=system:serviceaccount:shop:deployer no $ kubectl get rolebinding -n shop -o wide $ kubectl describe role deployment-manager -n shop Typical causes are a missing verb in the Role, a binding in a different namespace, a typo in the ServiceAccount name, or a Role bound to the wrong subject. Add only the missing verb ( patch here), apply, and re-run auth can-i . Resist the urge to bind cluster-admin \"to make it work\". The error message gave you exactly what the account was missing. Common mistakes Granting cluster-admin to pipelines and humans for convenience Leaving the default ServiceAccount token mounted in every Pod Running containers as root with a writable filesystem Believing a Role denies things; RBAC only allows"},"kubernetes/scheduling-and-troubleshooting":{"html":"\u003cp>Two skills separate someone who uses Kubernetes from someone who can run it: placing workloads on purpose, and diagnosing a broken Pod quickly without guessing. This lesson gives you a method for the second, and the tools for the first.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>nodeSelector, affinity, taints and tolerations\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Spreading replicas for availability\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>A fixed diagnostic order for failing Pods\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>What each common Pod status means\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"placement\">Placement\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"table-wrap\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\n\u003cth>Tool\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Purpose\u003c/th>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>nodeSelector\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Run only on nodes with a given label\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003cstrong>Node affinity\u003c/strong>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Same, with richer rules and &quot;preferred&quot; versus &quot;required&quot;\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003cstrong>Taint\u003c/strong> (on a node)\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Repel Pods unless they tolerate it\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003cstrong>Toleration\u003c/strong> (on a Pod)\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Allows scheduling onto a tainted node\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003cstrong>Pod anti-affinity / topology spread\u003c/strong>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Keep replicas on different nodes or zones\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Taints and tolerations are for \u003cem>keeping others away\u003c/em>: dedicate GPU nodes by tainting them, and only GPU workloads tolerate the taint. Affinity is for \u003cem>attracting\u003c/em> Pods.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"kubectl label node worker-3 disktype=ssd\nkubectl taint nodes worker-3 dedicated=gpu:NoSchedule\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl label node worker-3 disktype=ssd\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl taint nodes worker-3 dedicated=gpu:NoSchedule\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Spread replicas so one node or zone failure does not take all of them:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"yaml\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"      topologySpreadConstraints:\n        - maxSkew: 1\n          topologyKey: topology.kubernetes.io/zone\n          whenUnsatisfiable: ScheduleAnyway\n          labelSelector:\n            matchLabels:\n              app: shop-api\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">topologySpreadConstraints:\u003c/span>\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">maxSkew:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">1\u003c/span>\n          \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">topologyKey:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">topology.kubernetes.io/zone\u003c/span>\n          \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">whenUnsatisfiable:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">ScheduleAnyway\u003c/span>\n          \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">labelSelector:\u003c/span>\n            \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">matchLabels:\u003c/span>\n              \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">app:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">shop-api\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Protect availability during voluntary disruptions such as node drains with a \u003cstrong>PodDisruptionBudget\u003c/strong>:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"yaml\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"apiVersion: policy/v1\nkind: PodDisruptionBudget\nmetadata:\n  name: shop-api\n  namespace: shop\nspec:\n  minAvailable: 2\n  selector:\n    matchLabels:\n      app: shop-api\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">apiVersion:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">policy/v1\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">kind:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">PodDisruptionBudget\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">metadata:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">shop-api\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">namespace:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">shop\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">spec:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">minAvailable:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">2\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">selector:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">matchLabels:\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">app:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">shop-api\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"the-diagnostic-order\">The diagnostic order\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Do the same steps every time, from broad to specific:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"kubectl get pods -n shop                      # status and restarts\nkubectl describe pod NAME -n shop             # events at the bottom\nkubectl logs NAME -n shop --previous          # logs of the crashed container\nkubectl get events -n shop --sort-by=.lastTimestamp\nkubectl get endpoints,svc -n shop             # networking\nkubectl top pod -n shop                       # resource use, if metrics are installed\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl get pods -n shop                      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># status and restarts\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl describe pod NAME -n shop             \u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># events at the bottom\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl logs NAME -n shop --previous          \u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># logs of the crashed container\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl get events -n shop --sort-by=.lastTimestamp\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl get endpoints,svc -n shop             \u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># networking\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl top pod -n shop                       \u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># resource use, if metrics are installed\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cdiv class=\"table-wrap\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\n\u003cth>Status\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Meaning\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Usual cause and first step\u003c/th>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>Pending\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Not scheduled yet\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>No node fits (requests too big, taints, unbound PVC). \u003ccode>describe\u003c/code> shows \u003ccode>FailedScheduling\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>ImagePullBackOff\u003c/code> / \u003ccode>ErrImagePull\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Cannot fetch the image\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Wrong name or tag, or missing registry credentials\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>CrashLoopBackOff\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Container keeps exiting\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Application error or bad config. Read \u003ccode>logs --previous\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>OOMKilled\u003c/code> (in last state)\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Exceeded memory limit\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Raise the limit or fix the leak\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>CreateContainerConfigError\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Missing ConfigMap, Secret or key\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Create it or correct the reference\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>Running\u003c/code> but \u003ccode>0/1\u003c/code> ready\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Readiness probe failing\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Check probe path, port, app start-up\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>Terminating\u003c/code> for long\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Finalizer or stuck process\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Investigate the cause before forcing deletion\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>For a node that is unhealthy, \u003ccode>kubectl describe node\u003c/code> shows conditions such as \u003ccode>MemoryPressure\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>DiskPressure\u003c/code>. To empty a node for maintenance without downtime, use \u003ccode>kubectl drain worker-2 --ignore-daemonsets --delete-emptydir-data\u003c/code>, which respects PodDisruptionBudgets, then \u003ccode>kubectl uncordon worker-2\u003c/code> when finished.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-a-pod-will-not-schedule\">Incident: a Pod will not schedule\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>The new \u003ccode>batch\u003c/code> Deployment shows 0 of 3 Pods running. All three are \u003ccode>Pending\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"kubectl describe pod batch-5f9c7b8d6-abcde -n shop | tail -4\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl describe pod batch-5f9c7b8d6-abcde -n shop | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">tail\u003c/span> -4\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">Events:\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">  Warning  FailedScheduling  default-scheduler  0/4 nodes are available: 1 node(s) had untolerated taint {dedicated: gpu}, 3 Insufficient memory.\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Read the message literally: of four nodes, one is tainted and the Pod has no toleration, and the other three do not have enough free memory for the \u003cstrong>request\u003c/strong> (the scheduler places by requests, not by current use). Check what is being asked and what is free:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"kubectl get deploy batch -n shop -o jsonpath='{.spec.template.spec.containers[0].resources}'\nkubectl describe node worker-1 | grep -A6 &quot;Allocated resources&quot;\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl get deploy batch -n shop -o jsonpath=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;{.spec.template.spec.containers[0].resources}&#x27;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl describe node worker-1 | grep -A6 \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Allocated resources&quot;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>If the request of 8Gi is far larger than the workload needs, lower it. If it is genuinely needed, add capacity or let a cluster autoscaler add a node. Do not add a toleration for the GPU taint just to get scheduled, since that defeats the reason the taint exists.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Reading \u003ccode>kubectl logs\u003c/code> without \u003ccode>--previous\u003c/code> after a crash\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Skipping \u003ccode>describe\u003c/code> and its events\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Putting every replica on one node\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Draining nodes with no PodDisruptionBudget on critical services\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Forcing deletion of Pods without understanding why they are stuck\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"placement","text":"Placement"},{"id":"the-diagnostic-order","text":"The diagnostic order"},{"id":"incident-a-pod-will-not-schedule","text":"Incident: a Pod will not schedule"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Kubernetes: scheduling and eviction","u":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/scheduling-eviction/"},{"t":"Kubernetes: debug applications","u":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/tasks/debug/debug-application/"},{"t":"kubectl quick reference","u":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/reference/kubectl/quick-reference/"}],"text":"Two skills separate someone who uses Kubernetes from someone who can run it: placing workloads on purpose, and diagnosing a broken Pod quickly without guessing. This lesson gives you a method for the second, and the tools for the first. What you will learn nodeSelector, affinity, taints and tolerations Spreading replicas for availability A fixed diagnostic order for failing Pods What each common Pod status means Placement Tool Purpose nodeSelector Run only on nodes with a given label Node affinity Same, with richer rules and \"preferred\" versus \"required\" Taint (on a node) Repel Pods unless they tolerate it Toleration (on a Pod) Allows scheduling onto a tainted node Pod anti-affinity / topology spread Keep replicas on different nodes or zones Taints and tolerations are for keeping others away : dedicate GPU nodes by tainting them, and only GPU workloads tolerate the taint. Affinity is for attracting Pods. $ kubectl label node worker-3 disktype=ssd $ kubectl taint nodes worker-3 dedicated=gpu:NoSchedule Spread replicas so one node or zone failure does not take all of them: topologySpreadConstraints: - maxSkew: 1 topologyKey: topology.kubernetes.io/zone whenUnsatisfiable: ScheduleAnyway labelSelector: matchLabels: app: shop-api Protect availability during voluntary disruptions such as node drains with a PodDisruptionBudget : apiVersion: policy/v1 kind: PodDisruptionBudget metadata: name: shop-api namespace: shop spec: minAvailable: 2 selector: matchLabels: app: shop-api The diagnostic order Do the same steps every time, from broad to specific: $ kubectl get pods -n shop # status and restarts $ kubectl describe pod NAME -n shop # events at the bottom $ kubectl logs NAME -n shop --previous # logs of the crashed container $ kubectl get events -n shop --sort-by=.lastTimestamp $ kubectl get endpoints,svc -n shop # networking $ kubectl top pod -n shop # resource use, if metrics are installed Status Meaning Usual cause and first step Pending Not scheduled yet No node fits (requests too big, taints, unbound PVC). describe shows FailedScheduling ImagePullBackOff / ErrImagePull Cannot fetch the image Wrong name or tag, or missing registry credentials CrashLoopBackOff Container keeps exiting Application error or bad config. Read logs --previous OOMKilled (in last state) Exceeded memory limit Raise the limit or fix the leak CreateContainerConfigError Missing ConfigMap, Secret or key Create it or correct the reference Running but 0/1 ready Readiness probe failing Check probe path, port, app start-up Terminating for long Finalizer or stuck process Investigate the cause before forcing deletion For a node that is unhealthy, kubectl describe node shows conditions such as MemoryPressure and DiskPressure . To empty a node for maintenance without downtime, use kubectl drain worker-2 --ignore-daemonsets --delete-emptydir-data , which respects PodDisruptionBudgets, then kubectl uncordon worker-2 when finished. Incident: a Pod will not schedule Incident The new batch Deployment shows 0 of 3 Pods running. All three are Pending . $ kubectl describe pod batch-5f9c7b8d6-abcde -n shop | tail -4 Events: Warning FailedScheduling default-scheduler 0/4 nodes are available: 1 node(s) had untolerated taint {dedicated: gpu}, 3 Insufficient memory. Read the message literally: of four nodes, one is tainted and the Pod has no toleration, and the other three do not have enough free memory for the request (the scheduler places by requests, not by current use). Check what is being asked and what is free: $ kubectl get deploy batch -n shop -o jsonpath= &#x27;{.spec.template.spec.containers[0].resources}&#x27; $ kubectl describe node worker-1 | grep -A6 \"Allocated resources\" If the request of 8Gi is far larger than the workload needs, lower it. If it is genuinely needed, add capacity or let a cluster autoscaler add a node. Do not add a toleration for the GPU taint just to get scheduled, since that defeats the reason the taint exists. Common mistakes Reading kubectl logs w"},"kubernetes/helm-and-kustomize":{"html":"\u003cp>Raw YAML does not scale. The same Deployment needs different replicas, hostnames and secrets in development, staging and production, and copying files invites drift. Two tools solve this in different ways: \u003cstrong>Helm\u003c/strong> templates and packages applications, and \u003cstrong>Kustomize\u003c/strong> patches plain YAML without templating.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Helm concepts: chart, values, release, repository\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Installing, upgrading and rolling back a release\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Reading and writing chart templates\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Kustomize bases and overlays, and when to choose which\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"helm-concepts\">Helm concepts\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A \u003cstrong>chart\u003c/strong> is a package of templates plus default values. A \u003cstrong>release\u003c/strong> is one installed instance of a chart in a cluster, with a name and a revision history. \u003cstrong>Values\u003c/strong> are the settings that fill the templates.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"helm repo add bitnami https://charts.bitnami.com/bitnami\nhelm repo update\nhelm search repo nginx --versions | head -4\nhelm show values bitnami/nginx | head -30\nhelm install web bitnami/nginx -n shop --create-namespace -f values-prod.yaml\nhelm list -n shop\nhelm status web -n shop\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>helm repo add bitnami https://charts.bitnami.com/bitnami\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>helm repo update\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>helm search repo nginx --versions | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">head\u003c/span> -4\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>helm show values bitnami/nginx | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">head\u003c/span> -30\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>helm install web bitnami/nginx -n shop --create-namespace -f values-prod.yaml\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>helm list -n shop\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>helm status web -n shop\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Always keep your overrides in a values file stored in version control, not typed as \u003ccode>--set\u003c/code> flags, so the deployment is reproducible and reviewable.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"yaml\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"replicaCount: 3\nimage:\n  tag: &quot;1.27.1&quot;\nresources:\n  requests:\n    cpu: 100m\n    memory: 128Mi\ningress:\n  enabled: true\n  hostname: shop.example.com\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">replicaCount:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">3\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">image:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">tag:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;1.27.1&quot;\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">resources:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">requests:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">cpu:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">100m\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">memory:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">128Mi\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">ingress:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">enabled:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-literal\">true\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">hostname:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">shop.example.com\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"upgrade-diff-and-rollback\">Upgrade, diff and rollback\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"helm template web bitnami/nginx -f values-prod.yaml | less\nhelm upgrade web bitnami/nginx -n shop -f values-prod.yaml --atomic --timeout 5m\nhelm history web -n shop\nhelm rollback web 2 -n shop\nhelm get values web -n shop\nhelm uninstall web -n shop\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>helm template web bitnami/nginx -f values-prod.yaml | less\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>helm upgrade web bitnami/nginx -n shop -f values-prod.yaml --atomic --\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">timeout\u003c/span> 5m\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>helm \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">history\u003c/span> web -n shop\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>helm rollback web 2 -n shop\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>helm get values web -n shop\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>helm uninstall web -n shop\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>helm template\u003c/code> renders the final YAML without touching the cluster, which is the quickest way to see what a change will do. \u003ccode>--atomic\u003c/code> rolls back automatically if the upgrade fails. The \u003ccode>helm diff\u003c/code> plugin shows the changes against the live release before you apply them. Lint charts you write with \u003ccode>helm lint\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"writing-a-chart\">Writing a chart\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>helm create shop\u003c/code> scaffolds a chart. The key files are \u003ccode>Chart.yaml\u003c/code> (name and version), \u003ccode>values.yaml\u003c/code> (defaults) and \u003ccode>templates/\u003c/code> (Go templates).\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"text\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"apiVersion: apps/v1\nkind: Deployment\nmetadata:\n  name: {{ include &quot;shop.fullname&quot; . }}\nspec:\n  replicas: {{ .Values.replicaCount }}\n  selector:\n    matchLabels:\n      app: {{ include &quot;shop.name&quot; . }}\n  template:\n    metadata:\n      labels:\n        app: {{ include &quot;shop.name&quot; . }}\n    spec:\n      containers:\n        - name: api\n          image: &quot;{{ .Values.image.repository }}:{{ .Values.image.tag }}&quot;\n          {{- with .Values.resources }}\n          resources:\n            {{- toYaml . | nindent 12 }}\n          {{- end }}\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">apiVersion: apps/v1\nkind: Deployment\nmetadata:\n  name: {{ include &quot;shop.fullname&quot; . }}\nspec:\n  replicas: {{ .Values.replicaCount }}\n  selector:\n    matchLabels:\n      app: {{ include &quot;shop.name&quot; . }}\n  template:\n    metadata:\n      labels:\n        app: {{ include &quot;shop.name&quot; . }}\n    spec:\n      containers:\n        - name: api\n          image: &quot;{{ .Values.image.repository }}:{{ .Values.image.tag }}&quot;\n          {{- with .Values.resources }}\n          resources:\n            {{- toYaml . | nindent 12 }}\n          {{- end }}\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>{{ .Values.x }}\u003c/code> reads a value, \u003ccode>include\u003c/code> reuses a helper, and \u003ccode>toYaml | nindent\u003c/code> inserts a block with correct indentation. Indentation mistakes are the most common template bug, so render with \u003ccode>helm template\u003c/code> and validate the result.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"kustomize\">Kustomize\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Kustomize starts from plain, valid YAML (the \u003cstrong>base\u003c/strong>) and applies \u003cstrong>overlays\u003c/strong> that patch it. It is built into \u003ccode>kubectl\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"text\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"app/\n  base/\n    deployment.yaml\n    service.yaml\n    kustomization.yaml\n  overlays/\n    staging/\n      kustomization.yaml\n    production/\n      kustomization.yaml\n      replica-patch.yaml\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">app/\n  base/\n    deployment.yaml\n    service.yaml\n    kustomization.yaml\n  overlays/\n    staging/\n      kustomization.yaml\n    production/\n      kustomization.yaml\n      replica-patch.yaml\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"yaml\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"apiVersion: kustomize.config.k8s.io/v1beta1\nkind: Kustomization\nresources:\n  - ../../base\nnamespace: shop\nimages:\n  - name: registry.example.com/shop/api\n    newTag: 1.8.2\npatches:\n  - path: replica-patch.yaml\ncommonLabels:\n  env: production\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">apiVersion:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">kustomize.config.k8s.io/v1beta1\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">kind:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">Kustomization\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">resources:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">../../base\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">namespace:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">shop\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">images:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">registry.example.com/shop/api\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">newTag:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">1.8\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">.2\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">patches:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">path:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">replica-patch.yaml\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">commonLabels:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">env:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">production\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"kubectl kustomize overlays/production | head -30\nkubectl apply -k overlays/production\nkubectl diff -k overlays/production\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl kustomize overlays/production | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">head\u003c/span> -30\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl apply -k overlays/production\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl diff -k overlays/production\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"choosing-between-them\">Choosing between them\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"table-wrap\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\n\u003cth>Situation\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Choice\u003c/th>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Installing third-party software (databases, ingress controllers)\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Helm, using the vendor chart\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Your own app with a few environment differences\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Kustomize overlays\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Needs conditionals, loops and versioned packages\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Helm\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>You want the YAML to stay readable and template-free\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Kustomize\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>They combine well: render a Helm chart and patch the output with Kustomize, or let Argo CD deploy either. Whatever you pick, deploy through a pipeline, never by hand from a laptop.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-upgrade-that-wiped-the-customisations\">Incident: the upgrade that wiped the customisations\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>An engineer upgrades a release with \u003ccode>helm upgrade web bitnami/nginx --version 18.2.0\u003c/code> and no values file. The site reverts to one replica and the default hostname, because the previous overrides were passed with \u003ccode>--set\u003c/code> and were not retained.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cp>Helm builds the new release from the chart defaults plus whatever you pass this time. Recover and prevent:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"helm history web -n shop\nhelm rollback web 4 -n shop\nhelm get values web -n shop -o yaml &gt; recovered-values.yaml\nhelm upgrade web bitnami/nginx -n shop -f recovered-values.yaml --version 18.2.0 --atomic\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>helm \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">history\u003c/span> web -n shop\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>helm rollback web 4 -n shop\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>helm get values web -n shop -o yaml &gt; recovered-values.yaml\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>helm upgrade web bitnami/nginx -n shop -f recovered-values.yaml --version 18.2.0 --atomic\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Keep all values in files under version control, use the same files in every upgrade, preview with \u003ccode>helm template\u003c/code> or \u003ccode>helm diff\u003c/code>, and let a pipeline run the command so the inputs are always the same.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Using \u003ccode>--set\u003c/code> for production settings that are not stored anywhere\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Upgrading charts without reading the changelog and diff\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Template indentation errors that produce invalid YAML\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Putting secrets in values files in plain text\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Using both Helm and manual \u003ccode>kubectl edit\u003c/code> on the same objects\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"helm-concepts","text":"Helm concepts"},{"id":"upgrade-diff-and-rollback","text":"Upgrade, diff and rollback"},{"id":"writing-a-chart","text":"Writing a chart"},{"id":"kustomize","text":"Kustomize"},{"id":"choosing-between-them","text":"Choosing between them"},{"id":"incident-the-upgrade-that-wiped-the-customisations","text":"Incident: the upgrade that wiped the customisations"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Helm documentation","u":"https://helm.sh/docs/"},{"t":"Helm: chart best practices","u":"https://helm.sh/docs/chart_best_practices/"},{"t":"Kubernetes: declarative management with Kustomize","u":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/tasks/manage-kubernetes-objects/kustomization/"}],"text":"Raw YAML does not scale. The same Deployment needs different replicas, hostnames and secrets in development, staging and production, and copying files invites drift. Two tools solve this in different ways: Helm templates and packages applications, and Kustomize patches plain YAML without templating. What you will learn Helm concepts: chart, values, release, repository Installing, upgrading and rolling back a release Reading and writing chart templates Kustomize bases and overlays, and when to choose which Helm concepts A chart is a package of templates plus default values. A release is one installed instance of a chart in a cluster, with a name and a revision history. Values are the settings that fill the templates. $ helm repo add bitnami https://charts.bitnami.com/bitnami $ helm repo update $ helm search repo nginx --versions | head -4 $ helm show values bitnami/nginx | head -30 $ helm install web bitnami/nginx -n shop --create-namespace -f values-prod.yaml $ helm list -n shop $ helm status web -n shop Always keep your overrides in a values file stored in version control, not typed as --set flags, so the deployment is reproducible and reviewable. replicaCount: 3 image: tag: \"1.27.1\" resources: requests: cpu: 100m memory: 128Mi ingress: enabled: true hostname: shop.example.com Upgrade, diff and rollback $ helm template web bitnami/nginx -f values-prod.yaml | less $ helm upgrade web bitnami/nginx -n shop -f values-prod.yaml --atomic -- timeout 5m $ helm history web -n shop $ helm rollback web 2 -n shop $ helm get values web -n shop $ helm uninstall web -n shop helm template renders the final YAML without touching the cluster, which is the quickest way to see what a change will do. --atomic rolls back automatically if the upgrade fails. The helm diff plugin shows the changes against the live release before you apply them. Lint charts you write with helm lint . Writing a chart helm create shop scaffolds a chart. The key files are Chart.yaml (name and version), values.yaml (defaults) and templates/ (Go templates). apiVersion: apps/v1 kind: Deployment metadata: name: {{ include \"shop.fullname\" . }} spec: replicas: {{ .Values.replicaCount }} selector: matchLabels: app: {{ include \"shop.name\" . }} template: metadata: labels: app: {{ include \"shop.name\" . }} spec: containers: - name: api image: \"{{ .Values.image.repository }}:{{ .Values.image.tag }}\" {{- with .Values.resources }} resources: {{- toYaml . | nindent 12 }} {{- end }} {{ .Values.x }} reads a value, include reuses a helper, and toYaml | nindent inserts a block with correct indentation. Indentation mistakes are the most common template bug, so render with helm template and validate the result. Kustomize Kustomize starts from plain, valid YAML (the base ) and applies overlays that patch it. It is built into kubectl . app/ base/ deployment.yaml service.yaml kustomization.yaml overlays/ staging/ kustomization.yaml production/ kustomization.yaml replica-patch.yaml apiVersion: kustomize.config.k8s.io/v1beta1 kind: Kustomization resources: - ../../base namespace: shop images: - name: registry.example.com/shop/api newTag: 1.8 .2 patches: - path: replica-patch.yaml commonLabels: env: production $ kubectl kustomize overlays/production | head -30 $ kubectl apply -k overlays/production $ kubectl diff -k overlays/production Choosing between them Situation Choice Installing third-party software (databases, ingress controllers) Helm, using the vendor chart Your own app with a few environment differences Kustomize overlays Needs conditionals, loops and versioned packages Helm You want the YAML to stay readable and template-free Kustomize They combine well: render a Helm chart and patch the output with Kustomize, or let Argo CD deploy either. Whatever you pick, deploy through a pipeline, never by hand from a laptop. Incident: the upgrade that wiped the customisations Incident An engineer upgrades a release with helm upgrade web bitnami/nginx --version 18.2.0 and no values file. The site re"},"kubernetes/crds-and-operators":{"html":"\u003cp>Kubernetes ships with Deployments, Services and Pods, but its API is extensible. Databases, certificate managers, service meshes and delivery tools all add their own resource kinds. Understanding this model explains how most of the cloud-native ecosystem works.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>What a CustomResourceDefinition (CRD) is\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>The reconcile loop that powers every controller\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>How operators automate running complex software\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Inspecting and troubleshooting custom resources\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"customresourcedefinitions\">CustomResourceDefinitions\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A \u003cstrong>CRD\u003c/strong> registers a new resource type with the API server. After that, you can create, list, watch and delete objects of that type with \u003ccode>kubectl\u003c/code>, and they get RBAC, validation and storage like built-in kinds.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"yaml\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"apiVersion: apiextensions.k8s.io/v1\nkind: CustomResourceDefinition\nmetadata:\n  name: backups.ops.example.com\nspec:\n  group: ops.example.com\n  scope: Namespaced\n  names:\n    plural: backups\n    singular: backup\n    kind: Backup\n  versions:\n    - name: v1\n      served: true\n      storage: true\n      schema:\n        openAPIV3Schema:\n          type: object\n          properties:\n            spec:\n              type: object\n              required: [&quot;schedule&quot;, &quot;target&quot;]\n              properties:\n                schedule:\n                  type: string\n                target:\n                  type: string\n                retentionDays:\n                  type: integer\n                  minimum: 1\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">apiVersion:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">apiextensions.k8s.io/v1\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">kind:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">CustomResourceDefinition\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">metadata:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">backups.ops.example.com\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">spec:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">group:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">ops.example.com\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">scope:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">Namespaced\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">names:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">plural:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">backups\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">singular:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">backup\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">kind:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">Backup\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">versions:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">v1\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">served:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-literal\">true\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">storage:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-literal\">true\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">schema:\u003c/span>\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">openAPIV3Schema:\u003c/span>\n          \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">type:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">object\u003c/span>\n          \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">properties:\u003c/span>\n            \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">spec:\u003c/span>\n              \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">type:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">object\u003c/span>\n              \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">required:\u003c/span> [\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;schedule&quot;\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;target&quot;\u003c/span>]\n              \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">properties:\u003c/span>\n                \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">schedule:\u003c/span>\n                  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">type:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">string\u003c/span>\n                \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">target:\u003c/span>\n                  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">type:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">string\u003c/span>\n                \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">retentionDays:\u003c/span>\n                  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">type:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">integer\u003c/span>\n                  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">minimum:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">1\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The schema gives validation: a Backup without a \u003ccode>schedule\u003c/code> is rejected at creation. A custom resource is only data until something acts on it.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"yaml\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"apiVersion: ops.example.com/v1\nkind: Backup\nmetadata:\n  name: orders-db\n  namespace: shop\nspec:\n  schedule: &quot;0 2 * * *&quot;\n  target: orders-db\n  retentionDays: 14\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">apiVersion:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">ops.example.com/v1\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">kind:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">Backup\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">metadata:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">orders-db\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">namespace:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">shop\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">spec:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">schedule:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;0 2 * * *&quot;\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">target:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">orders-db\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">retentionDays:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">14\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"the-controller-pattern\">The controller pattern\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A \u003cstrong>controller\u003c/strong> is a program that runs a loop: observe the actual state, compare it with the desired state in the object&#39;s \u003ccode>spec\u003c/code>, and act to close the gap. This is \u003cstrong>reconciliation\u003c/strong>, and it is level-based: the controller does not need to know what changed, only what is wanted now.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"text\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"watch Backup objects\nfor each Backup:\n    desired = spec (schedule, target, retention)\n    actual  = existing CronJob, storage, last status\n    if actual != desired: create or patch resources\n    write result to status\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">watch Backup objects\nfor each Backup:\n    desired = spec (schedule, target, retention)\n    actual  = existing CronJob, storage, last status\n    if actual != desired: create or patch resources\n    write result to status\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Built-in controllers work the same way. The Deployment controller sees \u003ccode>replicas: 3\u003c/code>, finds two Pods and creates a third. Because the loop repeats continuously, the system heals itself after failures. Controllers report progress in \u003ccode>status\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>conditions\u003c/code>, which is where you look first when something is wrong.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"operators\">Operators\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>An \u003cstrong>operator\u003c/strong> is a controller plus a CRD that encodes human operational knowledge for a specific application: how to install it, back it up, scale it, upgrade it and recover it. A PostgreSQL operator might turn this into a running, replicated, backed-up cluster:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"yaml\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"apiVersion: postgresql.cnpg.io/v1\nkind: Cluster\nmetadata:\n  name: orders-db\n  namespace: shop\nspec:\n  instances: 3\n  storage:\n    size: 50Gi\n  backup:\n    retentionPolicy: &quot;14d&quot;\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">apiVersion:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">postgresql.cnpg.io/v1\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">kind:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">Cluster\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">metadata:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">orders-db\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">namespace:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">shop\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">spec:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">instances:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">3\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">storage:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">size:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">50Gi\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">backup:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">retentionPolicy:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;14d&quot;\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Operators exist for databases, message queues, certificate management (cert-manager), monitoring (Prometheus Operator) and many other systems. They are the usual way to run stateful software on Kubernetes, because they capture failure handling you would otherwise do by hand.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"working-with-custom-resources\">Working with custom resources\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"kubectl get crd | head\nkubectl get crd backups.ops.example.com -o jsonpath='{.spec.versions[*].name}'\nkubectl api-resources --api-group=ops.example.com\nkubectl explain backup.spec\nkubectl get backups -n shop\nkubectl describe backup orders-db -n shop\nkubectl get backup orders-db -n shop -o jsonpath='{.status.conditions}'\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl get crd | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">head\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl get crd backups.ops.example.com -o jsonpath=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;{.spec.versions[*].name}&#x27;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl api-resources --api-group=ops.example.com\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl explain backup.spec\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl get backups -n shop\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl describe backup orders-db -n shop\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl get backup orders-db -n shop -o jsonpath=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;{.status.conditions}&#x27;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>kubectl explain\u003c/code> documents the schema of any resource, including custom ones. When an object misbehaves, read its \u003ccode>status\u003c/code>, then the events, then the \u003cstrong>controller&#39;s logs\u003c/strong>.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"kubectl get pods -A | grep -i operator\nkubectl logs deploy/backup-operator -n ops-system --tail=50\nkubectl get events -n shop --sort-by=.lastTimestamp | tail\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl get pods -A | grep -i operator\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl logs deploy/backup-operator -n ops-system --\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">tail\u003c/span>=50\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl get events -n shop --sort-by=.lastTimestamp | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">tail\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"running-operators-responsibly\">Running operators responsibly\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Pin operator and CRD versions and test upgrades in staging\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Upgrade CRDs before the operator that uses them\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Deleting a CRD \u003cstrong>deletes every object of that kind\u003c/strong>, and sometimes their managed resources\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Give operators only the RBAC they need; they are powerful controllers\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Back up the data operators manage, not just their YAML\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-crd-that-took-the-database-with-it\">Incident: the CRD that took the database with it\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>During a clean-up an engineer deletes the PostgreSQL operator&#39;s CRDs with \u003ccode>kubectl delete crd\u003c/code> to &quot;reinstall them fresh&quot;. Every database custom resource disappears, and the operator, seeing them removed, begins deleting the managed clusters.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cp>Deleting a CRD cascades to all its objects. Stop the damage and recover:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"kubectl scale deploy/cnpg-controller-manager -n cnpg-system --replicas=0\nkubectl get pvc -n shop\nkubectl get volumesnapshot -n shop\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl scale deploy/cnpg-controller-manager -n cnpg-system --replicas=0\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl get pvc -n shop\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl get volumesnapshot -n shop\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Stop the operator first so it cannot delete more, check whether the PersistentVolumes survived (reclaim policy \u003ccode>Retain\u003c/code> saves them), and restore from backup or snapshots. Afterwards protect against it: use RBAC so only a few people can delete CRDs, set PV reclaim policy to \u003ccode>Retain\u003c/code> for databases, take backups outside the cluster and rehearse the restore.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Deleting CRDs without understanding the cascade\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Treating a custom resource as if it had no controller behind it\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Installing operators from unreviewed manifests with cluster-admin rights\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Skipping CRD upgrades documented in the release notes\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Debugging only the custom resource and never the operator&#39;s logs\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"customresourcedefinitions","text":"CustomResourceDefinitions"},{"id":"the-controller-pattern","text":"The controller pattern"},{"id":"operators","text":"Operators"},{"id":"working-with-custom-resources","text":"Working with custom resources"},{"id":"running-operators-responsibly","text":"Running operators responsibly"},{"id":"incident-the-crd-that-took-the-database-with-it","text":"Incident: the CRD that took the database with it"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Kubernetes: custom resources","u":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/extend-kubernetes/api-extension/custom-resources/"},{"t":"Kubernetes: operator pattern","u":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/extend-kubernetes/operator/"},{"t":"Kubernetes concepts","u":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/"}],"text":"Kubernetes ships with Deployments, Services and Pods, but its API is extensible. Databases, certificate managers, service meshes and delivery tools all add their own resource kinds. Understanding this model explains how most of the cloud-native ecosystem works. What you will learn What a CustomResourceDefinition (CRD) is The reconcile loop that powers every controller How operators automate running complex software Inspecting and troubleshooting custom resources CustomResourceDefinitions A CRD registers a new resource type with the API server. After that, you can create, list, watch and delete objects of that type with kubectl , and they get RBAC, validation and storage like built-in kinds. apiVersion: apiextensions.k8s.io/v1 kind: CustomResourceDefinition metadata: name: backups.ops.example.com spec: group: ops.example.com scope: Namespaced names: plural: backups singular: backup kind: Backup versions: - name: v1 served: true storage: true schema: openAPIV3Schema: type: object properties: spec: type: object required: [ \"schedule\" , \"target\" ] properties: schedule: type: string target: type: string retentionDays: type: integer minimum: 1 The schema gives validation: a Backup without a schedule is rejected at creation. A custom resource is only data until something acts on it. apiVersion: ops.example.com/v1 kind: Backup metadata: name: orders-db namespace: shop spec: schedule: \"0 2 * * *\" target: orders-db retentionDays: 14 The controller pattern A controller is a program that runs a loop: observe the actual state, compare it with the desired state in the object&#39;s spec , and act to close the gap. This is reconciliation , and it is level-based: the controller does not need to know what changed, only what is wanted now. watch Backup objects for each Backup: desired = spec (schedule, target, retention) actual = existing CronJob, storage, last status if actual != desired: create or patch resources write result to status Built-in controllers work the same way. The Deployment controller sees replicas: 3 , finds two Pods and creates a third. Because the loop repeats continuously, the system heals itself after failures. Controllers report progress in status and conditions , which is where you look first when something is wrong. Operators An operator is a controller plus a CRD that encodes human operational knowledge for a specific application: how to install it, back it up, scale it, upgrade it and recover it. A PostgreSQL operator might turn this into a running, replicated, backed-up cluster: apiVersion: postgresql.cnpg.io/v1 kind: Cluster metadata: name: orders-db namespace: shop spec: instances: 3 storage: size: 50Gi backup: retentionPolicy: \"14d\" Operators exist for databases, message queues, certificate management (cert-manager), monitoring (Prometheus Operator) and many other systems. They are the usual way to run stateful software on Kubernetes, because they capture failure handling you would otherwise do by hand. Working with custom resources $ kubectl get crd | head $ kubectl get crd backups.ops.example.com -o jsonpath= &#x27;{.spec.versions[*].name}&#x27; $ kubectl api-resources --api-group=ops.example.com $ kubectl explain backup.spec $ kubectl get backups -n shop $ kubectl describe backup orders-db -n shop $ kubectl get backup orders-db -n shop -o jsonpath= &#x27;{.status.conditions}&#x27; kubectl explain documents the schema of any resource, including custom ones. When an object misbehaves, read its status , then the events, then the controller&#39;s logs . $ kubectl get pods -A | grep -i operator $ kubectl logs deploy/backup-operator -n ops-system -- tail =50 $ kubectl get events -n shop --sort-by=.lastTimestamp | tail Running operators responsibly Pin operator and CRD versions and test upgrades in staging Upgrade CRDs before the operator that uses them Deleting a CRD deletes every object of that kind , and sometimes their managed resources Give operators only the RBAC they need; they are powerful controllers Back up "},"kubernetes/cni-and-cluster-networking":{"html":"\u003cp>Every Pod gets its own IP address and can talk to every other Pod without NAT. That promise sounds simple, but a plugin has to deliver it on real networks. Knowing how makes the difference between guessing and diagnosing when connections fail.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>The Kubernetes network model and its three rules\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>What a CNI plugin does and how Calico, Cilium and Flannel differ\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>How a Service becomes iptables or IPVS rules via kube-proxy\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Default-deny NetworkPolicy and how to test it\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"the-network-model\">The network model\u003c/h2>\n\u003col>\n\u003cli>Every Pod gets a unique, cluster-wide IP address\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Pods can communicate with all other Pods without NAT\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Agents on a node can communicate with all Pods on that node\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ol>\n\u003cp>Service addresses are \u003cstrong>virtual\u003c/strong>: no interface owns a ClusterIP. Traffic to it is rewritten to a healthy backend Pod&#39;s IP.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"kubectl get pods -n shop -o wide\nkubectl get svc shop-api -n shop\nkubectl get endpointslices -n shop -l kubernetes.io/service-name=shop-api\nkubectl cluster-info dump | grep -m1 -E &quot;cluster-cidr|service-cluster-ip-range&quot;\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl get pods -n shop -o wide\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl get svc shop-api -n shop\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl get endpointslices -n shop -l kubernetes.io/service-name=shop-api\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl cluster-info dump | grep -m1 -E \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;cluster-cidr|service-cluster-ip-range&quot;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"the-cni-plugin\">The CNI plugin\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>The \u003cstrong>Container Network Interface (CNI)\u003c/strong> is a standard that lets the kubelet ask a plugin to give a new Pod a network interface and an address. Popular plugins differ in how they carry traffic between nodes:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"table-wrap\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\n\u003cth>Plugin\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Approach\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Notable features\u003c/th>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Flannel\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Simple VXLAN overlay\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Minimal, no NetworkPolicy\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Calico\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Routed (BGP) or overlay\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Mature NetworkPolicy, scalable\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Cilium\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>eBPF dataplane\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Fast, rich policy, observability, can replace kube-proxy\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Cloud CNIs (AWS VPC CNI, Azure CNI)\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Pods get real VPC or VNet addresses\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Native routing, but consume subnet addresses\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>An \u003cstrong>overlay\u003c/strong> wraps Pod traffic in another packet (VXLAN or similar) to cross nodes, which works anywhere but adds overhead. \u003cstrong>Routed\u003c/strong> designs advertise Pod CIDRs so the underlying network delivers them directly. Choose at cluster creation: switching plugins later is disruptive.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"ls /etc/cni/net.d/\nkubectl get pods -n kube-system -o wide | grep -E &quot;calico|cilium|flannel|aws-node&quot;\nkubectl get nodes -o custom-columns=NAME:.metadata.name,PODCIDR:.spec.podCIDR\nip route | grep -E &quot;cali|cilium|vxlan|flannel&quot;\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">ls\u003c/span> /etc/cni/net.d/\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl get pods -n kube-system -o wide | grep -E \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;calico|cilium|flannel|aws-node&quot;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl get nodes -o custom-columns=NAME:.metadata.name,PODCIDR:.spec.podCIDR\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>ip route | grep -E \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;cali|cilium|vxlan|flannel&quot;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>If new Pods hang in \u003ccode>ContainerCreating\u003c/code> with &quot;network plugin not ready&quot; or &quot;failed to set up sandbox&quot;, the CNI plugin on that node is not healthy. Check its DaemonSet Pod.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"how-services-work\">How Services work\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>\u003cstrong>kube-proxy\u003c/strong> runs on every node, watches Services and EndpointSlices, and programs the kernel (iptables or IPVS rules, or nothing at all when Cilium replaces it) to rewrite ClusterIP traffic to a backend.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"sudo iptables -t nat -L KUBE-SERVICES -n | head -8\nsudo ipvsadm -Ln | head\nkubectl get cm kube-proxy -n kube-system -o jsonpath='{.data.config\\.conf}' | grep mode\nkubectl run nettest --rm -it --image=busybox:1.36 --restart=Never -- wget -qO- -T 3 http://shop-api.shop.svc.cluster.local\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sudo\u003c/span> iptables -t nat -L KUBE-SERVICES -n | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">head\u003c/span> -8\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sudo\u003c/span> ipvsadm -Ln | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">head\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl get cm kube-proxy -n kube-system -o jsonpath=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;{.data.config\\.conf}&#x27;\u003c/span> | grep mode\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl run nettest --\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">rm\u003c/span> -it --image=busybox:1.36 --restart=Never -- wget -qO- -T 3 http://shop-api.shop.svc.cluster.local\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>A Service with no endpoints usually means its selector does not match any Pod labels or the Pods are not Ready.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"networkpolicy\">NetworkPolicy\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>By default every Pod can reach every other Pod. A \u003cstrong>NetworkPolicy\u003c/strong> restricts that, but only if the CNI enforces it (Flannel alone does not). Start with default deny, then open what is needed.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"yaml\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1\nkind: NetworkPolicy\nmetadata:\n  name: default-deny\n  namespace: shop\nspec:\n  podSelector: {}\n  policyTypes: [&quot;Ingress&quot;, &quot;Egress&quot;]\n---\napiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1\nkind: NetworkPolicy\nmetadata:\n  name: allow-api\n  namespace: shop\nspec:\n  podSelector:\n    matchLabels:\n      app: shop-api\n  policyTypes: [&quot;Ingress&quot;, &quot;Egress&quot;]\n  ingress:\n    - from:\n        - namespaceSelector:\n            matchLabels:\n              kubernetes.io/metadata.name: ingress-nginx\n      ports:\n        - port: 8080\n  egress:\n    - to:\n        - podSelector:\n            matchLabels:\n              app: orders-db\n      ports:\n        - port: 5432\n    - to:\n        - namespaceSelector: {}\n          podSelector:\n            matchLabels:\n              k8s-app: kube-dns\n      ports:\n        - port: 53\n          protocol: UDP\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">apiVersion:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">networking.k8s.io/v1\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">kind:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">NetworkPolicy\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">metadata:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">default-deny\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">namespace:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">shop\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">spec:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">podSelector:\u003c/span> {}\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">policyTypes:\u003c/span> [\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Ingress&quot;\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Egress&quot;\u003c/span>]\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-meta\">---\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">apiVersion:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">networking.k8s.io/v1\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">kind:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">NetworkPolicy\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">metadata:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">allow-api\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">namespace:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">shop\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">spec:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">podSelector:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">matchLabels:\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">app:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">shop-api\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">policyTypes:\u003c/span> [\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Ingress&quot;\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Egress&quot;\u003c/span>]\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">ingress:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">from:\u003c/span>\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">namespaceSelector:\u003c/span>\n            \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">matchLabels:\u003c/span>\n              \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">kubernetes.io/metadata.name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">ingress-nginx\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">ports:\u003c/span>\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">port:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">8080\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">egress:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">to:\u003c/span>\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">podSelector:\u003c/span>\n            \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">matchLabels:\u003c/span>\n              \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">app:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">orders-db\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">ports:\u003c/span>\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">port:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">5432\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">to:\u003c/span>\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">namespaceSelector:\u003c/span> {}\n          \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">podSelector:\u003c/span>\n            \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">matchLabels:\u003c/span>\n              \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">k8s-app:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">kube-dns\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">ports:\u003c/span>\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">port:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">53\u003c/span>\n          \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">protocol:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">UDP\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Notice the DNS egress rule. After a default-deny on egress, forgetting DNS breaks name resolution for every Pod.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-everything-times-out-after-the-security-hardening\">Incident: everything times out after the security hardening\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A security sprint adds a default-deny NetworkPolicy to the \u003ccode>shop\u003c/code> namespace. Minutes later the API reports \u003ccode>Temporary failure in name resolution\u003c/code> and checkout stops working.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cp>Egress to CoreDNS was blocked along with everything else.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"kubectl exec deploy/shop-api -n shop -- nslookup orders-db\nkubectl get networkpolicy -n shop\nkubectl describe networkpolicy default-deny -n shop | tail -6\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">exec\u003c/span> deploy/shop-api -n shop -- nslookup orders-db\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">;; connection timed out; no servers could be reached\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl get networkpolicy -n shop\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl describe networkpolicy default-deny -n shop | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">tail\u003c/span> -6\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Add an egress rule allowing UDP and TCP port 53 to the DNS Pods, and then rules for each real dependency. Roll out policies in staging first, watch for denied flows with the CNI&#39;s observability tools, and keep a tested rollback.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Writing NetworkPolicies on a CNI that does not enforce them\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Default-deny egress without allowing DNS\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Pod CIDR that overlaps with VPC or on-premises ranges\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Running out of subnet addresses with a VPC-native CNI\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Confusing a Service with no endpoints for a network problem\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"the-network-model","text":"The network model"},{"id":"the-cni-plugin","text":"The CNI plugin"},{"id":"how-services-work","text":"How Services work"},{"id":"networkpolicy","text":"NetworkPolicy"},{"id":"incident-everything-times-out-after-the-security-hardening","text":"Incident: everything times out after the security hardening"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Kubernetes: services, load balancing and networking","u":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/services-networking/"},{"t":"Kubernetes: network policies","u":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/services-networking/network-policies/"},{"t":"Cilium documentation","u":"https://docs.cilium.io/en/stable/"}],"text":"Every Pod gets its own IP address and can talk to every other Pod without NAT. That promise sounds simple, but a plugin has to deliver it on real networks. Knowing how makes the difference between guessing and diagnosing when connections fail. What you will learn The Kubernetes network model and its three rules What a CNI plugin does and how Calico, Cilium and Flannel differ How a Service becomes iptables or IPVS rules via kube-proxy Default-deny NetworkPolicy and how to test it The network model Every Pod gets a unique, cluster-wide IP address Pods can communicate with all other Pods without NAT Agents on a node can communicate with all Pods on that node Service addresses are virtual : no interface owns a ClusterIP. Traffic to it is rewritten to a healthy backend Pod&#39;s IP. $ kubectl get pods -n shop -o wide $ kubectl get svc shop-api -n shop $ kubectl get endpointslices -n shop -l kubernetes.io/service-name=shop-api $ kubectl cluster-info dump | grep -m1 -E \"cluster-cidr|service-cluster-ip-range\" The CNI plugin The Container Network Interface (CNI) is a standard that lets the kubelet ask a plugin to give a new Pod a network interface and an address. Popular plugins differ in how they carry traffic between nodes: Plugin Approach Notable features Flannel Simple VXLAN overlay Minimal, no NetworkPolicy Calico Routed (BGP) or overlay Mature NetworkPolicy, scalable Cilium eBPF dataplane Fast, rich policy, observability, can replace kube-proxy Cloud CNIs (AWS VPC CNI, Azure CNI) Pods get real VPC or VNet addresses Native routing, but consume subnet addresses An overlay wraps Pod traffic in another packet (VXLAN or similar) to cross nodes, which works anywhere but adds overhead. Routed designs advertise Pod CIDRs so the underlying network delivers them directly. Choose at cluster creation: switching plugins later is disruptive. $ ls /etc/cni/net.d/ $ kubectl get pods -n kube-system -o wide | grep -E \"calico|cilium|flannel|aws-node\" $ kubectl get nodes -o custom-columns=NAME:.metadata.name,PODCIDR:.spec.podCIDR $ ip route | grep -E \"cali|cilium|vxlan|flannel\" If new Pods hang in ContainerCreating with \"network plugin not ready\" or \"failed to set up sandbox\", the CNI plugin on that node is not healthy. Check its DaemonSet Pod. How Services work kube-proxy runs on every node, watches Services and EndpointSlices, and programs the kernel (iptables or IPVS rules, or nothing at all when Cilium replaces it) to rewrite ClusterIP traffic to a backend. $ sudo iptables -t nat -L KUBE-SERVICES -n | head -8 $ sudo ipvsadm -Ln | head $ kubectl get cm kube-proxy -n kube-system -o jsonpath= &#x27;{.data.config\\.conf}&#x27; | grep mode $ kubectl run nettest -- rm -it --image=busybox:1.36 --restart=Never -- wget -qO- -T 3 http://shop-api.shop.svc.cluster.local A Service with no endpoints usually means its selector does not match any Pod labels or the Pods are not Ready. NetworkPolicy By default every Pod can reach every other Pod. A NetworkPolicy restricts that, but only if the CNI enforces it (Flannel alone does not). Start with default deny, then open what is needed. apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1 kind: NetworkPolicy metadata: name: default-deny namespace: shop spec: podSelector: {} policyTypes: [ \"Ingress\" , \"Egress\" ] --- apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1 kind: NetworkPolicy metadata: name: allow-api namespace: shop spec: podSelector: matchLabels: app: shop-api policyTypes: [ \"Ingress\" , \"Egress\" ] ingress: - from: - namespaceSelector: matchLabels: kubernetes.io/metadata.name: ingress-nginx ports: - port: 8080 egress: - to: - podSelector: matchLabels: app: orders-db ports: - port: 5432 - to: - namespaceSelector: {} podSelector: matchLabels: k8s-app: kube-dns ports: - port: 53 protocol: UDP Notice the DNS egress rule. After a default-deny on egress, forgetting DNS breaks name resolution for every Pod. Incident: everything times out after the security hardening Incident A security sprint adds a default-deny NetworkPolicy to the shop namespace. M"},"kubernetes/cluster-install-upgrade-etcd":{"html":"\u003cp>Managed services hide this work, but exams, on-premises clusters and many incidents depend on it. If you understand how a cluster is built, upgraded and recovered, you can reason about any Kubernetes environment.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Node prerequisites and a \u003ccode>kubeadm\u003c/code> bootstrap\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>The safe upgrade order for control plane and workers\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Backing up and restoring etcd\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Certificate expiry and node drain and maintenance\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"preparing-nodes\">Preparing nodes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Each node needs a container runtime, the kubelet, kubeadm and kubectl at compatible versions, swap disabled, the right kernel modules and sysctl settings, and unique hostnames and MAC addresses.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"sudo swapoff -a\nsudo sed -i '/ swap / s/^/#/' /etc/fstab\nprintf 'overlay\\nbr_netfilter\\n' | sudo tee /etc/modules-load.d/k8s.conf\nprintf 'net.bridge.bridge-nf-call-iptables = 1\\nnet.ipv4.ip_forward = 1\\n' | sudo tee /etc/sysctl.d/k8s.conf\nsudo sysctl --system\nsudo apt-mark hold kubelet kubeadm kubectl\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sudo\u003c/span> swapoff -a\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sudo\u003c/span> sed -i \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;/ swap / s/^/#/&#x27;\u003c/span> /etc/fstab\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">printf\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;overlay\\nbr_netfilter\\n&#x27;\u003c/span> | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sudo\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">tee\u003c/span> /etc/modules-load.d/k8s.conf\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">printf\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;net.bridge.bridge-nf-call-iptables = 1\\nnet.ipv4.ip_forward = 1\\n&#x27;\u003c/span> | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sudo\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">tee\u003c/span> /etc/sysctl.d/k8s.conf\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sudo\u003c/span> sysctl --system\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sudo\u003c/span> apt-mark hold kubelet kubeadm kubectl\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Holding the Kubernetes packages stops a routine \u003ccode>apt upgrade\u003c/code> from moving the cluster to a new version unannounced.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"bootstrapping-with-kubeadm\">Bootstrapping with kubeadm\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"sudo kubeadm init --pod-network-cidr=10.244.0.0/16 --control-plane-endpoint=k8s-api.example.com\nmkdir -p $HOME/.kube &amp;&amp; sudo cp /etc/kubernetes/admin.conf $HOME/.kube/config\nsudo chown &quot;$(id -u):$(id -g)&quot; $HOME/.kube/config\nkubectl get nodes\nkubectl apply -f cni-manifest.yaml\nsudo kubeadm token create --print-join-command\nsudo kubeadm join k8s-api.example.com:6443 --token abcdef.0123456789abcdef --discovery-token-ca-cert-hash sha256:1234\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sudo\u003c/span> kubeadm init --pod-network-cidr=10.244.0.0/16 --control-plane-endpoint=k8s-api.example.com\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">mkdir\u003c/span> -p \u003cspan class=\"hljs-variable\">$HOME\u003c/span>/.kube &amp;&amp; \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sudo\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">cp\u003c/span> /etc/kubernetes/admin.conf \u003cspan class=\"hljs-variable\">$HOME\u003c/span>/.kube/config\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sudo\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">chown\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;\u003cspan class=\"hljs-subst\">$(id -u)\u003c/span>:\u003cspan class=\"hljs-subst\">$(id -g)\u003c/span>&quot;\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-variable\">$HOME\u003c/span>/.kube/config\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl get nodes\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl apply -f cni-manifest.yaml\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sudo\u003c/span> kubeadm token create --print-join-command\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sudo\u003c/span> kubeadm \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">join\u003c/span> k8s-api.example.com:6443 --token abcdef.0123456789abcdef --discovery-token-ca-cert-hash sha256:1234\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>kubeadm init\u003c/code> generates certificates, writes static Pod manifests for the API server, controller manager, scheduler and etcd into \u003ccode>/etc/kubernetes/manifests/\u003c/code>, and starts the kubelet. Nodes stay \u003ccode>NotReady\u003c/code> until a CNI plugin is installed. Use a stable DNS name or load balancer for the control-plane endpoint so you can add more control-plane nodes later.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"upgrading\">Upgrading\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Kubernetes supports upgrading \u003cstrong>one minor version at a time\u003c/strong> (for example 1.30 to 1.31, never 1.29 to 1.31). Read the release notes and deprecation list first, check API versions your manifests use, and back up etcd before you start.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Order matters: the control plane first, then the workers, with components never newer than the API server.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"sudo apt-mark unhold kubeadm &amp;&amp; sudo apt-get install -y kubeadm=1.31.2-1.1 &amp;&amp; sudo apt-mark hold kubeadm\nsudo kubeadm upgrade plan\nsudo kubeadm upgrade apply v1.31.2\nkubectl drain cp-1 --ignore-daemonsets --delete-emptydir-data\nsudo apt-get install -y kubelet=1.31.2-1.1 kubectl=1.31.2-1.1\nsudo systemctl daemon-reload &amp;&amp; sudo systemctl restart kubelet\nkubectl uncordon cp-1\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sudo\u003c/span> apt-mark unhold kubeadm &amp;&amp; \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sudo\u003c/span> apt-get install -y kubeadm=1.31.2-1.1 &amp;&amp; \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sudo\u003c/span> apt-mark hold kubeadm\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sudo\u003c/span> kubeadm upgrade plan\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sudo\u003c/span> kubeadm upgrade apply v1.31.2\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl drain cp-1 --ignore-daemonsets --delete-emptydir-data\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sudo\u003c/span> apt-get install -y kubelet=1.31.2-1.1 kubectl=1.31.2-1.1\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sudo\u003c/span> systemctl daemon-reload &amp;&amp; \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sudo\u003c/span> systemctl restart kubelet\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl uncordon cp-1\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>On each worker: upgrade kubeadm, run \u003ccode>sudo kubeadm upgrade node\u003c/code>, drain the node, upgrade the kubelet, restart it and uncordon. Do one node at a time, and respect PodDisruptionBudgets so an application keeps enough replicas during the drain.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"backing-up-etcd\">Backing up etcd\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>etcd stores every object in the cluster. Losing it without a backup means losing the cluster&#39;s state.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"sudo ETCDCTL_API=3 etcdctl snapshot save /backup/etcd-$(date +%F).db \\\n    --endpoints=https://127.0.0.1:2379 \\\n    --cacert=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/ca.crt \\\n    --cert=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.crt \\\n    --key=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.key\nsudo ETCDCTL_API=3 etcdutl snapshot status /backup/etcd-2025-06-02.db -w table\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sudo\u003c/span> ETCDCTL_API=3 etcdctl snapshot save /backup/etcd-$(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">date\u003c/span> +%F).db \\\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd cont\">    --endpoints=https://127.0.0.1:2379 \\\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd cont\">    --cacert=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/ca.crt \\\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd cont\">    --cert=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.crt \\\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd cont\">    --key=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.key\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sudo\u003c/span> ETCDCTL_API=3 etcdutl snapshot status /backup/etcd-2025-06-02.db -w table\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Store snapshots off the node, encrypt them (they contain Secrets), and schedule them. A backup you have never restored is a hope, not a backup.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"sudo systemctl stop kubelet\nsudo etcdutl snapshot restore /backup/etcd-2025-06-02.db --data-dir /var/lib/etcd-restore\nsudo sed -i 's#/var/lib/etcd#/var/lib/etcd-restore#' /etc/kubernetes/manifests/etcd.yaml\nsudo systemctl start kubelet\nkubectl get nodes\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sudo\u003c/span> systemctl stop kubelet\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sudo\u003c/span> etcdutl snapshot restore /backup/etcd-2025-06-02.db --data-dir /var/lib/etcd-restore\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sudo\u003c/span> sed -i \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;s#/var/lib/etcd#/var/lib/etcd-restore#&#x27;\u003c/span> /etc/kubernetes/manifests/etcd.yaml\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sudo\u003c/span> systemctl start kubelet\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl get nodes\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The restore writes a new data directory, and you point the etcd static Pod at it.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"certificates-and-maintenance\">Certificates and maintenance\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>kubeadm certificates expire after a year unless renewed, and upgrades renew them. An expired certificate makes the API server unreachable.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"sudo kubeadm certs check-expiration\nsudo kubeadm certs renew all\nkubectl cordon worker-2\nkubectl drain worker-2 --ignore-daemonsets --delete-emptydir-data --grace-period=60\nkubectl uncordon worker-2\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sudo\u003c/span> kubeadm certs check-expiration\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sudo\u003c/span> kubeadm certs renew all\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl cordon worker-2\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl drain worker-2 --ignore-daemonsets --delete-emptydir-data --grace-period=60\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl uncordon worker-2\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>cordon\u003c/code> stops new Pods landing on a node; \u003ccode>drain\u003c/code> also evicts the existing ones safely; \u003ccode>uncordon\u003c/code> returns it to service.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-kubectl-stops-working-one-year-after-install\">Incident: kubectl stops working one year after install\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>On a Monday morning every \u003ccode>kubectl\u003c/code> command fails with \u003ccode>x509: certificate has expired or is not yet valid\u003c/code>. The cluster was built exactly a year ago and nobody upgraded it since.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cp>The control-plane certificates expired. Workloads keep running, but nothing can be managed.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"sudo kubeadm certs check-expiration | head -6\nsudo kubeadm certs renew all\nsudo crictl ps | grep -E &quot;kube-apiserver|etcd&quot;\nsudo cp /etc/kubernetes/admin.conf $HOME/.kube/config\nkubectl get nodes\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sudo\u003c/span> kubeadm certs check-expiration | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">head\u003c/span> -6\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sudo\u003c/span> kubeadm certs renew all\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sudo\u003c/span> crictl ps | grep -E \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;kube-apiserver|etcd&quot;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sudo\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">cp\u003c/span> /etc/kubernetes/admin.conf \u003cspan class=\"hljs-variable\">$HOME\u003c/span>/.kube/config\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl get nodes\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Renew the certificates, restart the static Pods (move their manifests out and back, or restart the kubelet), refresh the admin kubeconfig and verify. Add a calendar alert and a Prometheus rule on certificate expiry, and upgrade the cluster at least yearly.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Skipping minor versions during an upgrade\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Upgrading workers before the control plane\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>No tested etcd restore procedure\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Ignoring certificate expiry dates\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Draining without PodDisruptionBudgets for critical services\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"preparing-nodes","text":"Preparing nodes"},{"id":"bootstrapping-with-kubeadm","text":"Bootstrapping with kubeadm"},{"id":"upgrading","text":"Upgrading"},{"id":"backing-up-etcd","text":"Backing up etcd"},{"id":"certificates-and-maintenance","text":"Certificates and maintenance"},{"id":"incident-kubectl-stops-working-one-year-after-install","text":"Incident: kubectl stops working one year after install"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Kubernetes: installing with kubeadm","u":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/setup/production-environment/tools/kubeadm/"},{"t":"Kubernetes: upgrading kubeadm clusters","u":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/tasks/administer-cluster/kubeadm/kubeadm-upgrade/"},{"t":"Kubernetes: operating etcd (backup and restore)","u":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/tasks/administer-cluster/configure-upgrade-etcd/"}],"text":"Managed services hide this work, but exams, on-premises clusters and many incidents depend on it. If you understand how a cluster is built, upgraded and recovered, you can reason about any Kubernetes environment. What you will learn Node prerequisites and a kubeadm bootstrap The safe upgrade order for control plane and workers Backing up and restoring etcd Certificate expiry and node drain and maintenance Preparing nodes Each node needs a container runtime, the kubelet, kubeadm and kubectl at compatible versions, swap disabled, the right kernel modules and sysctl settings, and unique hostnames and MAC addresses. $ sudo swapoff -a $ sudo sed -i &#x27;/ swap / s/^/#/&#x27; /etc/fstab $ printf &#x27;overlay\\nbr_netfilter\\n&#x27; | sudo tee /etc/modules-load.d/k8s.conf $ printf &#x27;net.bridge.bridge-nf-call-iptables = 1\\nnet.ipv4.ip_forward = 1\\n&#x27; | sudo tee /etc/sysctl.d/k8s.conf $ sudo sysctl --system $ sudo apt-mark hold kubelet kubeadm kubectl Holding the Kubernetes packages stops a routine apt upgrade from moving the cluster to a new version unannounced. Bootstrapping with kubeadm $ sudo kubeadm init --pod-network-cidr=10.244.0.0/16 --control-plane-endpoint=k8s-api.example.com $ mkdir -p $HOME /.kube && sudo cp /etc/kubernetes/admin.conf $HOME /.kube/config $ sudo chown \" $(id -u) : $(id -g) \" $HOME /.kube/config $ kubectl get nodes $ kubectl apply -f cni-manifest.yaml $ sudo kubeadm token create --print-join-command $ sudo kubeadm join k8s-api.example.com:6443 --token abcdef.0123456789abcdef --discovery-token-ca-cert-hash sha256:1234 kubeadm init generates certificates, writes static Pod manifests for the API server, controller manager, scheduler and etcd into /etc/kubernetes/manifests/ , and starts the kubelet. Nodes stay NotReady until a CNI plugin is installed. Use a stable DNS name or load balancer for the control-plane endpoint so you can add more control-plane nodes later. Upgrading Kubernetes supports upgrading one minor version at a time (for example 1.30 to 1.31, never 1.29 to 1.31). Read the release notes and deprecation list first, check API versions your manifests use, and back up etcd before you start. Order matters: the control plane first, then the workers, with components never newer than the API server. $ sudo apt-mark unhold kubeadm && sudo apt-get install -y kubeadm=1.31.2-1.1 && sudo apt-mark hold kubeadm $ sudo kubeadm upgrade plan $ sudo kubeadm upgrade apply v1.31.2 $ kubectl drain cp-1 --ignore-daemonsets --delete-emptydir-data $ sudo apt-get install -y kubelet=1.31.2-1.1 kubectl=1.31.2-1.1 $ sudo systemctl daemon-reload && sudo systemctl restart kubelet $ kubectl uncordon cp-1 On each worker: upgrade kubeadm, run sudo kubeadm upgrade node , drain the node, upgrade the kubelet, restart it and uncordon. Do one node at a time, and respect PodDisruptionBudgets so an application keeps enough replicas during the drain. Backing up etcd etcd stores every object in the cluster. Losing it without a backup means losing the cluster&#39;s state. $ sudo ETCDCTL_API=3 etcdctl snapshot save /backup/etcd-$( date +%F).db \\ --endpoints=https://127.0.0.1:2379 \\ --cacert=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/ca.crt \\ --cert=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.crt \\ --key=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.key $ sudo ETCDCTL_API=3 etcdutl snapshot status /backup/etcd-2025-06-02.db -w table Store snapshots off the node, encrypt them (they contain Secrets), and schedule them. A backup you have never restored is a hope, not a backup. $ sudo systemctl stop kubelet $ sudo etcdutl snapshot restore /backup/etcd-2025-06-02.db --data-dir /var/lib/etcd-restore $ sudo sed -i &#x27;s#/var/lib/etcd#/var/lib/etcd-restore#&#x27; /etc/kubernetes/manifests/etcd.yaml $ sudo systemctl start kubelet $ kubectl get nodes The restore writes a new data directory, and you point the etcd static Pod at it. Certificates and maintenance kubeadm certificates expire after a year unless renewed, and upgrades renew them. An expired certificate makes the API server un"},"kubernetes/cka-cks-exam-strategy":{"html":"\u003cp>The Certified Kubernetes Administrator (CKA) and Certified Kubernetes Security Specialist (CKS) are performance-based exams: you solve tasks at a real terminal against real clusters, under a time limit. Knowing the material is not enough. Speed and accuracy at the command line decide the result.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>What each exam tests\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Terminal setup that saves minutes\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Imperative commands that generate YAML\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>A time-management plan\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-exam-tip\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Exam tip\u003c/div>\u003cp>Exam details change. Domain weights, the Kubernetes version, the allowed documentation and the passing score are published by the Linux Foundation and the CNCF. Check the current candidate handbook before you book, and treat the numbers in this lesson as an orientation only.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-the-exams-cover\">What the exams cover\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"table-wrap\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\n\u003cth>\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>CKA\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>CKS\u003c/th>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Focus\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Administering a cluster\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Securing a cluster and its workloads\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Typical areas\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Cluster setup and upgrades, workloads and scheduling, services and networking, storage, troubleshooting\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Cluster hardening, system hardening, minimizing microservice vulnerabilities, supply chain security, monitoring and runtime security\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Prerequisite\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>None\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>An active CKA\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Format\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Hands-on tasks in a browser terminal\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Hands-on tasks in a browser terminal\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Every lesson in this module maps to CKA areas. The security lesson, plus topics such as audit logs, runtime detection and image scanning, build toward CKS.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"terminal-setup\">Terminal setup\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Spend the first minute on a small set of aliases and completion. Many exam environments already provide \u003ccode>k\u003c/code> and completion, so check before typing.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"alias k=kubectl\nsource &lt;(kubectl completion bash)\ncomplete -o default -F __start_kubectl k\nexport do=&quot;--dry-run=client -o yaml&quot;\nexport now=&quot;--force --grace-period=0&quot;\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">alias\u003c/span> k=kubectl\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">source\u003c/span> &lt;(kubectl completion bash)\ncomplete -o default -F __start_kubectl k\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">export\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">do\u003c/span>=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;--dry-run=client -o yaml&quot;\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">export\u003c/span> now=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;--force --grace-period=0&quot;\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Configure \u003ccode>vim\u003c/code> for YAML so pasted indentation does not break:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"cat &gt; ~/.vimrc &lt;&lt;'EOF'\nset expandtab\nset tabstop=2\nset shiftwidth=2\nEOF\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">cat\u003c/span> &gt; ~/.vimrc &lt;&lt;\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;EOF&#x27;\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">set\u003c/span> expandtab\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">set\u003c/span> tabstop=2\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">set\u003c/span> shiftwidth=2\nEOF\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"generate-yaml-do-not-type-it\">Generate YAML, do not type it\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Imperative commands create manifests for you in seconds. Redirect the output to a file, edit the few fields you need, then apply.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"k run web --image=nginx:1.27 $do &gt; pod.yaml\nk create deployment api --image=nginx:1.27 --replicas=3 $do &gt; deploy.yaml\nk expose deployment api --port=80 --target-port=8080 $do &gt; svc.yaml\nk create configmap cfg --from-literal=LOG_LEVEL=debug $do\nk create role reader --verb=get,list --resource=pods $do\nk create rolebinding rb --role=reader --serviceaccount=default:app $do\nk explain pod.spec.securityContext\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>k run web --image=nginx:1.27 \u003cspan class=\"hljs-variable\">$do\u003c/span> &gt; pod.yaml\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>k create deployment api --image=nginx:1.27 --replicas=3 \u003cspan class=\"hljs-variable\">$do\u003c/span> &gt; deploy.yaml\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>k expose deployment api --port=80 --target-port=8080 \u003cspan class=\"hljs-variable\">$do\u003c/span> &gt; svc.yaml\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>k create configmap cfg --from-literal=LOG_LEVEL=debug \u003cspan class=\"hljs-variable\">$do\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>k create role reader --verb=get,list --resource=pods \u003cspan class=\"hljs-variable\">$do\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>k create rolebinding rb --role=reader --serviceaccount=default:app \u003cspan class=\"hljs-variable\">$do\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>k explain pod.spec.securityContext\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>When you forget a field, \u003ccode>k explain --recursive pod.spec | less\u003c/code> is faster than searching the documentation. Learn the layout of the allowed documentation site and use its search for things such as NetworkPolicy and PersistentVolume examples.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"tasks-to-be-able-to-do-from-memory\">Tasks to be able to do from memory\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Upgrade a cluster with the node-by-node procedure (\u003ccode>kubeadm upgrade\u003c/code>, drain, upgrade kubelet, uncordon)\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Back up and restore etcd with \u003ccode>etcdctl snapshot\u003c/code>\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Create and fix RBAC, NetworkPolicies, PVCs and Ingress\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Troubleshoot a node whose kubelet is stopped: \u003ccode>systemctl status kubelet\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>journalctl -u kubelet\u003c/code>\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Find why a Pod is Pending or crashing using the order from the previous lesson\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>For CKS: AppArmor or seccomp profiles, Pod Security Admission, image policy, audit policy, and tools such as Trivy and Falco\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"time-plan\">Time plan\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Exams commonly contain 15 to 20 tasks in about two hours, each with a weight. A workable approach:\u003c/p>\n\u003col>\n\u003cli>Read the whole task list and note the weights. Do quick, high-weight tasks first.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Switch to the correct context at the start of \u003cstrong>every\u003c/strong> task. The task text tells you the command to run.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>If a task is not progressing after about six to eight minutes, flag it and move on.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Verify each answer: run \u003ccode>get\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>describe\u003c/code>, or a test request before moving on.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Keep the last ten minutes for flagged tasks.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ol>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-right-answer-wrong-cluster\">Incident: right answer, wrong cluster\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>You spend ten minutes solving a task perfectly, then the check fails. The task said to use context \u003ccode>k8s-b\u003c/code>, and you were still on \u003ccode>k8s-a\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cp>Every task begins with a context command, and skipping it is the most common way to lose points on a task you actually solved. Build the habit: copy the context command from the task, run it, confirm with \u003ccode>k config current-context\u003c/code>, and only then start. This is the same lesson as the first one in this module, except here the cost is marks instead of a production outage.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Practise by timing yourself on a local cluster. Create a deliberately broken setup (a wrong image tag, a missing key, a bad selector), and fix it using only the commands in this module. Do that until the diagnostic order is automatic.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Typing YAML from scratch instead of generating it\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Not setting the context for each task\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Spending 20 minutes on one hard task\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Not verifying the result\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Studying only theory and never practising under a clock\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"what-the-exams-cover","text":"What the exams cover"},{"id":"terminal-setup","text":"Terminal setup"},{"id":"generate-yaml-do-not-type-it","text":"Generate YAML, do not type it"},{"id":"tasks-to-be-able-to-do-from-memory","text":"Tasks to be able to do from memory"},{"id":"time-plan","text":"Time plan"},{"id":"incident-right-answer-wrong-cluster","text":"Incident: right answer, wrong cluster"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Certified Kubernetes Administrator (CKA)","u":"https://training.linuxfoundation.org/certification/certified-kubernetes-administrator-cka/"},{"t":"Certified Kubernetes Security Specialist (CKS)","u":"https://training.linuxfoundation.org/certification/certified-kubernetes-security-specialist/"},{"t":"kubectl quick reference","u":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/reference/kubectl/quick-reference/"}],"text":"The Certified Kubernetes Administrator (CKA) and Certified Kubernetes Security Specialist (CKS) are performance-based exams: you solve tasks at a real terminal against real clusters, under a time limit. Knowing the material is not enough. Speed and accuracy at the command line decide the result. What you will learn What each exam tests Terminal setup that saves minutes Imperative commands that generate YAML A time-management plan Exam tip Exam details change. Domain weights, the Kubernetes version, the allowed documentation and the passing score are published by the Linux Foundation and the CNCF. Check the current candidate handbook before you book, and treat the numbers in this lesson as an orientation only. What the exams cover CKA CKS Focus Administering a cluster Securing a cluster and its workloads Typical areas Cluster setup and upgrades, workloads and scheduling, services and networking, storage, troubleshooting Cluster hardening, system hardening, minimizing microservice vulnerabilities, supply chain security, monitoring and runtime security Prerequisite None An active CKA Format Hands-on tasks in a browser terminal Hands-on tasks in a browser terminal Every lesson in this module maps to CKA areas. The security lesson, plus topics such as audit logs, runtime detection and image scanning, build toward CKS. Terminal setup Spend the first minute on a small set of aliases and completion. Many exam environments already provide k and completion, so check before typing. alias k=kubectl source \u003c(kubectl completion bash) complete -o default -F __start_kubectl k export do = \"--dry-run=client -o yaml\" export now= \"--force --grace-period=0\" Configure vim for YAML so pasted indentation does not break: cat > ~/.vimrc \u003c\u003c &#x27;EOF&#x27; set expandtab set tabstop=2 set shiftwidth=2 EOF Generate YAML, do not type it Imperative commands create manifests for you in seconds. Redirect the output to a file, edit the few fields you need, then apply. $ k run web --image=nginx:1.27 $do > pod.yaml $ k create deployment api --image=nginx:1.27 --replicas=3 $do > deploy.yaml $ k expose deployment api --port=80 --target-port=8080 $do > svc.yaml $ k create configmap cfg --from-literal=LOG_LEVEL=debug $do $ k create role reader --verb=get,list --resource=pods $do $ k create rolebinding rb --role=reader --serviceaccount=default:app $do $ k explain pod.spec.securityContext When you forget a field, k explain --recursive pod.spec | less is faster than searching the documentation. Learn the layout of the allowed documentation site and use its search for things such as NetworkPolicy and PersistentVolume examples. Tasks to be able to do from memory Upgrade a cluster with the node-by-node procedure ( kubeadm upgrade , drain, upgrade kubelet, uncordon) Back up and restore etcd with etcdctl snapshot Create and fix RBAC, NetworkPolicies, PVCs and Ingress Troubleshoot a node whose kubelet is stopped: systemctl status kubelet , journalctl -u kubelet Find why a Pod is Pending or crashing using the order from the previous lesson For CKS: AppArmor or seccomp profiles, Pod Security Admission, image policy, audit policy, and tools such as Trivy and Falco Time plan Exams commonly contain 15 to 20 tasks in about two hours, each with a weight. A workable approach: Read the whole task list and note the weights. Do quick, high-weight tasks first. Switch to the correct context at the start of every task. The task text tells you the command to run. If a task is not progressing after about six to eight minutes, flag it and move on. Verify each answer: run get , describe , or a test request before moving on. Keep the last ten minutes for flagged tasks. Incident: right answer, wrong cluster Incident You spend ten minutes solving a task perfectly, then the check fails. The task said to use context k8s-b , and you were still on k8s-a . Every task begins with a context command, and skipping it is the most common way to lose points on a task you actually solved. Build the habit: co"},"kubernetes/probes-metrics-and-logging":{"html":"\u003cp>Kubernetes restarts failed containers and removes unready Pods from Services, but only if you tell it what &quot;failed&quot; and &quot;ready&quot; mean. It can also show a lot about what is going on, if you set up metrics and logs. Probes, metrics and logs are the sensory system of a cluster, and wrong settings here cause some of the most confusing outages.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>The three probes and what each should check\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Probe settings that avoid restart storms\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Resource metrics, the metrics server and what autoscaling uses\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Application metrics and Prometheus scraping\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Logging patterns and what to collect\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"the-three-probes\">The three probes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"table-wrap\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\n\u003cth>Probe\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Question\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>On failure\u003c/th>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Startup\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Has the app finished starting?\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Keeps other probes off; restarts if it never succeeds\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Readiness\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Can it serve traffic now?\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Removes the Pod from Service endpoints, no restart\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Liveness\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Is it stuck for good?\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Restarts the container\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"yaml\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"containers:\n  - name: api\n    image: registry.example.com/api:1.8.2\n    ports: [{ containerPort: 8080 }]\n    startupProbe:\n      httpGet: { path: /healthz, port: 8080 }\n      periodSeconds: 5\n      failureThreshold: 30        # up to 150 seconds to start\n    readinessProbe:\n      httpGet: { path: /ready, port: 8080 }\n      periodSeconds: 5\n      timeoutSeconds: 2\n      failureThreshold: 3\n    livenessProbe:\n      httpGet: { path: /healthz, port: 8080 }\n      periodSeconds: 10\n      timeoutSeconds: 2\n      failureThreshold: 3\n    resources:\n      requests: { cpu: 200m, memory: 256Mi }\n      limits: { memory: 512Mi }\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">containers:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">api\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">image:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">registry.example.com/api:1.8.2\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">ports:\u003c/span> [{ \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">containerPort:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">8080\u003c/span> }]\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">startupProbe:\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">httpGet:\u003c/span> { \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">path:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">/healthz\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">port:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">8080\u003c/span> }\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">periodSeconds:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">5\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">failureThreshold:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">30\u003c/span>        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># up to 150 seconds to start\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">readinessProbe:\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">httpGet:\u003c/span> { \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">path:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">/ready\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">port:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">8080\u003c/span> }\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">periodSeconds:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">5\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">timeoutSeconds:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">2\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">failureThreshold:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">3\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">livenessProbe:\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">httpGet:\u003c/span> { \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">path:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">/healthz\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">port:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">8080\u003c/span> }\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">periodSeconds:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">10\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">timeoutSeconds:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">2\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">failureThreshold:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">3\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">resources:\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">requests:\u003c/span> { \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">cpu:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">200m\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">memory:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">256Mi\u003c/span> }\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">limits:\u003c/span> { \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">memory:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">512Mi\u003c/span> }\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Rules that prevent trouble:\u003c/p>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>A liveness probe should check only the process itself (is the event loop alive?), never a dependency. If the database is down and liveness fails, every Pod restarts at once and the outage gets worse.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Readiness may check dependencies the app cannot work without, but be careful: if all Pods become unready together, the Service has no endpoints.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Use a startup probe for slow starters instead of a long \u003ccode>initialDelaySeconds\u003c/code>.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Set timeouts shorter than the period, and thresholds that tolerate a short delay.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"metrics\">Metrics\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>The \u003cstrong>metrics server\u003c/strong> gives \u003ccode>kubectl top\u003c/code> and feeds the horizontal Pod autoscaler with CPU and memory use. It is not a monitoring system and keeps no history. For history and alerts, run Prometheus (or a compatible service) that scrapes:\u003c/p>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>node and Pod resource use (cAdvisor, kubelet)\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>cluster object state (\u003ccode>kube-state-metrics\u003c/code>: Pod phase, restarts, Deployment replicas)\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>your applications, which expose \u003ccode>/metrics\u003c/code> and are discovered through annotations or \u003ccode>ServiceMonitor\u003c/code> objects\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"kubectl top pods -n shop --sort-by=memory\nkubectl get --raw /apis/metrics.k8s.io/v1beta1/nodes | head -c 200\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl top pods -n shop --sort-by=memory\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">NAME           CPU(cores)   MEMORY(bytes)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">api-7d9f-abc   180m         410Mi\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl get --raw /apis/metrics.k8s.io/v1beta1/nodes | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">head\u003c/span> -c 200\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Useful alerts: Pods restarting repeatedly, containers near their memory limit, Deployments with fewer available replicas than desired, nodes not ready, and persistent volumes nearly full.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"logging\">Logging\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Applications write to standard output, and the node keeps the files under \u003ccode>/var/log/containers\u003c/code>. Those files are rotated and disappear with the Pod, so run a node-level agent (a DaemonSet such as Fluent Bit or Vector) that ships logs to central storage with labels for namespace, Pod and container. Also collect cluster events, because \u003ccode>kubectl get events\u003c/code> forgets after about an hour.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"kubectl logs deploy/api --since=15m --all-containers\nkubectl logs api-7d9f-abc --previous\nkubectl get events -A --sort-by=.lastTimestamp | tail -20\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">kubectl logs deploy/api --since=15m --all-containers\nkubectl logs api-7d9f-abc --previous\nkubectl get events -A --sort-by=.lastTimestamp | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">tail\u003c/span> -20\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Write structured logs with a request or trace id, and avoid logging secrets and personal data.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"predict\">\u003cp class=\"predict-q\">\u003cstrong>Predict first.\u003c/strong> A liveness probe calls /health, which checks the database. The database goes down. What happens?\u003c/p>\u003clabel class=\"predict-l\">Your guess\u003ctextarea rows=\"2\" class=\"predict-guess\" placeholder=\"Type what you expect, then reveal the answer.\">\u003c/textarea>\u003c/label>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-primary\" data-act=\"predict-reveal\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Reveal the answer\u003c/button>\u003cdiv class=\"predict-out\" hidden>\u003cp class=\"predict-ans\">\u003cstrong>Answer:\u003c/strong> All Pods fail liveness and are restarted repeatedly, which adds load and delays recovery once the database returns.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Keep liveness limited to the process. Put dependency checks in readiness, or in alerts.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-restart-storm\">Incident: the restart storm\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A short database failover causes every API Pod to restart, and the service stays down for ten minutes after the database is back.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"kubectl get pods -l app=api\nkubectl describe pod api-7d9f-abc | grep -A2 Liveness\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl get pods -l app=api\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">api-7d9f-abc   0/1   Running   9 (20s ago)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl describe pod api-7d9f-abc | grep -A2 Liveness\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">Liveness probe failed: HTTP probe failed with statuscode: 500\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The liveness endpoint ran a database query. During failover it failed, all Pods were killed, and they then started together and overloaded the database. The team changed liveness to a simple process check, kept the database check in readiness with a lenient threshold, added a startup probe, and alerted on restart rate.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Liveness probes that check dependencies\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>No readiness probe, so traffic goes to Pods still starting\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Treating the metrics server as monitoring\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Only collecting logs from Pods that still exist\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"the-three-probes","text":"The three probes"},{"id":"metrics","text":"Metrics"},{"id":"logging","text":"Logging"},{"id":"incident-the-restart-storm","text":"Incident: the restart storm"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Kubernetes docs: Configure liveness, readiness and startup probes","u":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/tasks/configure-pod-container/configure-liveness-readiness-startup-probes/"},{"t":"Kubernetes docs: Logging architecture","u":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/cluster-administration/logging/"}],"text":"Kubernetes restarts failed containers and removes unready Pods from Services, but only if you tell it what \"failed\" and \"ready\" mean. It can also show a lot about what is going on, if you set up metrics and logs. Probes, metrics and logs are the sensory system of a cluster, and wrong settings here cause some of the most confusing outages. What you will learn The three probes and what each should check Probe settings that avoid restart storms Resource metrics, the metrics server and what autoscaling uses Application metrics and Prometheus scraping Logging patterns and what to collect The three probes Probe Question On failure Startup Has the app finished starting? Keeps other probes off; restarts if it never succeeds Readiness Can it serve traffic now? Removes the Pod from Service endpoints, no restart Liveness Is it stuck for good? Restarts the container containers: - name: api image: registry.example.com/api:1.8.2 ports: [{ containerPort: 8080 }] startupProbe: httpGet: { path: /healthz , port: 8080 } periodSeconds: 5 failureThreshold: 30 # up to 150 seconds to start readinessProbe: httpGet: { path: /ready , port: 8080 } periodSeconds: 5 timeoutSeconds: 2 failureThreshold: 3 livenessProbe: httpGet: { path: /healthz , port: 8080 } periodSeconds: 10 timeoutSeconds: 2 failureThreshold: 3 resources: requests: { cpu: 200m , memory: 256Mi } limits: { memory: 512Mi } Rules that prevent trouble: A liveness probe should check only the process itself (is the event loop alive?), never a dependency. If the database is down and liveness fails, every Pod restarts at once and the outage gets worse. Readiness may check dependencies the app cannot work without, but be careful: if all Pods become unready together, the Service has no endpoints. Use a startup probe for slow starters instead of a long initialDelaySeconds . Set timeouts shorter than the period, and thresholds that tolerate a short delay. Metrics The metrics server gives kubectl top and feeds the horizontal Pod autoscaler with CPU and memory use. It is not a monitoring system and keeps no history. For history and alerts, run Prometheus (or a compatible service) that scrapes: node and Pod resource use (cAdvisor, kubelet) cluster object state ( kube-state-metrics : Pod phase, restarts, Deployment replicas) your applications, which expose /metrics and are discovered through annotations or ServiceMonitor objects $ kubectl top pods -n shop --sort-by=memory NAME CPU(cores) MEMORY(bytes) api-7d9f-abc 180m 410Mi $ kubectl get --raw /apis/metrics.k8s.io/v1beta1/nodes | head -c 200 Useful alerts: Pods restarting repeatedly, containers near their memory limit, Deployments with fewer available replicas than desired, nodes not ready, and persistent volumes nearly full. Logging Applications write to standard output, and the node keeps the files under /var/log/containers . Those files are rotated and disappear with the Pod, so run a node-level agent (a DaemonSet such as Fluent Bit or Vector) that ships logs to central storage with labels for namespace, Pod and container. Also collect cluster events, because kubectl get events forgets after about an hour. kubectl logs deploy/api --since=15m --all-containers kubectl logs api-7d9f-abc --previous kubectl get events -A --sort-by=.lastTimestamp | tail -20 Write structured logs with a request or trace id, and avoid logging secrets and personal data. Predict first. A liveness probe calls /health, which checks the database. The database goes down. What happens? Your guess Answer: All Pods fail liveness and are restarted repeatedly, which adds load and delays recovery once the database returns. Keep liveness limited to the process. Put dependency checks in readiness, or in alerts. Incident: the restart storm Incident A short database failover causes every API Pod to restart, and the service stays down for ten minutes after the database is back. $ kubectl get pods -l app=api api-7d9f-abc 0/1 Running 9 (20s ago) $ kubectl describe pod api-7d9f-abc | grep -A2 "},"kubernetes/service-mesh-and-mtls":{"html":"\u003cp>As services multiply, the same needs appear in all of them: encrypt traffic between services, know who is calling whom, retry failed calls sensibly, and shift traffic gradually between versions. A service mesh moves these concerns out of application code and into the platform. It is powerful and it is costly, so you should know both sides.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>What a mesh adds: identity, encryption, traffic control and telemetry\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Data plane and control plane\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Mutual TLS and authorization policy\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Traffic splitting, retries and timeouts\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>The cost: complexity, resources and debugging\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"parts-of-a-mesh\">Parts of a mesh\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>The \u003cstrong>data plane\u003c/strong> is a set of proxies that handle service-to-service traffic. In the sidecar model each Pod gets a proxy container (often Envoy); newer designs use a per-node proxy or an eBPF-based approach to avoid a sidecar in every Pod. The \u003cstrong>control plane\u003c/strong> (Istio, Linkerd, Cilium service mesh and others) configures the proxies, issues certificates and collects telemetry.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Applications keep making plain calls to \u003ccode>http://payments\u003c/code>; the proxy intercepts and adds behaviour.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"mutual-tls-and-identity\">Mutual TLS and identity\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>With mutual TLS (mTLS) both sides present certificates, so each service knows who is calling and the traffic is encrypted. The mesh gives each workload an identity (for example, based on its Kubernetes service account) and rotates short-lived certificates automatically. You get encryption between services without changing code.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>On top of identity you can write authorization rules: only the \u003ccode>checkout\u003c/code> workload may call \u003ccode>POST /charge\u003c/code> on \u003ccode>payments\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"yaml\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"apiVersion: security.istio.io/v1\nkind: AuthorizationPolicy\nmetadata: { name: payments-allow-checkout, namespace: shop }\nspec:\n  selector: { matchLabels: { app: payments } }\n  action: ALLOW\n  rules:\n    - from: [{ source: { principals: [&quot;cluster.local/ns/shop/sa/checkout&quot;] } }]\n      to:   [{ operation: { methods: [&quot;POST&quot;], paths: [&quot;/charge&quot;] } }]\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">apiVersion:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">security.istio.io/v1\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">kind:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">AuthorizationPolicy\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">metadata:\u003c/span> { \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">payments-allow-checkout\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">namespace:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">shop\u003c/span> }\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">spec:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">selector:\u003c/span> { \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">matchLabels:\u003c/span> { \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">app:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">payments\u003c/span> } }\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">action:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">ALLOW\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">rules:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">from:\u003c/span> [{ \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">source:\u003c/span> { \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">principals:\u003c/span> [\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;cluster.local/ns/shop/sa/checkout&quot;\u003c/span>] } }]\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">to:\u003c/span>   [{ \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">operation:\u003c/span> { \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">methods:\u003c/span> [\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;POST&quot;\u003c/span>], \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">paths:\u003c/span> [\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;/charge&quot;\u003c/span>] } }]\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Roll out mTLS in permissive mode first (accept both plain and mTLS), check that nothing still sends plain traffic, then switch to strict.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"traffic-control\">Traffic control\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"yaml\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"apiVersion: networking.istio.io/v1\nkind: VirtualService\nmetadata: { name: reviews }\nspec:\n  hosts: [reviews]\n  http:\n    - route:\n        - destination: { host: reviews, subset: v1 }\n          weight: 90\n        - destination: { host: reviews, subset: v2 }\n          weight: 10\n      timeout: 2s\n      retries: { attempts: 2, perTryTimeout: 500ms, retryOn: &quot;5xx,connect-failure&quot; }\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">apiVersion:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">networking.istio.io/v1\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">kind:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">VirtualService\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">metadata:\u003c/span> { \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">reviews\u003c/span> }\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">spec:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">hosts:\u003c/span> [\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">reviews\u003c/span>]\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">http:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">route:\u003c/span>\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">destination:\u003c/span> { \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">host:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">reviews\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">subset:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">v1\u003c/span> }\n          \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">weight:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">90\u003c/span>\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">destination:\u003c/span> { \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">host:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">reviews\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">subset:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">v2\u003c/span> }\n          \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">weight:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">10\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">timeout:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">2s\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">retries:\u003c/span> { \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">attempts:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">2\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">perTryTimeout:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">500ms\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">retryOn:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;5xx,connect-failure&quot;\u003c/span> }\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>This sends 10 percent of traffic to version 2, with a timeout and limited retries. Be careful with retries: they multiply load during an outage (a retry storm) and are only safe for requests that can be repeated. Circuit breaking and outlier detection remove failing instances from rotation.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"telemetry\">Telemetry\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Because proxies see every call, the mesh can report request rate, errors and latency per service pair without code changes, and can create trace spans. Applications must still pass trace headers along for full traces.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"the-cost\">The cost\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Extra resources: each sidecar uses CPU and memory, and adds a little latency.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>More moving parts to upgrade, and a new class of failures (certificate expiry, proxy misconfiguration).\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Debugging has another layer: a 503 may come from the proxy, not the app.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Skills: the team must understand the mesh.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003cp>A mesh pays off with many services, strict security needs (mTLS everywhere, fine-grained policy) or frequent progressive delivery. For a handful of services, simpler tools may be enough: network policies, an ingress controller, and application-level TLS.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"predict\">\u003cp class=\"predict-q\">\u003cstrong>Predict first.\u003c/strong> You switch a namespace to strict mTLS and a legacy job outside the mesh can no longer reach a service. Why?\u003c/p>\u003clabel class=\"predict-l\">Your guess\u003ctextarea rows=\"2\" class=\"predict-guess\" placeholder=\"Type what you expect, then reveal the answer.\">\u003c/textarea>\u003c/label>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-primary\" data-act=\"predict-reveal\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Reveal the answer\u003c/button>\u003cdiv class=\"predict-out\" hidden>\u003cp class=\"predict-ans\">\u003cstrong>Answer:\u003c/strong> The job sends plain traffic and has no mesh certificate, and strict mode rejects connections that are not mutual TLS.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Use permissive mode while migrating, find non-mesh clients from telemetry, then enable strict.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-certificate-that-expired-inside-the-mesh\">Incident: the certificate that expired inside the mesh\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>At 02:10 many internal calls fail with 503 and &quot;upstream connect error&quot;, but applications look healthy.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"istioctl proxy-status | head -3\nkubectl logs deploy/istiod -n istio-system --since=1h | grep -i cert\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>istioctl proxy-status | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">head\u003c/span> -3\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">NAME                CLUSTER  CDS     LDS     EDS     RDS\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">api-7d9f.shop       prod     STALE   STALE   STALE   STALE\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl logs deploy/istiod -n istio-system --since=1h | grep -i cert\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">x509: certificate has expired\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The mesh&#39;s root certificate had expired because rotation had been left manual. Proxies could not get new configuration or certificates. The team issued a new root, rolled the control plane and proxies, and then automated rotation with an alert on certificate expiry 30 days ahead.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Enabling strict mTLS without migration\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Unlimited retries\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>No alert on mesh certificate expiry\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Adopting a mesh for two services\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"parts-of-a-mesh","text":"Parts of a mesh"},{"id":"mutual-tls-and-identity","text":"Mutual TLS and identity"},{"id":"traffic-control","text":"Traffic control"},{"id":"telemetry","text":"Telemetry"},{"id":"the-cost","text":"The cost"},{"id":"incident-the-certificate-that-expired-inside-the-mesh","text":"Incident: the certificate that expired inside the mesh"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Istio docs: Security","u":"https://istio.io/latest/docs/concepts/security/"},{"t":"Envoy docs","u":"https://www.envoyproxy.io/docs/envoy/latest/"}],"text":"As services multiply, the same needs appear in all of them: encrypt traffic between services, know who is calling whom, retry failed calls sensibly, and shift traffic gradually between versions. A service mesh moves these concerns out of application code and into the platform. It is powerful and it is costly, so you should know both sides. What you will learn What a mesh adds: identity, encryption, traffic control and telemetry Data plane and control plane Mutual TLS and authorization policy Traffic splitting, retries and timeouts The cost: complexity, resources and debugging Parts of a mesh The data plane is a set of proxies that handle service-to-service traffic. In the sidecar model each Pod gets a proxy container (often Envoy); newer designs use a per-node proxy or an eBPF-based approach to avoid a sidecar in every Pod. The control plane (Istio, Linkerd, Cilium service mesh and others) configures the proxies, issues certificates and collects telemetry. Applications keep making plain calls to http://payments ; the proxy intercepts and adds behaviour. Mutual TLS and identity With mutual TLS (mTLS) both sides present certificates, so each service knows who is calling and the traffic is encrypted. The mesh gives each workload an identity (for example, based on its Kubernetes service account) and rotates short-lived certificates automatically. You get encryption between services without changing code. On top of identity you can write authorization rules: only the checkout workload may call POST /charge on payments . apiVersion: security.istio.io/v1 kind: AuthorizationPolicy metadata: { name: payments-allow-checkout , namespace: shop } spec: selector: { matchLabels: { app: payments } } action: ALLOW rules: - from: [{ source: { principals: [ \"cluster.local/ns/shop/sa/checkout\" ] } }] to: [{ operation: { methods: [ \"POST\" ], paths: [ \"/charge\" ] } }] Roll out mTLS in permissive mode first (accept both plain and mTLS), check that nothing still sends plain traffic, then switch to strict. Traffic control apiVersion: networking.istio.io/v1 kind: VirtualService metadata: { name: reviews } spec: hosts: [ reviews ] http: - route: - destination: { host: reviews , subset: v1 } weight: 90 - destination: { host: reviews , subset: v2 } weight: 10 timeout: 2s retries: { attempts: 2 , perTryTimeout: 500ms , retryOn: \"5xx,connect-failure\" } This sends 10 percent of traffic to version 2, with a timeout and limited retries. Be careful with retries: they multiply load during an outage (a retry storm) and are only safe for requests that can be repeated. Circuit breaking and outlier detection remove failing instances from rotation. Telemetry Because proxies see every call, the mesh can report request rate, errors and latency per service pair without code changes, and can create trace spans. Applications must still pass trace headers along for full traces. The cost Extra resources: each sidecar uses CPU and memory, and adds a little latency. More moving parts to upgrade, and a new class of failures (certificate expiry, proxy misconfiguration). Debugging has another layer: a 503 may come from the proxy, not the app. Skills: the team must understand the mesh. A mesh pays off with many services, strict security needs (mTLS everywhere, fine-grained policy) or frequent progressive delivery. For a handful of services, simpler tools may be enough: network policies, an ingress controller, and application-level TLS. Predict first. You switch a namespace to strict mTLS and a legacy job outside the mesh can no longer reach a service. Why? Your guess Answer: The job sends plain traffic and has no mesh certificate, and strict mode rejects connections that are not mutual TLS. Use permissive mode while migrating, find non-mesh clients from telemetry, then enable strict. Incident: the certificate that expired inside the mesh Incident At 02:10 many internal calls fail with 503 and \"upstream connect error\", but applications look healthy. $ istioctl proxy-status | head "},"kubernetes/network-policies-in-practice":{"html":"\u003cp>By default every pod in a Kubernetes cluster can talk to every other pod. A compromised pod in one namespace can reach the database in another. Network policies are the firewall rules of the cluster. They are simple, but they are easy to get wrong in ways that either break the application or protect nothing.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>The default is open, and what a NetworkPolicy changes\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Default deny for ingress and egress\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Allow rules with pod selectors, namespace selectors and ports\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Test policies, and remember DNS\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-a-policy-does\">What a policy does\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A NetworkPolicy selects pods with labels and says which traffic is allowed to or from them. Two facts matter first. Policies are \u003cstrong>additive allow rules\u003c/strong>: once any policy selects a pod for a direction, only traffic that some policy allows is permitted in that direction. And policies are enforced by the \u003cstrong>network plugin\u003c/strong>; if your plugin does not support them, the objects exist and do nothing. Calico, Cilium and several cloud plugins support them.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"start-with-default-deny\">Start with default deny\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Begin each namespace with a policy that denies all ingress to all pods, then add what is needed.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"yaml\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1\nkind: NetworkPolicy\nmetadata:\n  name: default-deny-ingress\n  namespace: shop\nspec:\n  podSelector: {}\n  policyTypes: [&quot;Ingress&quot;]\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">apiVersion:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">networking.k8s.io/v1\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">kind:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">NetworkPolicy\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">metadata:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">default-deny-ingress\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">namespace:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">shop\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">spec:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">podSelector:\u003c/span> {}\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">policyTypes:\u003c/span> [\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Ingress&quot;\u003c/span>]\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The empty \u003ccode>podSelector\u003c/code> selects every pod in the namespace. With no ingress rules listed, nothing is allowed in. Do the same for egress later, once you know what each workload needs to reach.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"allow-what-is-needed\">Allow what is needed\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Add policies that name the exact paths. This one lets only the \u003ccode>api\u003c/code> pods connect to the \u003ccode>db\u003c/code> pods on the database port:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"yaml\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1\nkind: NetworkPolicy\nmetadata:\n  name: db-from-api\n  namespace: shop\nspec:\n  podSelector:\n    matchLabels: { app: db }\n  policyTypes: [&quot;Ingress&quot;]\n  ingress:\n    - from:\n        - podSelector:\n            matchLabels: { app: api }\n      ports:\n        - { protocol: TCP, port: 5432 }\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">apiVersion:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">networking.k8s.io/v1\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">kind:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">NetworkPolicy\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">metadata:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">db-from-api\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">namespace:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">shop\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">spec:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">podSelector:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">matchLabels:\u003c/span> { \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">app:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">db\u003c/span> }\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">policyTypes:\u003c/span> [\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Ingress&quot;\u003c/span>]\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">ingress:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">from:\u003c/span>\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">podSelector:\u003c/span>\n            \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">matchLabels:\u003c/span> { \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">app:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">api\u003c/span> }\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">ports:\u003c/span>\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> { \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">protocol:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">TCP\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">port:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">5432\u003c/span> }\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>To allow traffic from another namespace, use a \u003ccode>namespaceSelector\u003c/code> (namespaces need labels, for example \u003ccode>kubernetes.io/metadata.name\u003c/code>). Be careful: putting \u003ccode>podSelector\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>namespaceSelector\u003c/code> in the same list item means \u003cem>both must match\u003c/em>, while two separate items mean \u003cem>either\u003c/em>.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"egress-dns-and-testing\">Egress, DNS and testing\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>If you restrict egress, remember DNS. Pods must reach the cluster DNS service on port 53 (UDP and TCP), or every name lookup fails and the application looks broken for no visible reason.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"kubectl -n shop run probe --rm -it --image=busybox:1.36 --restart=Never -- \\\n    wget -qO- -T 3 http://db:5432\nkubectl -n shop get networkpolicy\nkubectl -n shop describe networkpolicy db-from-api\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl -n shop run probe --\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">rm\u003c/span> -it --image=busybox:1.36 --restart=Never -- \\\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd cont\">    wget -qO- -T 3 http://db:5432\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl -n shop get networkpolicy\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl -n shop describe networkpolicy db-from-api\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Test both directions: the path that must work, and a path that must be blocked. Keep policies in version control next to the application manifests, and review them in the same pull request as the workload change.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"predict\">\u003cp class=\"predict-q\">\u003cstrong>Predict first.\u003c/strong> You add a default deny egress policy and your pods stop resolving names. What did you forget?\u003c/p>\u003clabel class=\"predict-l\">Your guess\u003ctextarea rows=\"2\" class=\"predict-guess\" placeholder=\"Type what you expect, then reveal the answer.\">\u003c/textarea>\u003c/label>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-primary\" data-act=\"predict-reveal\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Reveal the answer\u003c/button>\u003cdiv class=\"predict-out\" hidden>\u003cp class=\"predict-ans\">\u003cstrong>Answer:\u003c/strong> An egress rule that allows DNS (port 53 to the cluster DNS pods).\u003c/p>\u003cp>Egress deny blocks DNS queries too.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-lateral-movement-in-one-hop\">Incident: Lateral movement in one hop\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A vulnerable image in the \u003ccode>marketing\u003c/code> namespace was exploited, and the attacker connected straight to the payments database.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"kubectl get networkpolicy -A\nkubectl -n marketing exec web-6d9 -- nc -zv payments-db.payments 5432\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl get networkpolicy -A\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">No resources found\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl -n marketing \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">exec\u003c/span> web-6d9 -- nc -zv payments-db.payments 5432\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">payments-db.payments (10.96.14.8:5432) open\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The cluster had no network policies, so any pod could reach any other. The incident review led to default deny policies in every namespace, explicit allow rules for the payments database from the payments namespace only, and an admission policy that rejects a new namespace until it has a default deny. A weekly test job tries a blocked connection from a canary pod and alerts if it succeeds.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Assuming policies work without a plugin that enforces them\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Forgetting DNS when restricting egress\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Mixing up selector lists, so a rule is far wider than intended\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Writing policies once and never testing the blocked paths\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"what-a-policy-does","text":"What a policy does"},{"id":"start-with-default-deny","text":"Start with default deny"},{"id":"allow-what-is-needed","text":"Allow what is needed"},{"id":"egress-dns-and-testing","text":"Egress, DNS and testing"},{"id":"incident-lateral-movement-in-one-hop","text":"Incident: Lateral movement in one hop"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Kubernetes network policies","u":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/services-networking/network-policies/"},{"t":"Declare a network policy","u":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/tasks/administer-cluster/declare-network-policy/"}],"text":"By default every pod in a Kubernetes cluster can talk to every other pod. A compromised pod in one namespace can reach the database in another. Network policies are the firewall rules of the cluster. They are simple, but they are easy to get wrong in ways that either break the application or protect nothing. What you will learn The default is open, and what a NetworkPolicy changes Default deny for ingress and egress Allow rules with pod selectors, namespace selectors and ports Test policies, and remember DNS What a policy does A NetworkPolicy selects pods with labels and says which traffic is allowed to or from them. Two facts matter first. Policies are additive allow rules : once any policy selects a pod for a direction, only traffic that some policy allows is permitted in that direction. And policies are enforced by the network plugin ; if your plugin does not support them, the objects exist and do nothing. Calico, Cilium and several cloud plugins support them. Start with default deny Begin each namespace with a policy that denies all ingress to all pods, then add what is needed. apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1 kind: NetworkPolicy metadata: name: default-deny-ingress namespace: shop spec: podSelector: {} policyTypes: [ \"Ingress\" ] The empty podSelector selects every pod in the namespace. With no ingress rules listed, nothing is allowed in. Do the same for egress later, once you know what each workload needs to reach. Allow what is needed Add policies that name the exact paths. This one lets only the api pods connect to the db pods on the database port: apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1 kind: NetworkPolicy metadata: name: db-from-api namespace: shop spec: podSelector: matchLabels: { app: db } policyTypes: [ \"Ingress\" ] ingress: - from: - podSelector: matchLabels: { app: api } ports: - { protocol: TCP , port: 5432 } To allow traffic from another namespace, use a namespaceSelector (namespaces need labels, for example kubernetes.io/metadata.name ). Be careful: putting podSelector and namespaceSelector in the same list item means both must match , while two separate items mean either . Egress, DNS and testing If you restrict egress, remember DNS. Pods must reach the cluster DNS service on port 53 (UDP and TCP), or every name lookup fails and the application looks broken for no visible reason. $ kubectl -n shop run probe -- rm -it --image=busybox:1.36 --restart=Never -- \\ wget -qO- -T 3 http://db:5432 $ kubectl -n shop get networkpolicy $ kubectl -n shop describe networkpolicy db-from-api Test both directions: the path that must work, and a path that must be blocked. Keep policies in version control next to the application manifests, and review them in the same pull request as the workload change. Predict first. You add a default deny egress policy and your pods stop resolving names. What did you forget? Your guess Answer: An egress rule that allows DNS (port 53 to the cluster DNS pods). Egress deny blocks DNS queries too. Incident: Lateral movement in one hop Incident A vulnerable image in the marketing namespace was exploited, and the attacker connected straight to the payments database. $ kubectl get networkpolicy -A No resources found $ kubectl -n marketing exec web-6d9 -- nc -zv payments-db.payments 5432 payments-db.payments (10.96.14.8:5432) open The cluster had no network policies, so any pod could reach any other. The incident review led to default deny policies in every namespace, explicit allow rules for the payments database from the payments namespace only, and an admission policy that rejects a new namespace until it has a default deny. A weekly test job tries a blocked connection from a canary pod and alerts if it succeeds. Common mistakes Assuming policies work without a plugin that enforces them Forgetting DNS when restricting egress Mixing up selector lists, so a rule is far wider than intended Writing policies once and never testing the blocked paths"},"kubernetes/statefulsets-and-running-databases":{"html":"\u003cp>A Deployment treats pods as interchangeable. A database node is not interchangeable: it has its own data, a name the others know it by, and a role (primary or replica). StatefulSets give pods a stable identity and storage, but they do not make a database safe by themselves. This lesson covers what they provide and what you still have to do.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>What a StatefulSet guarantees: names, order and storage\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Headless services and stable DNS names\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Volume claim templates and what happens when a pod moves\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>When to use an operator or a managed database instead\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"stable-identity\">Stable identity\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A StatefulSet creates pods named \u003ccode>name-0\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>name-1\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>name-2\u003c/code>. If \u003ccode>db-1\u003c/code> is deleted, the replacement is again called \u003ccode>db-1\u003c/code> and gets the same storage. By default pods start in order (0 first) and stop in reverse order, which suits clusters where the first node bootstraps the others.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"yaml\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"apiVersion: apps/v1\nkind: StatefulSet\nmetadata: { name: db }\nspec:\n  serviceName: db-headless\n  replicas: 3\n  selector: { matchLabels: { app: db } }\n  template:\n    metadata: { labels: { app: db } }\n    spec:\n      containers:\n        - name: db\n          image: postgres:16\n          volumeMounts: [{ name: data, mountPath: /var/lib/postgresql/data }]\n  volumeClaimTemplates:\n    - metadata: { name: data }\n      spec:\n        accessModes: [&quot;ReadWriteOnce&quot;]\n        storageClassName: fast\n        resources: { requests: { storage: 100Gi } }\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">apiVersion:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">apps/v1\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">kind:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">StatefulSet\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">metadata:\u003c/span> { \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">db\u003c/span> }\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">spec:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">serviceName:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">db-headless\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">replicas:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">3\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">selector:\u003c/span> { \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">matchLabels:\u003c/span> { \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">app:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">db\u003c/span> } }\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">template:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">metadata:\u003c/span> { \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">labels:\u003c/span> { \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">app:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">db\u003c/span> } }\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">spec:\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">containers:\u003c/span>\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">db\u003c/span>\n          \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">image:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">postgres:16\u003c/span>\n          \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">volumeMounts:\u003c/span> [{ \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">data\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">mountPath:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">/var/lib/postgresql/data\u003c/span> }]\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">volumeClaimTemplates:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">metadata:\u003c/span> { \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">data\u003c/span> }\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">spec:\u003c/span>\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">accessModes:\u003c/span> [\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;ReadWriteOnce&quot;\u003c/span>]\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">storageClassName:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">fast\u003c/span>\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">resources:\u003c/span> { \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">requests:\u003c/span> { \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">storage:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">100Gi\u003c/span> } }\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"headless-service-and-dns\">Headless service and DNS\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A \u003cstrong>headless service\u003c/strong> (\u003ccode>clusterIP: None\u003c/code>) does not load balance. Instead, DNS returns a record per pod: \u003ccode>db-0.db-headless.shop.svc.cluster.local\u003c/code>. Peers and clients can therefore reach one specific member, for example the primary at \u003ccode>db-0\u003c/code>. Use a normal service in front for clients that need any healthy node.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"storage-and-what-happens-on-failure\">Storage and what happens on failure\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Each pod gets its own PersistentVolumeClaim from the \u003ccode>volumeClaimTemplates\u003c/code>. If the pod is deleted or rescheduled, the new pod attaches the same claim. Note two things:\u003c/p>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Deleting a StatefulSet does \u003cstrong>not\u003c/strong> delete the claims, so data survives, and you must remove them deliberately. Newer versions allow a retention policy for this.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>A volume is tied to a zone in most clouds. If the zone fails, the pod cannot start elsewhere without that data, so replication across zones is the database&#39;s job, not Kubernetes&#39;.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003cp>A PodDisruptionBudget stops a node drain from taking down too many members at once, for example \u003ccode>maxUnavailable: 1\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"be-honest-about-databases\">Be honest about databases\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>StatefulSets give identity and storage. They do not give you backups, failover, upgrades, tuning or point-in-time recovery. For databases you have three choices:\u003c/p>\n\u003col>\n\u003cli>A \u003cstrong>managed service\u003c/strong> from your cloud: least work, usually the right default for important data.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>A Kubernetes \u003cstrong>operator\u003c/strong> for that database, which encodes failover, backups and upgrades as automation. Use a well-maintained one, and test restores.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>A hand-written StatefulSet: only for learning or simple, replaceable data such as a cache.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ol>\n\u003cp>Whichever you choose, rehearse: kill the primary, restore from backup into a fresh cluster, and measure how long it takes.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"predict\">\u003cp class=\"predict-q\">\u003cstrong>Predict first.\u003c/strong> You delete a StatefulSet to recreate it with a new setting. What happens to its data volumes by default?\u003c/p>\u003clabel class=\"predict-l\">Your guess\u003ctextarea rows=\"2\" class=\"predict-guess\" placeholder=\"Type what you expect, then reveal the answer.\">\u003c/textarea>\u003c/label>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-primary\" data-act=\"predict-reveal\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Reveal the answer\u003c/button>\u003cdiv class=\"predict-out\" hidden>\u003cp class=\"predict-ans\">\u003cstrong>Answer:\u003c/strong> The PersistentVolumeClaims remain, so data is kept, and the recreated pods reattach to them.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Claims are not deleted with the StatefulSet unless you configure a retention policy.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-replica-that-came-back-empty\">Incident: The replica that came back empty\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>After a node failure, a database replica restarted and began a full resync that took six hours and slowed the primary.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"kubectl get pvc -l app=db\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl get pvc -l app=db\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">NAME        STATUS   VOLUME     CAPACITY\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">data-db-0   Bound    pvc-a1     100Gi\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">data-db-1   Pending  &lt;none&gt;     100Gi\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The volume for \u003ccode>db-1\u003c/code> lived in a zone that was down, and the claim could not be bound elsewhere, so the replacement pod started with a new empty volume created by the storage class. The team switched to an operator with zone-aware replicas, added a PodDisruptionBudget, and scheduled a monthly restore drill. They also moved the main production database to a managed service, and kept the StatefulSet for a rebuildable analytics store.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Treating a StatefulSet as a complete database solution\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Forgetting backups and restore tests\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Assuming storage can follow a pod to another zone\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Deleting claims by accident while cleaning up\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"stable-identity","text":"Stable identity"},{"id":"headless-service-and-dns","text":"Headless service and DNS"},{"id":"storage-and-what-happens-on-failure","text":"Storage and what happens on failure"},{"id":"be-honest-about-databases","text":"Be honest about databases"},{"id":"incident-the-replica-that-came-back-empty","text":"Incident: The replica that came back empty"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"StatefulSets","u":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/workloads/controllers/statefulset/"},{"t":"Run a replicated stateful application","u":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/tasks/run-application/run-replicated-stateful-application/"}],"text":"A Deployment treats pods as interchangeable. A database node is not interchangeable: it has its own data, a name the others know it by, and a role (primary or replica). StatefulSets give pods a stable identity and storage, but they do not make a database safe by themselves. This lesson covers what they provide and what you still have to do. What you will learn What a StatefulSet guarantees: names, order and storage Headless services and stable DNS names Volume claim templates and what happens when a pod moves When to use an operator or a managed database instead Stable identity A StatefulSet creates pods named name-0 , name-1 , name-2 . If db-1 is deleted, the replacement is again called db-1 and gets the same storage. By default pods start in order (0 first) and stop in reverse order, which suits clusters where the first node bootstraps the others. apiVersion: apps/v1 kind: StatefulSet metadata: { name: db } spec: serviceName: db-headless replicas: 3 selector: { matchLabels: { app: db } } template: metadata: { labels: { app: db } } spec: containers: - name: db image: postgres:16 volumeMounts: [{ name: data , mountPath: /var/lib/postgresql/data }] volumeClaimTemplates: - metadata: { name: data } spec: accessModes: [ \"ReadWriteOnce\" ] storageClassName: fast resources: { requests: { storage: 100Gi } } Headless service and DNS A headless service ( clusterIP: None ) does not load balance. Instead, DNS returns a record per pod: db-0.db-headless.shop.svc.cluster.local . Peers and clients can therefore reach one specific member, for example the primary at db-0 . Use a normal service in front for clients that need any healthy node. Storage and what happens on failure Each pod gets its own PersistentVolumeClaim from the volumeClaimTemplates . If the pod is deleted or rescheduled, the new pod attaches the same claim. Note two things: Deleting a StatefulSet does not delete the claims, so data survives, and you must remove them deliberately. Newer versions allow a retention policy for this. A volume is tied to a zone in most clouds. If the zone fails, the pod cannot start elsewhere without that data, so replication across zones is the database&#39;s job, not Kubernetes&#39;. A PodDisruptionBudget stops a node drain from taking down too many members at once, for example maxUnavailable: 1 . Be honest about databases StatefulSets give identity and storage. They do not give you backups, failover, upgrades, tuning or point-in-time recovery. For databases you have three choices: A managed service from your cloud: least work, usually the right default for important data. A Kubernetes operator for that database, which encodes failover, backups and upgrades as automation. Use a well-maintained one, and test restores. A hand-written StatefulSet: only for learning or simple, replaceable data such as a cache. Whichever you choose, rehearse: kill the primary, restore from backup into a fresh cluster, and measure how long it takes. Predict first. You delete a StatefulSet to recreate it with a new setting. What happens to its data volumes by default? Your guess Answer: The PersistentVolumeClaims remain, so data is kept, and the recreated pods reattach to them. Claims are not deleted with the StatefulSet unless you configure a retention policy. Incident: The replica that came back empty Incident After a node failure, a database replica restarted and began a full resync that took six hours and slowed the primary. $ kubectl get pvc -l app=db NAME STATUS VOLUME CAPACITY data-db-0 Bound pvc-a1 100Gi data-db-1 Pending \u003cnone> 100Gi The volume for db-1 lived in a zone that was down, and the claim could not be bound elsewhere, so the replacement pod started with a new empty volume created by the storage class. The team switched to an operator with zone-aware replicas, added a PodDisruptionBudget, and scheduled a monthly restore drill. They also moved the main production database to a managed service, and kept the StatefulSet for a rebuildable analytics store. Co"},"kubernetes/debugging-kubernetes-workloads":{"html":"\u003cp>Kubernetes reports problems in a small set of states: Pending, ImagePullBackOff, CrashLoopBackOff, Running but not Ready, and a Service that routes nowhere. Each state points to a different part of the system. Learn the state, then the one or two commands that explain it.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>What each Pod state means and where to look\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003ccode>describe\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>logs --previous\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>get events\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>exec\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>kubectl debug\u003c/code>\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Checking Services, endpoints, DNS and network policy\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Node-level problems: pressure, taints and capacity\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"start-with-the-pod\">Start with the Pod\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"kubectl get pods -o wide\nkubectl describe pod shop-7d9f-abc | sed -n '/State:/,/Ready:/p;/Events:/,$p'\nkubectl logs shop-7d9f-abc --previous --tail=50\nkubectl get events --sort-by=.lastTimestamp -n shop | tail -15\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl get pods -o wide\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">NAME            READY   STATUS             RESTARTS   AGE\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">shop-7d9f-abc   0/1     CrashLoopBackOff   6          9m\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl describe pod shop-7d9f-abc | sed -n \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;/State:/,/Ready:/p;/Events:/,$p&#x27;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl logs shop-7d9f-abc --previous --\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">tail\u003c/span>=50\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl get events --sort-by=.lastTimestamp -n shop | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">tail\u003c/span> -15\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>describe\u003c/code> shows the last state, exit code and events. \u003ccode>logs --previous\u003c/code> shows the output of the container that just died, which \u003ccode>logs\u003c/code> alone does not.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"table-wrap\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\n\u003cth>State\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Meaning\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>First check\u003c/th>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Pending\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Not scheduled\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Events: insufficient CPU or memory, taints, unbound volume claim\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>ImagePullBackOff\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Cannot pull the image\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Image name and tag, registry secret, network\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>CrashLoopBackOff\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Starts and exits repeatedly\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>logs --previous\u003c/code>, exit code, OOMKilled, config\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Running, not Ready\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Readiness probe fails\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Probe path and port, app startup time\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Evicted\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Node under pressure\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Node conditions, resource requests\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Terminating forever\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Finalizer or stuck volume\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>get pod -o yaml\u003c/code> for finalizers\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"get-inside-even-without-a-shell\">Get inside, even without a shell\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"kubectl exec -it shop-7d9f-abc -- sh\nkubectl debug -it shop-7d9f-abc --image=busybox:1.36 --target=app   # ephemeral container\nkubectl debug node/worker-2 -it --image=busybox:1.36                # look at a node\nkubectl get pod shop-7d9f-abc -o yaml | less                         # the full spec and status\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">kubectl \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">exec\u003c/span> -it shop-7d9f-abc -- sh\nkubectl debug -it shop-7d9f-abc --image=busybox:1.36 --target=app   \u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># ephemeral container\u003c/span>\nkubectl debug node/worker-2 -it --image=busybox:1.36                \u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># look at a node\u003c/span>\nkubectl get pod shop-7d9f-abc -o yaml | less                         \u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># the full spec and status\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>An ephemeral container shares the Pod&#39;s namespaces, so you can test with tools the production image does not ship.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"a-service-that-goes-nowhere\">A Service that goes nowhere\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"kubectl get svc shop -o wide\nkubectl get endpoints shop\nkubectl get pods --show-labels\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl get svc shop -o wide\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl get endpoints shop\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">NAME   ENDPOINTS\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">shop   &lt;none&gt;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl get pods --show-labels\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Empty endpoints mean the Service selector matches no Ready Pod. Compare the selector with the Pod labels and check readiness. Then test from inside the cluster:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"kubectl run tmp --rm -it --image=busybox:1.36 --restart=Never -- wget -qO- http://shop.shop.svc.cluster.local\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">kubectl run tmp --\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">rm\u003c/span> -it --image=busybox:1.36 --restart=Never -- wget -qO- http://shop.shop.svc.cluster.local\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>If the name does not resolve, check CoreDNS Pods. If it resolves but hangs, check NetworkPolicy and the target port.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"predict\">\u003cp class=\"predict-q\">\u003cstrong>Predict first.\u003c/strong> A Pod is Pending and the event says &quot;0/3 nodes are available: 3 Insufficient memory&quot;. What are your options?\u003c/p>\u003clabel class=\"predict-l\">Your guess\u003ctextarea rows=\"2\" class=\"predict-guess\" placeholder=\"Type what you expect, then reveal the answer.\">\u003c/textarea>\u003c/label>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-primary\" data-act=\"predict-reveal\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Reveal the answer\u003c/button>\u003cdiv class=\"predict-out\" hidden>\u003cp class=\"predict-ans\">\u003cstrong>Answer:\u003c/strong> Lower the memory request, add node capacity, or free resources by scaling other workloads.\u003c/p>\u003cp>The scheduler places Pods by requests, not by actual use. A request larger than any node's free allocatable memory can never schedule.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"3\" data-edges=\"get&gt;desc;get&gt;logs;desc&gt;svc;logs&gt;node\" style=\"--rows:3\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">From a broken Pod to a cause\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"16.7\" y1=\"50.0\" x2=\"50.0\" y2=\"16.7\"/>\u003cline x1=\"16.7\" y1=\"50.0\" x2=\"50.0\" y2=\"83.3\"/>\u003cline x1=\"50.0\" y1=\"16.7\" x2=\"83.3\" y2=\"16.7\"/>\u003cline x1=\"50.0\" y1=\"83.3\" x2=\"83.3\" y2=\"83.3\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"get\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:16.7%;top:50.0%;--w:29.3%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Pod state\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-observe\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"desc\" data-kind=\"observe\" style=\"left:50.0%;top:16.7%;--w:29.3%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Describe and events\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-observe\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"logs\" data-kind=\"observe\" style=\"left:50.0%;top:83.3%;--w:29.3%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Logs, previous run\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-edge\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"svc\" data-kind=\"edge\" style=\"left:83.3%;top:16.7%;--w:29.3%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Service and endpoints\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Network edge\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-security\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"node\" data-kind=\"security\" style=\"left:83.3%;top:83.3%;--w:29.3%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Node and policy\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Pod state to Describe and events; 2, Pod state to Logs, previous run; 3, Describe and events to Service and endpoints; 4, Logs, previous run to Node and policy.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-observe\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Observability\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-edge\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Network edge\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-security\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Security\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"get\">\u003cdt>Pod state \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> kubectl get pods shows Pending, CrashLoopBackOff or not Ready.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> The state alone does not give the cause.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"desc\">\u003cdt>Describe and events \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Shows exit code, scheduling messages, probe failures and pull errors.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Events expire after about an hour, so look soon.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"logs\">\u003cdt>Logs, previous run \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> logs --previous prints output from the crashed container.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Plain logs show only the new, empty container.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"svc\">\u003cdt>Service and endpoints \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Network edge\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Empty endpoints mean the selector or readiness is wrong.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> A correct Pod behind a mismatched selector still receives no traffic.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"node\">\u003cdt>Node and policy \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Check node conditions, taints, quotas and NetworkPolicy.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> The Pod can be fine while the node or policy blocks it.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-healthy-pods-no-traffic\">Incident: healthy Pods, no traffic\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>After a rollout, the shop returns 503 from the ingress although all Pods show Running.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"kubectl get pods\nkubectl describe pod shop-8c4d-x2k | grep -i -A2 readiness\nkubectl get cm shop-config -o yaml | grep PORT\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl get pods\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">shop-8c4d-x2k   0/1   Running   0   4m\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl describe pod shop-8c4d-x2k | grep -i -A2 readiness\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">Readiness probe failed: Get &quot;http://10.2.1.14:8080/healthz&quot;: connect: connection refused\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl get cm shop-config -o yaml | grep PORT\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">PORT: &quot;9090&quot;\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>A config change moved the application to port 9090, but the readiness probe still checked 8080. No Pod became Ready, so the Service had no endpoints. The fix was to align the probe and the config, and the team added a rollout check that fails if the new ReplicaSet does not become Ready.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Reading \u003ccode>logs\u003c/code> instead of \u003ccode>logs --previous\u003c/code> after a crash\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Assuming Running means working\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Forgetting events disappear\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Editing Pods directly instead of the Deployment\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"start-with-the-pod","text":"Start with the Pod"},{"id":"get-inside-even-without-a-shell","text":"Get inside, even without a shell"},{"id":"a-service-that-goes-nowhere","text":"A Service that goes nowhere"},{"id":"incident-healthy-pods-no-traffic","text":"Incident: healthy Pods, no traffic"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Kubernetes docs: Debug Pods","u":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/tasks/debug/debug-application/debug-pods/"},{"t":"Kubernetes docs: Debug Services","u":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/tasks/debug/debug-application/debug-service/"}],"text":"Kubernetes reports problems in a small set of states: Pending, ImagePullBackOff, CrashLoopBackOff, Running but not Ready, and a Service that routes nowhere. Each state points to a different part of the system. Learn the state, then the one or two commands that explain it. What you will learn What each Pod state means and where to look describe , logs --previous , get events , exec and kubectl debug Checking Services, endpoints, DNS and network policy Node-level problems: pressure, taints and capacity Start with the Pod $ kubectl get pods -o wide NAME READY STATUS RESTARTS AGE shop-7d9f-abc 0/1 CrashLoopBackOff 6 9m $ kubectl describe pod shop-7d9f-abc | sed -n &#x27;/State:/,/Ready:/p;/Events:/,$p&#x27; $ kubectl logs shop-7d9f-abc --previous -- tail =50 $ kubectl get events --sort-by=.lastTimestamp -n shop | tail -15 describe shows the last state, exit code and events. logs --previous shows the output of the container that just died, which logs alone does not. State Meaning First check Pending Not scheduled Events: insufficient CPU or memory, taints, unbound volume claim ImagePullBackOff Cannot pull the image Image name and tag, registry secret, network CrashLoopBackOff Starts and exits repeatedly logs --previous , exit code, OOMKilled, config Running, not Ready Readiness probe fails Probe path and port, app startup time Evicted Node under pressure Node conditions, resource requests Terminating forever Finalizer or stuck volume get pod -o yaml for finalizers Get inside, even without a shell kubectl exec -it shop-7d9f-abc -- sh kubectl debug -it shop-7d9f-abc --image=busybox:1.36 --target=app # ephemeral container kubectl debug node/worker-2 -it --image=busybox:1.36 # look at a node kubectl get pod shop-7d9f-abc -o yaml | less # the full spec and status An ephemeral container shares the Pod&#39;s namespaces, so you can test with tools the production image does not ship. A Service that goes nowhere $ kubectl get svc shop -o wide $ kubectl get endpoints shop NAME ENDPOINTS shop \u003cnone> $ kubectl get pods --show-labels Empty endpoints mean the Service selector matches no Ready Pod. Compare the selector with the Pod labels and check readiness. Then test from inside the cluster: kubectl run tmp -- rm -it --image=busybox:1.36 --restart=Never -- wget -qO- http://shop.shop.svc.cluster.local If the name does not resolve, check CoreDNS Pods. If it resolves but hangs, check NetworkPolicy and the target port. Predict first. A Pod is Pending and the event says \"0/3 nodes are available: 3 Insufficient memory\". What are your options? Your guess Answer: Lower the memory request, add node capacity, or free resources by scaling other workloads. The scheduler places Pods by requests, not by actual use. A request larger than any node's free allocatable memory can never schedule. From a broken Pod to a cause Flow in order: 1, Pod state to Describe and events; 2, Pod state to Logs, previous run; 3, Describe and events to Service and endpoints; 4, Logs, previous run to Node and policy. Step Observability Network edge Security Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Pod state Step What it does: kubectl get pods shows Pending, CrashLoopBackOff or not Ready. If it fails or is weak: The state alone does not give the cause. Describe and events Observability What it does: Shows exit code, scheduling messages, probe failures and pull errors. If it fails or is weak: Events expire after about an hour, so look soon. Logs, previous run Observability What it does: logs --previous prints output from the crashed container. If it fails or is weak: Plain logs show only the new, empty container. Service and endpoints Network edge What it does: Empty endpoints mean the selector or readiness is wrong. If it fails or is weak: A correct Pod behind a mismatched selector still receives no traffic. Node and policy Security What it does: Check node conditions, taints, quotas and NetworkPolicy. If it fa"}});